\id XXA paradiso
\usfm 3.1
\ide UTF-8
\rem anagnosis work=paradiso edition=longfellow.en lang=en
\rem source Project Gutenberg 1004, https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/1004
\rem rights Public domain (Longfellow, 1867)
\rem note Who speaks each speech (zsay) is an editorial annotation, not Longfellow's.
\h Paradiso
\toc1 Paradiso
\mt1 Paradiso
\c 1
\cp 1
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* The glory of Him who moveth everything
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* Doth penetrate the universe, and shine
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* In one part more and in another less.
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* Within that heaven which most his light receives
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* Was I, and things beheld which to repeat
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* Nor knows, nor can, who from above descends;
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* Because in drawing near to its desire
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* Our intellect ingulphs itself so far,
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* That after it the memory cannot go.
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* Truly whatever of the holy realm
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* I had the power to treasure in my mind
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* Shall now become the subject of my song.
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* O good Apollo, for this last emprise
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* Make of me such a vessel of thy power
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* As giving the beloved laurel asks!
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* One summit of Parnassus hitherto
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* Has been enough for me, but now with both
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* I needs must enter the arena left.
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* Enter into my bosom, thou, and breathe
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* As at the time when Marsyas thou didst draw
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* Out of the scabbard of those limbs of his.
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* O power divine, lend’st thou thyself to me
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* So that the shadow of the blessed realm
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* Stamped in my brain I can make manifest,
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* Thou’lt see me come unto thy darling tree,
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* And crown myself thereafter with those leaves
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* Of which the theme and thou shall make me worthy.
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* So seldom, Father, do we gather them
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* For triumph or of Caesar or of Poet,
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* (The fault and shame of human inclinations,)
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* That the Peneian foliage should bring forth
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* Joy to the joyous Delphic deity,
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* When any one it makes to thirst for it.
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* A little spark is followed by great flame;
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* Perchance with better voices after me
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* Shall prayer be made that Cyrrha may respond!
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* To mortal men by passages diverse
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* Uprises the world’s lamp; but by that one
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* Which circles four uniteth with three crosses,
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* With better course and with a better star
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* Conjoined it issues, and the mundane wax
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* Tempers and stamps more after its own fashion.
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* Almost that passage had made morning there
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* And evening here, and there was wholly white
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* That hemisphere, and black the other part,
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* When Beatrice towards the left-hand side
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* I saw turned round, and gazing at the sun;
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* Never did eagle fasten so upon it!
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* And even as a second ray is wont
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* To issue from the first and reascend,
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* Like to a pilgrim who would fain return,
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* Thus of her action, through the eyes infused
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* In my imagination, mine I made,
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* And sunward fixed mine eyes beyond our wont.
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* There much is lawful which is here unlawful
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* Unto our powers, by virtue of the place
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* Made for the human species as its own.
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* Not long I bore it, nor so little while
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* But I beheld it sparkle round about
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* Like iron that comes molten from the fire;
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* And suddenly it seemed that day to day
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* Was added, as if He who has the power
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* Had with another sun the heaven adorned.
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* With eyes upon the everlasting wheels
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* Stood Beatrice all intent, and I, on her
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* Fixing my vision from above removed,
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* Such at her aspect inwardly became
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* As Glaucus, tasting of the herb that made him
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* Peer of the other gods beneath the sea.
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* To represent transhumanise in words
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* Impossible were; the example, then, suffice
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* Him for whom Grace the experience reserves.
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* If I was merely what of me thou newly
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* Createdst, Love who governest the heaven,
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* Thou knowest, who didst lift me with thy light!
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* When now the wheel, which thou dost make eternal
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* Desiring thee, made me attentive to it
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* By harmony thou dost modulate and measure,
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* Then seemed to me so much of heaven enkindled
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* By the sun’s flame, that neither rain nor river
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* E’er made a lake so widely spread abroad.
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* The newness of the sound and the great light
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* Kindled in me a longing for their cause,
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* Never before with such acuteness felt;
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* Whence she, who saw me as I saw myself,
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* To quiet in me my perturbed mind,
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* Opened her mouth, ere I did mine to ask,
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* And she began: \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*“Thou makest thyself so dull\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*With false imagining, that thou seest not\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*What thou wouldst see if thou hadst shaken it off.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Thou art not upon earth, as thou believest;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*But lightning, fleeing its appropriate site,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Ne’er ran as thou, who thitherward returnest.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* If of my former doubt I was divested
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* By these brief little words more smiled than spoken,
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* I in a new one was the more ensnared;
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* And said: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“Already did I rest content\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*From great amazement; but am now amazed\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*In what way I transcend these bodies light.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* Whereupon she, after a pitying sigh,
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* Her eyes directed tow’rds me with that look
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* A mother casts on a delirious child;
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* And she began: \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*“All things whate’er they be\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Have order among themselves, and this is form,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*That makes the universe resemble God.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Here do the higher creatures see the footprints\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Of the Eternal Power, which is the end\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Whereto is made the law already mentioned.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*In the order that I speak of are inclined\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*All natures, by their destinies diverse,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*More or less near unto their origin;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Hence they move onward unto ports diverse\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*O’er the great sea of being; and each one\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*With instinct given it which bears it on.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*This bears away the fire towards the moon;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*This is in mortal hearts the motive power\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*This binds together and unites the earth.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Nor only the created things that are\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Without intelligence this bow shoots forth,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*But those that have both intellect and love.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*The Providence that regulates all this\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Makes with its light the heaven forever quiet,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Wherein that turns which has the greatest haste.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*And thither now, as to a site decreed,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 125 \vp 125\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Bears us away the virtue of that cord\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 126 \vp 126\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Which aims its arrows at a joyous mark.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 127 \vp 127\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*True is it, that as oftentimes the form\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 128 \vp 128\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Accords not with the intention of the art,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 129 \vp 129\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Because in answering is matter deaf,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 130 \vp 130\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*So likewise from this course doth deviate\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 131 \vp 131\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Sometimes the creature, who the power possesses,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 132 \vp 132\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Though thus impelled, to swerve some other way,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 133 \vp 133\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*(In the same wise as one may see the fire\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 134 \vp 134\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Fall from a cloud,) if the first impetus\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 135 \vp 135\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Earthward is wrested by some false delight.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 136 \vp 136\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Thou shouldst not wonder more, if well I judge,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 137 \vp 137\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*At thine ascent, than at a rivulet\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 138 \vp 138\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*From some high mount descending to the lowland.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 139 \vp 139\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Marvel it would be in thee, if deprived\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 140 \vp 140\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Of hindrance, thou wert seated down below,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 141 \vp 141\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*As if on earth the living fire were quiet.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 142 \vp 142\vp* Thereat she heavenward turned again her face.
\c 2
\cp 2
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* O Ye, who in some pretty little boat,
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* Eager to listen, have been following
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* Behind my ship, that singing sails along,
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* Turn back to look again upon your shores;
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* Do not put out to sea, lest peradventure,
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* In losing me, you might yourselves be lost.
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* The sea I sail has never yet been passed;
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* Minerva breathes, and pilots me Apollo,
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* And Muses nine point out to me the Bears.
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* Ye other few who have the neck uplifted
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* Betimes to th’ bread of Angels upon which
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* One liveth here and grows not sated by it,
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* Well may you launch upon the deep salt-sea
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* Your vessel, keeping still my wake before you
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* Upon the water that grows smooth again.
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* Those glorious ones who unto Colchos passed
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* Were not so wonder-struck as you shall be,
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* When Jason they beheld a ploughman made!
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* The con-created and perpetual thirst
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* For the realm deiform did bear us on,
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* As swift almost as ye the heavens behold.
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* Upward gazed Beatrice, and I at her;
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* And in such space perchance as strikes a bolt
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* And flies, and from the notch unlocks itself,
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* Arrived I saw me where a wondrous thing
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* Drew to itself my sight; and therefore she
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* From whom no care of mine could be concealed,
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* Towards me turning, blithe as beautiful,
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* Said unto me: \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*“Fix gratefully thy mind\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*On God, who unto the first star has brought us.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* It seemed to me a cloud encompassed us,
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* Luminous, dense, consolidate and bright
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* As adamant on which the sun is striking.
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* Into itself did the eternal pearl
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* Receive us, even as water doth receive
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* A ray of light, remaining still unbroken.
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* If I was body, (and we here conceive not
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* How one dimension tolerates another,
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* Which needs must be if body enter body,)
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* More the desire should be enkindled in us
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* That essence to behold, wherein is seen
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* How God and our own nature were united.
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* There will be seen what we receive by faith,
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* Not demonstrated, but self-evident
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* In guise of the first truth that man believes.
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* I made reply: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“Madonna, as devoutly\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*As most I can do I give thanks to Him\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Who has removed me from the mortal world.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*But tell me what the dusky spots may be\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Upon this body, which below on earth\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Make people tell that fabulous tale of Cain?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* Somewhat she smiled; and then, \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*“If the opinion\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Of mortals be erroneous,”\zsay-e\* she said,
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*“Where’er the key of sense doth not unlock,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Certes, the shafts of wonder should not pierce thee\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Now, forasmuch as, following the senses,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Thou seest that the reason has short wings.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*But tell me what thou think’st of it thyself.”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* And I: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“What seems to us up here diverse,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Is caused, I think, by bodies rare and dense.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* And she: \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*“Right truly shalt thou see immersed\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*In error thy belief, if well thou hearest\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*The argument that I shall make against it.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Lights many the eighth sphere displays to you\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Which in their quality and quantity\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*May noted be of aspects different.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*If this were caused by rare and dense alone,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*One only virtue would there be in all\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Or more or less diffused, or equally.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Virtues diverse must be perforce the fruits\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Of formal principles; and these, save one,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Of course would by thy reasoning be destroyed.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Besides, if rarity were of this dimness\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*The cause thou askest, either through and through\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*This planet thus attenuate were of matter,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Or else, as in a body is apportioned\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*The fat and lean, so in like manner this\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Would in its volume interchange the leaves.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Were it the former, in the sun’s eclipse\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*It would be manifest by the shining through\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Of light, as through aught tenuous interfused.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*This is not so; hence we must scan the other,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*And if it chance the other I demolish,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Then falsified will thy opinion be.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*But if this rarity go not through and through,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*There needs must be a limit, beyond which\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Its contrary prevents the further passing,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*And thence the foreign radiance is reflected,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Even as a colour cometh back from glass,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*The which behind itself concealeth lead.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Now thou wilt say the sunbeam shows itself\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*More dimly there than in the other parts,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*By being there reflected farther back.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*From this reply experiment will free thee\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*If e’er thou try it, which is wont to be\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*The fountain to the rivers of your arts.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Three mirrors shalt thou take, and two remove\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Alike from thee, the other more remote\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Between the former two shall meet thine eyes.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Turned towards these, cause that behind thy back\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Be placed a light, illuming the three mirrors\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*And coming back to thee by all reflected.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Though in its quantity be not so ample\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*The image most remote, there shalt thou see\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*How it perforce is equally resplendent.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Now, as beneath the touches of warm rays\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Naked the subject of the snow remains\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Both of its former colour and its cold,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Thee thus remaining in thy intellect,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Will I inform with such a living light,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*That it shall tremble in its aspect to thee.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Within the heaven of the divine repose\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Revolves a body, in whose virtue lies\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*The being of whatever it contains.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*The following heaven, that has so many eyes,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Divides this being by essences diverse,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Distinguished from it, and by it contained.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*The other spheres, by various differences,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*All the distinctions which they have within them\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Dispose unto their ends and their effects.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Thus do these organs of the world proceed,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*As thou perceivest now, from grade to grade;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Since from above they take, and act beneath.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Observe me well, how through this place I come\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 125 \vp 125\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Unto the truth thou wishest, that hereafter\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 126 \vp 126\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Thou mayst alone know how to keep the ford\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 127 \vp 127\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*The power and motion of the holy spheres,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 128 \vp 128\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*As from the artisan the hammer’s craft,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 129 \vp 129\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Forth from the blessed motors must proceed.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 130 \vp 130\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*The heaven, which lights so manifold make fair,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 131 \vp 131\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*From the Intelligence profound, which turns it,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 132 \vp 132\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*The image takes, and makes of it a seal.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 133 \vp 133\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*And even as the soul within your dust\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 134 \vp 134\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Through members different and accommodated\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 135 \vp 135\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*To faculties diverse expands itself,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 136 \vp 136\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*So likewise this Intelligence diffuses\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 137 \vp 137\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Its virtue multiplied among the stars.\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 138 \vp 138\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Itself revolving on its unity.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 139 \vp 139\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Virtue diverse doth a diverse alloyage\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 140 \vp 140\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Make with the precious body that it quickens,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 141 \vp 141\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*In which, as life in you, it is combined.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 142 \vp 142\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*From the glad nature whence it is derived,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 143 \vp 143\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*The mingled virtue through the body shines,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 144 \vp 144\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Even as gladness through the living pupil.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 145 \vp 145\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*From this proceeds whate’er from light to light\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 146 \vp 146\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Appeareth different, not from dense and rare:\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 147 \vp 147\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*This is the formal principle that produces,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 148 \vp 148\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*According to its goodness, dark and bright.”\zsay-e\*
\c 3
\cp 3
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* That Sun, which erst with love my bosom warmed,
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* Of beauteous truth had unto me discovered,
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* By proving and reproving, the sweet aspect.
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* And, that I might confess myself convinced
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* And confident, so far as was befitting,
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* I lifted more erect my head to speak.
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* But there appeared a vision, which withdrew me
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* So close to it, in order to be seen,
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* That my confession I remembered not.
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* Such as through polished and transparent glass,
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* Or waters crystalline and undisturbed,
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* But not so deep as that their bed be lost,
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* Come back again the outlines of our faces
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* So feeble, that a pearl on forehead white
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* Comes not less speedily unto our eyes;
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* Such saw I many faces prompt to speak,
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* So that I ran in error opposite
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* To that which kindled love ’twixt man and fountain.
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* As soon as I became aware of them,
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* Esteeming them as mirrored semblances,
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* To see of whom they were, mine eyes I turned,
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* And nothing saw, and once more turned them forward
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* Direct into the light of my sweet Guide,
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* Who smiling kindled in her holy eyes.
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*“Marvel thou not,”\zsay-e\* she said to me, \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*“because\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*I smile at this thy puerile conceit,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Since on the truth it trusts not yet its foot,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*But turns thee, as ’tis wont, on emptiness.\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*True substances are these which thou beholdest,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Here relegate for breaking of some vow.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Therefore speak with them, listen and believe;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*For the true light, which giveth peace to them,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Permits them not to turn from it their feet.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* And I unto the shade that seemed most wishful
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* To speak directed me, and I began,
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* As one whom too great eagerness bewilders:
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“O well-created spirit, who in the rays\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Of life eternal dost the sweetness taste\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Which being untasted ne’er is comprehended,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Grateful ’twill be to me, if thou content me\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Both with thy name and with your destiny.”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* Whereat she promptly and with laughing eyes:
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* \zsay-s |who="Piccarda"\*“Our charity doth never shut the doors\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* \zsay-s |who="Piccarda"\*Against a just desire, except as one\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* \zsay-s |who="Piccarda"\*Who wills that all her court be like herself.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* \zsay-s |who="Piccarda"\*I was a virgin sister in the world;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* \zsay-s |who="Piccarda"\*And if thy mind doth contemplate me well,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* \zsay-s |who="Piccarda"\*The being more fair will not conceal me from thee,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* \zsay-s |who="Piccarda"\*But thou shalt recognise I am Piccarda,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* \zsay-s |who="Piccarda"\*Who, stationed here among these other blessed,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* \zsay-s |who="Piccarda"\*Myself am blessed in the slowest sphere.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* \zsay-s |who="Piccarda"\*All our affections, that alone inflamed\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* \zsay-s |who="Piccarda"\*Are in the pleasure of the Holy Ghost,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* \zsay-s |who="Piccarda"\*Rejoice at being of his order formed;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* \zsay-s |who="Piccarda"\*And this allotment, which appears so low,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* \zsay-s |who="Piccarda"\*Therefore is given us, because our vows\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* \zsay-s |who="Piccarda"\*Have been neglected and in some part void.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* Whence I to her: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“In your miraculous aspects\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*There shines I know not what of the divine,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Which doth transform you from our first conceptions.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Therefore I was not swift in my remembrance;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*But what thou tellest me now aids me so,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*That the refiguring is easier to me.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*But tell me, ye who in this place are happy,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Are you desirous of a higher place,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*To see more or to make yourselves more friends?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* First with those other shades she smiled a little;
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* Thereafter answered me so full of gladness,
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* She seemed to burn in the first fire of love:
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* \zsay-s |who="Piccarda"\*“Brother, our will is quieted by virtue\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* \zsay-s |who="Piccarda"\*Of charity, that makes us wish alone\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* \zsay-s |who="Piccarda"\*For what we have, nor gives us thirst for more.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* \zsay-s |who="Piccarda"\*If to be more exalted we aspired,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* \zsay-s |who="Piccarda"\*Discordant would our aspirations be\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* \zsay-s |who="Piccarda"\*Unto the will of Him who here secludes us;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* \zsay-s |who="Piccarda"\*Which thou shalt see finds no place in these circles,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* \zsay-s |who="Piccarda"\*If being in charity is needful here,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* \zsay-s |who="Piccarda"\*And if thou lookest well into its nature;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* \zsay-s |who="Piccarda"\*Nay, ’tis essential to this blest existence\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* \zsay-s |who="Piccarda"\*To keep itself within the will divine,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* \zsay-s |who="Piccarda"\*Whereby our very wishes are made one;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* \zsay-s |who="Piccarda"\*So that, as we are station above station\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* \zsay-s |who="Piccarda"\*Throughout this realm, to all the realm ’tis pleasing,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* \zsay-s |who="Piccarda"\*As to the King, who makes his will our will.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* \zsay-s |who="Piccarda"\*And his will is our peace; this is the sea\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* \zsay-s |who="Piccarda"\*To which is moving onward whatsoever\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* \zsay-s |who="Piccarda"\*It doth create, and all that nature makes.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* Then it was clear to me how everywhere
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* In heaven is Paradise, although the grace
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* Of good supreme there rain not in one measure.
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* But as it comes to pass, if one food sates,
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* And for another still remains the longing,
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* We ask for this, and that decline with thanks,
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* E’en thus did I; with gesture and with word,
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* To learn from her what was the web wherein
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* She did not ply the shuttle to the end.
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* \zsay-s |who="Piccarda"\*“A perfect life and merit high in-heaven\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* \zsay-s |who="Piccarda"\*A lady o’er us,”\zsay-e\* said she, \zsay-s |who="Piccarda"\*“by whose rule\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* \zsay-s |who="Piccarda"\*Down in your world they vest and veil themselves,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* \zsay-s |who="Piccarda"\*That until death they may both watch and sleep\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* \zsay-s |who="Piccarda"\*Beside that Spouse who every vow accepts\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* \zsay-s |who="Piccarda"\*Which charity conformeth to his pleasure.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* \zsay-s |who="Piccarda"\*To follow her, in girlhood from the world\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* \zsay-s |who="Piccarda"\*I fled, and in her habit shut myself,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* \zsay-s |who="Piccarda"\*And pledged me to the pathway of her sect.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* \zsay-s |who="Piccarda"\*Then men accustomed unto evil more\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* \zsay-s |who="Piccarda"\*Than unto good, from the sweet cloister tore me;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* \zsay-s |who="Piccarda"\*God knows what afterward my life became.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* \zsay-s |who="Piccarda"\*This other splendour, which to thee reveals\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* \zsay-s |who="Piccarda"\*Itself on my right side, and is enkindled\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* \zsay-s |who="Piccarda"\*With all the illumination of our sphere,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* \zsay-s |who="Piccarda"\*What of myself I say applies to her;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* \zsay-s |who="Piccarda"\*A nun was she, and likewise from her head\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* \zsay-s |who="Piccarda"\*Was ta’en the shadow of the sacred wimple.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* \zsay-s |who="Piccarda"\*But when she too was to the world returned\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* \zsay-s |who="Piccarda"\*Against her wishes and against good usage,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* \zsay-s |who="Piccarda"\*Of the heart’s veil she never was divested.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* \zsay-s |who="Piccarda"\*Of great Costanza this is the effulgence,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* \zsay-s |who="Piccarda"\*Who from the second wind of Suabia\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* \zsay-s |who="Piccarda"\*Brought forth the third and latest puissance.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* Thus unto me she spake, and then began
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* \zsay-s |who="Piccarda"\*“Ave Maria”\zsay-e\* singing, and in singing
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* Vanished, as through deep water something heavy.
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* My sight, that followed her as long a time
\q1 \v 125 \vp 125\vp* As it was possible, when it had lost her
\q1 \v 126 \vp 126\vp* Turned round unto the mark of more desire,
\b
\q1 \v 127 \vp 127\vp* And wholly unto Beatrice reverted;
\q1 \v 128 \vp 128\vp* But she such lightnings flashed into mine eyes,
\q1 \v 129 \vp 129\vp* That at the first my sight endured it not;
\b
\q1 \v 130 \vp 130\vp* And this in questioning more backward made me.
\c 4
\cp 4
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* Between two viands, equally removed
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* And tempting, a free man would die of hunger
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* Ere either he could bring unto his teeth.
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* So would a lamb between the ravenings
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* Of two fierce wolves stand fearing both alike;
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* And so would stand a dog between two does.
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* Hence, if I held my peace, myself I blame not,
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* Impelled in equal measure by my doubts,
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* Since it must be so, nor do I commend.
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* I held my peace; but my desire was painted
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* Upon my face, and questioning with that
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* More fervent far than by articulate speech.
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* Beatrice did as Daniel had done
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* Relieving Nebuchadnezzar from the wrath
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* Which rendered him unjustly merciless,
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* And said: \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*“Well see I how attracteth thee\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*One and the other wish, so that thy care\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Binds itself so that forth it does not breathe.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Thou arguest, if good will be permanent,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*The violence of others, for what reason\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Doth it decrease the measure of my merit?\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Again for doubting furnish thee occasion\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Souls seeming to return unto the stars,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*According to the sentiment of Plato.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*These are the questions which upon thy wish\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Are thrusting equally; and therefore first\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Will I treat that which hath the most of gall.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*He of the Seraphim most absorbed in God,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Moses, and Samuel, and whichever John\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Thou mayst select, I say, and even Mary,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Have not in any other heaven their seats,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Than have those spirits that just appeared to thee,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Nor of existence more or fewer years;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*But all make beautiful the primal circle,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*And have sweet life in different degrees,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*By feeling more or less the eternal breath.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*They showed themselves here, not because allotted\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*This sphere has been to them, but to give sign\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Of the celestial which is least exalted.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*To speak thus is adapted to your mind,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Since only through the sense it apprehendeth\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*What then it worthy makes of intellect.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*On this account the Scripture condescends\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Unto your faculties, and feet and hands\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*To God attributes, and means something else;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*And Holy Church under an aspect human\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Gabriel and Michael represent to you,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*And him who made Tobias whole again.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*That which Timaeus argues of the soul\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Doth not resemble that which here is seen,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Because it seems that as he speaks he thinks.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*He says the soul unto its star returns,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Believing it to have been severed thence\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Whenever nature gave it as a form.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Perhaps his doctrine is of other guise\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Than the words sound, and possibly may be\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*With meaning that is not to be derided.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*If he doth mean that to these wheels return\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*The honour of their influence and the blame,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Perhaps his bow doth hit upon some truth.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*This principle ill understood once warped\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*The whole world nearly, till it went astray\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Invoking Jove and Mercury and Mars.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*The other doubt which doth disquiet thee\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Less venom has, for its malevolence\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Could never lead thee otherwhere from me.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*That as unjust our justice should appear\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*In eyes of mortals, is an argument\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Of faith, and not of sin heretical.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*But still, that your perception may be able\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*To thoroughly penetrate this verity,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*As thou desirest, I will satisfy thee.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*If it be violence when he who suffers\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Co-operates not with him who uses force,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*These souls were not on that account excused;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*For will is never quenched unless it will,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*But operates as nature doth in fire\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*If violence a thousand times distort it.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Hence, if it yieldeth more or less, it seconds\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*The force; and these have done so, having power\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Of turning back unto the holy place.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*If their will had been perfect, like to that\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Which Lawrence fast upon his gridiron held,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*And Mutius made severe to his own hand,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*It would have urged them back along the road\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Whence they were dragged, as soon as they were free;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*But such a solid will is all too rare.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*And by these words, if thou hast gathered them\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*As thou shouldst do, the argument is refuted\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*That would have still annoyed thee many times.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*But now another passage runs across\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Before thine eyes, and such that by thyself\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Thou couldst not thread it ere thou wouldst be weary.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*I have for certain put into thy mind\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*That soul beatified could never lie,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*For it is near the primal Truth,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*And then thou from Piccarda might’st have heard\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Costanza kept affection for the veil,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*So that she seemeth here to contradict me.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Many times, brother, has it come to pass,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*That, to escape from peril, with reluctance\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*That has been done it was not right to do,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*E’en as Alcmaeon (who, being by his father\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Thereto entreated, his own mother slew)\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Not to lose pity pitiless became.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*At this point I desire thee to remember\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*That force with will commingles, and they cause\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*That the offences cannot be excused.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Will absolute consenteth not to evil;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*But in so far consenteth as it fears,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*If it refrain, to fall into more harm.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Hence when Piccarda uses this expression,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*She meaneth the will absolute, and I\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*The other, so that both of us speak truth.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* Such was the flowing of the holy river
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* That issued from the fount whence springs all truth;
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* This put to rest my wishes one and all.
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“O love of the first lover, O divine,”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* Said I forthwith, \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“whose speech inundates me\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*And warms me so, it more and more revives me,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*My own affection is not so profound\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*As to suffice in rendering grace for grace;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Let Him, who sees and can, thereto respond.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Well I perceive that never sated is\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 125 \vp 125\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Our intellect unless the Truth illume it,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 126 \vp 126\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Beyond which nothing true expands itself.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 127 \vp 127\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*It rests therein, as wild beast in his lair,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 128 \vp 128\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*When it attains it; and it can attain it;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 129 \vp 129\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*If not, then each desire would frustrate be.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 130 \vp 130\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Therefore springs up, in fashion of a shoot,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 131 \vp 131\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Doubt at the foot of truth; and this is nature,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 132 \vp 132\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Which to the top from height to height impels us.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 133 \vp 133\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*This doth invite me, this assurance give me\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 134 \vp 134\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*With reverence, Lady, to inquire of you\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 135 \vp 135\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Another truth, which is obscure to me.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 136 \vp 136\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*I wish to know if man can satisfy you\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 137 \vp 137\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*For broken vows with other good deeds, so\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 138 \vp 138\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*That in your balance they will not be light.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 139 \vp 139\vp* Beatrice gazed upon me with her eyes
\q1 \v 140 \vp 140\vp* Full of the sparks of love, and so divine,
\q1 \v 141 \vp 141\vp* That, overcome my power, I turned my back
\b
\q1 \v 142 \vp 142\vp* And almost lost myself with eyes downcast.
\c 5
\cp 5
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*“If in the heat of love I flame upon thee\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Beyond the measure that on earth is seen,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*So that the valour of thine eyes I vanquish,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Marvel thou not thereat; for this proceeds\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*From perfect sight, which as it apprehends\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*To the good apprehended moves its feet.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Well I perceive how is already shining\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Into thine intellect the eternal light,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*That only seen enkindles always love;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*And if some other thing your love seduce,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*’Tis nothing but a vestige of the same,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Ill understood, which there is shining through.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Thou fain wouldst know if with another service\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*For broken vow can such return be made\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*As to secure the soul from further claim.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* This Canto thus did Beatrice begin;
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* And, as a man who breaks not off his speech,
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* Continued thus her holy argument:
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*“The greatest gift that in his largess God\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Creating made, and unto his own goodness\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Nearest conformed, and that which he doth prize\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Most highly, is the freedom of the will,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Wherewith the creatures of intelligence\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Both all and only were and are endowed.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Now wilt thou see, if thence thou reasonest,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*The high worth of a vow, if it be made\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*So that when thou consentest God consents:\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*For, closing between God and man the compact,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*A sacrifice is of this treasure made,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Such as I say, and made by its own act.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*What can be rendered then as compensation?\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Think’st thou to make good use of what thou’st offered,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*With gains ill gotten thou wouldst do good deed.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Now art thou certain of the greater point;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*But because Holy Church in this dispenses,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Which seems against the truth which I have shown thee,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Behoves thee still to sit awhile at table,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Because the solid food which thou hast taken\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Requireth further aid for thy digestion.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Open thy mind to that which I reveal,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*And fix it there within; for ’tis not knowledge,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*The having heard without retaining it.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*In the essence of this sacrifice two things\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Convene together; and the one is that\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Of which ’tis made, the other is the agreement.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*This last for evermore is cancelled not\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Unless complied with, and concerning this\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*With such precision has above been spoken.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Therefore it was enjoined upon the Hebrews\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*To offer still, though sometimes what was offered\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Might be commuted, as thou ought’st to know.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*The other, which is known to thee as matter,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*May well indeed be such that one errs not\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*If it for other matter be exchanged.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*But let none shift the burden on his shoulder\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*At his arbitrament, without the turning\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Both of the white and of the yellow key;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*And every permutation deem as foolish,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*If in the substitute the thing relinquished,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*As the four is in six, be not contained.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Therefore whatever thing has so great weight\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*In value that it drags down every balance,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Cannot be satisfied with other spending.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Let mortals never take a vow in jest;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Be faithful and not blind in doing that,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*As Jephthah was in his first offering,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Whom more beseemed to say, ‘I have done wrong,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Than to do worse by keeping; and as foolish\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Thou the great leader of the Greeks wilt find,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Whence wept Iphigenia her fair face,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*And made for her both wise and simple weep,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Who heard such kind of worship spoken of.’\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Christians, be ye more serious in your movements;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Be ye not like a feather at each wind,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*And think not every water washes you.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Ye have the Old and the New Testament,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*And the Pastor of the Church who guideth you\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Let this suffice you unto your salvation.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*If evil appetite cry aught else to you,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Be ye as men, and not as silly sheep,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*So that the Jew among you may not mock you.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Be ye not as the lamb that doth abandon\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Its mother’s milk, and frolicsome and simple\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Combats at its own pleasure with itself.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* Thus Beatrice to me even as I write it;
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* Then all desireful turned herself again
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* To that part where the world is most alive.
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* Her silence and her change of countenance
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* Silence imposed upon my eager mind,
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* That had already in advance new questions;
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* And as an arrow that upon the mark
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* Strikes ere the bowstring quiet hath become,
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* So did we speed into the second realm.
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* My Lady there so joyful I beheld,
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* As into the brightness of that heaven she entered,
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* More luminous thereat the planet grew;
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* And if the star itself was changed and smiled,
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* What became I, who by my nature am
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* Exceeding mutable in every guise!
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* As, in a fish-pond which is pure and tranquil,
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* The fishes draw to that which from without
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* Comes in such fashion that their food they deem it;
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* So I beheld more than a thousand splendours
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* Drawing towards us, and in each was heard:
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* \zsay-s |who="The spirits"\*“Lo, this is she who shall increase our love.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* And as each one was coming unto us,
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* Full of beatitude the shade was seen,
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* By the effulgence clear that issued from it.
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* Think, Reader, if what here is just beginning
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* No farther should proceed, how thou wouldst have
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* An agonizing need of knowing more;
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* And of thyself thou’lt see how I from these
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* Was in desire of hearing their conditions,
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* As they unto mine eyes were manifest.
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*“O thou well-born, unto whom Grace concedes\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*To see the thrones of the eternal triumph,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*Or ever yet the warfare be abandoned\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*With light that through the whole of heaven is spread\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*Kindled are we, and hence if thou desirest\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*To know of us, at thine own pleasure sate thee.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* Thus by some one among those holy spirits
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* Was spoken, and by Beatrice: \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*“Speak, speak\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Securely, and believe them even as Gods.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“Well I perceive how thou dost nest thyself\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 125 \vp 125\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*In thine own light, and drawest it from thine eyes,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 126 \vp 126\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Because they coruscate when thou dost smile,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 127 \vp 127\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*But know not who thou art, nor why thou hast,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 128 \vp 128\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Spirit august, thy station in the sphere\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 129 \vp 129\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*That veils itself to men in alien rays.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 130 \vp 130\vp* This said I in direction of the light
\q1 \v 131 \vp 131\vp* Which first had spoken to me; whence it became
\q1 \v 132 \vp 132\vp* By far more lucent than it was before.
\b
\q1 \v 133 \vp 133\vp* Even as the sun, that doth conceal himself
\q1 \v 134 \vp 134\vp* By too much light, when heat has worn away
\q1 \v 135 \vp 135\vp* The tempering influence of the vapours dense,
\b
\q1 \v 136 \vp 136\vp* By greater rapture thus concealed itself
\q1 \v 137 \vp 137\vp* In its own radiance the figure saintly,
\q1 \v 138 \vp 138\vp* And thus close, close enfolded answered me
\b
\q1 \v 139 \vp 139\vp* In fashion as the following Canto sings.
\c 6
\cp 6
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*“After that Constantine the eagle turned\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*Against the course of heaven, which it had followed\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*Behind the ancient who Lavinia took,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*Two hundred years and more the bird of God\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*In the extreme of Europe held itself,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*Near to the mountains whence it issued first;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*And under shadow of the sacred plumes\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*It governed there the world from hand to hand,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*And, changing thus, upon mine own alighted.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*Caesar I was, and am Justinian,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*Who, by the will of primal Love I feel,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*Took from the laws the useless and redundant;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*And ere unto the work I was attent,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*One nature to exist in Christ, not more,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*Believed, and with such faith was I contented.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*But blessed Agapetus, he who was\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*The supreme pastor, to the faith sincere\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*Pointed me out the way by words of his.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*Him I believed, and what was his assertion\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*I now see clearly, even as thou seest\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*Each contradiction to be false and true.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*As soon as with the Church I moved my feet,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*God in his grace it pleased with this high task\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*To inspire me, and I gave me wholly to it,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*And to my Belisarius I commended\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*The arms, to which was heaven’s right hand so joined\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*It was a signal that I should repose.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*Now here to the first question terminates\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*My answer; but the character thereof\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*Constrains me to continue with a sequel,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*In order that thou see with how great reason\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*Men move against the standard sacrosanct,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*Both who appropriate and who oppose it.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*Behold how great a power has made it worthy\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*Of reverence, beginning from the hour\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*When Pallas died to give it sovereignty.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*Thou knowest it made in Alba its abode\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*Three hundred years and upward, till at last\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*The three to three fought for it yet again.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*Thou knowest what it achieved from Sabine wrong\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*Down to Lucretia’s sorrow, in seven kings\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*O’ercoming round about the neighboring nations;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*Thou knowest what it achieved, borne by the Romans\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*Illustrious against Brennus, against Pyrrhus,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*Against the other princes and confederates.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*Torquatus thence and Quinctius, who from locks\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*Unkempt was named, Decii and Fabii,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*Received the fame I willingly embalm;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*It struck to earth the pride of the Arabians,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*Who, following Hannibal, had passed across\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*The Alpine ridges, Po, from which thou glidest;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*Beneath it triumphed while they yet were young\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*Pompey and Scipio, and to the hill\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*Beneath which thou wast born it bitter seemed;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*Then, near unto the time when heaven had willed\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*To bring the whole world to its mood serene,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*Did Caesar by the will of Rome assume it.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*What it achieved from Var unto the Rhine,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*Isere beheld and Saone, beheld the Seine,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*And every valley whence the Rhone is filled;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*What it achieved when it had left Ravenna,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*And leaped the Rubicon, was such a flight\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*That neither tongue nor pen could follow it.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*Round towards Spain it wheeled its legions; then\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*Towards Durazzo, and Pharsalia smote\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*That to the calid Nile was felt the pain.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*Antandros and the Simois, whence it started,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*It saw again, and there where Hector lies,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*And ill for Ptolemy then roused itself.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*From thence it came like lightning upon Juba;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*Then wheeled itself again into your West,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*Where the Pompeian clarion it heard.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*From what it wrought with the next standard-bearer\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*Brutus and Cassius howl in Hell together,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*And Modena and Perugia dolent were;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*Still doth the mournful Cleopatra weep\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*Because thereof, who, fleeing from before it,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*Took from the adder sudden and black death.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*With him it ran even to the Red Sea shore;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*With him it placed the world in so great peace,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*That unto Janus was his temple closed.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*But what the standard that has made me speak\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*Achieved before, and after should achieve\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*Throughout the mortal realm that lies beneath it,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*Becometh in appearance mean and dim,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*If in the hand of the third Caesar seen\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*With eye unclouded and affection pure,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*Because the living Justice that inspires me\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*Granted it, in the hand of him I speak of,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*The glory of doing vengeance for its wrath.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*Now here attend to what I answer thee;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*Later it ran with Titus to do vengeance\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*Upon the vengeance of the ancient sin.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*And when the tooth of Lombardy had bitten\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*The Holy Church, then underneath its wings\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*Did Charlemagne victorious succor her.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*Now hast thou power to judge of such as those\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*Whom I accused above, and of their crimes,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*Which are the cause of all your miseries.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*To the public standard one the yellow lilies\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*Opposes, the other claims it for a party,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*So that ’tis hard to see which sins the most.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*Let, let the Ghibellines ply their handicraft\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*Beneath some other standard; for this ever\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*Ill follows he who it and justice parts.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*And let not this new Charles e’er strike it down,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*He and his Guelfs, but let him fear the talons\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*That from a nobler lion stripped the fell.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*Already oftentimes the sons have wept\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*The father’s crime; and let him not believe\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*That God will change His scutcheon for the lilies.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*This little planet doth adorn itself\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*With the good spirits that have active been,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*That fame and honour might come after them;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*And whensoever the desires mount thither,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*Thus deviating, must perforce the rays\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*Of the true love less vividly mount upward.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*But in commensuration of our wages\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*With our desert is portion of our joy,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*Because we see them neither less nor greater.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*Herein doth living Justice sweeten so\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*Affection in us, that for evermore\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*It cannot warp to any iniquity.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*Voices diverse make up sweet melodies;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 125 \vp 125\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*So in this life of ours the seats diverse\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 126 \vp 126\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*Render sweet harmony among these spheres;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 127 \vp 127\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*And in the compass of this present pearl\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 128 \vp 128\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*Shineth the sheen of Romeo, of whom\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 129 \vp 129\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*The grand and beauteous work was ill rewarded.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 130 \vp 130\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*But the Provencals who against him wrought,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 131 \vp 131\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*They have not laughed, and therefore ill goes he\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 132 \vp 132\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*Who makes his hurt of the good deeds of others.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 133 \vp 133\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*Four daughters, and each one of them a queen,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 134 \vp 134\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*Had Raymond Berenger, and this for him\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 135 \vp 135\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*Did Romeo, a poor man and a pilgrim;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 136 \vp 136\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*And then malicious words incited him\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 137 \vp 137\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*To summon to a reckoning this just man,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 138 \vp 138\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*Who rendered to him seven and five for ten.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 139 \vp 139\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*Then he departed poor and stricken in years,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 140 \vp 140\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*And if the world could know the heart he had,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 141 \vp 141\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*In begging bit by bit his livelihood,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 142 \vp 142\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*Though much it laud him, it would laud him more.”\zsay-e\*
\c 7
\cp 7
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*“Osanna sanctus Deus Sabaoth,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*Superillustrans claritate tua\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* \zsay-s |who="Justinian"\*Felices ignes horum malahoth!”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* In this wise, to his melody returning,
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* This substance, upon which a double light
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* Doubles itself, was seen by me to sing,
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* And to their dance this and the others moved,
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* And in the manner of swift-hurrying sparks
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* Veiled themselves from me with a sudden distance.
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* Doubting was I, and saying, \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“Tell her, tell her,”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* Within me, \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“tell her,”\zsay-e\* saying, \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“tell my Lady,”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* Who slakes my thirst with her sweet effluences;
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* And yet that reverence which doth lord it over
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* The whole of me only by B and ICE,
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* Bowed me again like unto one who drowses.
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* Short while did Beatrice endure me thus;
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* And she began, lighting me with a smile
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* Such as would make one happy in the fire:
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*“According to infallible advisement,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*After what manner a just vengeance justly\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Could be avenged has put thee upon thinking,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*But I will speedily thy mind unloose;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*And do thou listen, for these words of mine\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Of a great doctrine will a present make thee.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*By not enduring on the power that wills\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Curb for his good, that man who ne’er was born,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Damning himself damned all his progeny;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Whereby the human species down below\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Lay sick for many centuries in great error,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Till to descend it pleased the Word of God\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*To where the nature, which from its own Maker\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Estranged itself, he joined to him in person\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*By the sole act of his eternal love.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Now unto what is said direct thy sight;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*This nature when united to its Maker,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Such as created, was sincere and good;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*But by itself alone was banished forth\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*From Paradise, because it turned aside\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Out of the way of truth and of its life.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Therefore the penalty the cross held out,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*If measured by the nature thus assumed,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*None ever yet with so great justice stung,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*And none was ever of so great injustice,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Considering who the Person was that suffered,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Within whom such a nature was contracted.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*From one act therefore issued things diverse;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*To God and to the Jews one death was pleasing;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Earth trembled at it and the Heaven was opened.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*It should no longer now seem difficult\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*To thee, when it is said that a just vengeance\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*By a just court was afterward avenged.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*But now do I behold thy mind entangled\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*From thought to thought within a knot, from which\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*With great desire it waits to free itself.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Thou sayest, ‘Well discern I what I hear;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*But it is hidden from me why God willed\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*For our redemption only this one mode.’\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Buried remaineth, brother, this decree\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Unto the eyes of every one whose nature\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Is in the flame of love not yet adult.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Verily, inasmuch as at this mark\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*One gazes long and little is discerned,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Wherefore this mode was worthiest will I say.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Goodness Divine, which from itself doth spurn\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*All envy, burning in itself so sparkles\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*That the eternal beauties it unfolds.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Whate’er from this immediately distils\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Has afterwards no end, for ne’er removed\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Is its impression when it sets its seal.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Whate’er from this immediately rains down\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Is wholly free, because it is not subject\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Unto the influences of novel things.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*The more conformed thereto, the more it pleases;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*For the blest ardour that irradiates all things\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*In that most like itself is most vivacious.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*With all of these things has advantaged been\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*The human creature; and if one be wanting,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*From his nobility he needs must fall.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*’Tis sin alone which doth disfranchise him,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*And render him unlike the Good Supreme,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*So that he little with its light is blanched,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*And to his dignity no more returns,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Unless he fill up where transgression empties\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*With righteous pains for criminal delights.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Your nature when it sinned so utterly\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*In its own seed, out of these dignities\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Even as out of Paradise was driven,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Nor could itself recover, if thou notest\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*With nicest subtilty, by any way,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Except by passing one of these two fords:\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Either that God through clemency alone\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Had pardon granted, or that man himself\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Had satisfaction for his folly made.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Fix now thine eye deep into the abyss\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Of the eternal counsel, to my speech\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*As far as may be fastened steadfastly!\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Man in his limitations had not power\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*To satisfy, not having power to sink\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*In his humility obeying then,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Far as he disobeying thought to rise;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*And for this reason man has been from power\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Of satisfying by himself excluded.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Therefore it God behoved in his own ways\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Man to restore unto his perfect life,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*I say in one, or else in both of them.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*But since the action of the doer is\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*So much more grateful, as it more presents\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*The goodness of the heart from which it issues,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Goodness Divine, that doth imprint the world,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Has been contented to proceed by each\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*And all its ways to lift you up again;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Nor ’twixt the first day and the final night\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Such high and such magnificent proceeding\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*By one or by the other was or shall be;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*For God more bounteous was himself to give\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*To make man able to uplift himself,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Than if he only of himself had pardoned;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*And all the other modes were insufficient\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*For justice, were it not the Son of God\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Himself had humbled to become incarnate.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Now, to fill fully each desire of thine,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Return I to elucidate one place,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*In order that thou there mayst see as I do.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Thou sayst: ‘I see the air, I see the fire,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 125 \vp 125\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*The water, and the earth, and all their mixtures\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 126 \vp 126\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Come to corruption, and short while endure;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 127 \vp 127\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*And these things notwithstanding were created;’\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 128 \vp 128\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Therefore if that which I have said were true,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 129 \vp 129\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*They should have been secure against corruption.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 130 \vp 130\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*The Angels, brother, and the land sincere\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 131 \vp 131\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*In which thou art, created may be called\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 132 \vp 132\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Just as they are in their entire existence;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 133 \vp 133\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*But all the elements which thou hast named,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 134 \vp 134\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*And all those things which out of them are made,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 135 \vp 135\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*By a created virtue are informed.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 136 \vp 136\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Created was the matter which they have;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 137 \vp 137\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Created was the informing influence\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 138 \vp 138\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Within these stars that round about them go.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 139 \vp 139\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*The soul of every brute and of the plants\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 140 \vp 140\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*By its potential temperament attracts\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 141 \vp 141\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*The ray and motion of the holy lights;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 142 \vp 142\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*But your own life immediately inspires\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 143 \vp 143\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Supreme Beneficence, and enamours it\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 144 \vp 144\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*So with herself, it evermore desires her.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 145 \vp 145\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*And thou from this mayst argue furthermore\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 146 \vp 146\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Your resurrection, if thou think again\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 147 \vp 147\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*How human flesh was fashioned at that time\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 148 \vp 148\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*When the first parents both of them were made.”\zsay-e\*
\c 8
\cp 8
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* The world used in its peril to believe
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* That the fair Cypria delirious love
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* Rayed out, in the third epicycle turning;
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* Wherefore not only unto her paid honour
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* Of sacrifices and of votive cry
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* The ancient nations in the ancient error,
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* But both Dione honoured they and Cupid,
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* That as her mother, this one as her son,
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* And said that he had sat in Dido’s lap;
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* And they from her, whence I beginning take,
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* Took the denomination of the star
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* That woos the sun, now following, now in front.
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* I was not ware of our ascending to it;
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* But of our being in it gave full faith
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* My Lady whom I saw more beauteous grow.
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* And as within a flame a spark is seen,
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* And as within a voice a voice discerned,
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* When one is steadfast, and one comes and goes,
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* Within that light beheld I other lamps
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* Move in a circle, speeding more and less,
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* Methinks in measure of their inward vision.
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* From a cold cloud descended never winds,
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* Or visible or not, so rapidly
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* They would not laggard and impeded seem
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* To any one who had those lights divine
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* Seen come towards us, leaving the gyration
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* Begun at first in the high Seraphim.
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* And behind those that most in front appeared
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* Sounded \zsay-s |who="The spirits"\*“Osanna!”\zsay-e\* so that never since
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* To hear again was I without desire.
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* Then unto us more nearly one approached,
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* And it alone began: \zsay-s |who="Charles Martel"\*“We all are ready\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* \zsay-s |who="Charles Martel"\*Unto thy pleasure, that thou joy in us.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* \zsay-s |who="Charles Martel"\*We turn around with the celestial Princes,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* \zsay-s |who="Charles Martel"\*One gyre and one gyration and one thirst,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* \zsay-s |who="Charles Martel"\*To whom thou in the world of old didst say,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* \zsay-s |who="Charles Martel"\*‘Ye who, intelligent, the third heaven are moving;’\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* \zsay-s |who="Charles Martel"\*And are so full of love, to pleasure thee\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* \zsay-s |who="Charles Martel"\*A little quiet will not be less sweet.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* After these eyes of mine themselves had offered
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* Unto my Lady reverently, and she
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* Content and certain of herself had made them,
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* Back to the light they turned, which so great promise
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* Made of itself, and \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“Say, who art thou?”\zsay-e\* was
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* My voice, imprinted with a great affection.
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* O how and how much I beheld it grow
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* With the new joy that superadded was
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* Unto its joys, as soon as I had spoken!
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* Thus changed, it said to me: \zsay-s |who="Charles Martel"\*“The world possessed me\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* \zsay-s |who="Charles Martel"\*Short time below; and, if it had been more,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* \zsay-s |who="Charles Martel"\*Much evil will be which would not have been.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* \zsay-s |who="Charles Martel"\*My gladness keepeth me concealed from thee,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* \zsay-s |who="Charles Martel"\*Which rayeth round about me, and doth hide me\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* \zsay-s |who="Charles Martel"\*Like as a creature swathed in its own silk.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* \zsay-s |who="Charles Martel"\*Much didst thou love me, and thou hadst good reason;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* \zsay-s |who="Charles Martel"\*For had I been below, I should have shown thee\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* \zsay-s |who="Charles Martel"\*Somewhat beyond the foliage of my love.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* \zsay-s |who="Charles Martel"\*That left-hand margin, which doth bathe itself\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* \zsay-s |who="Charles Martel"\*In Rhone, when it is mingled with the Sorgue,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* \zsay-s |who="Charles Martel"\*Me for its lord awaited in due time,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* \zsay-s |who="Charles Martel"\*And that horn of Ausonia, which is towned\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* \zsay-s |who="Charles Martel"\*With Bari, with Gaeta and Catona,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* \zsay-s |who="Charles Martel"\*Whence Tronto and Verde in the sea disgorge.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* \zsay-s |who="Charles Martel"\*Already flashed upon my brow the crown\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* \zsay-s |who="Charles Martel"\*Of that dominion which the Danube waters\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* \zsay-s |who="Charles Martel"\*After the German borders it abandons;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* \zsay-s |who="Charles Martel"\*And beautiful Trinacria, that is murky\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* \zsay-s |who="Charles Martel"\*’Twixt Pachino and Peloro, (on the gulf\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* \zsay-s |who="Charles Martel"\*Which greatest scath from Eurus doth receive,)\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* \zsay-s |who="Charles Martel"\*Not through Typhoeus, but through nascent sulphur,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* \zsay-s |who="Charles Martel"\*Would have awaited her own monarchs still,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* \zsay-s |who="Charles Martel"\*Through me from Charles descended and from Rudolph,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* \zsay-s |who="Charles Martel"\*If evil lordship, that exasperates ever\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* \zsay-s |who="Charles Martel"\*The subject populations, had not moved\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* \zsay-s |who="Charles Martel"\*Palermo to the outcry of ‘Death! death!’\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* \zsay-s |who="Charles Martel"\*And if my brother could but this foresee,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* \zsay-s |who="Charles Martel"\*The greedy poverty of Catalonia\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* \zsay-s |who="Charles Martel"\*Straight would he flee, that it might not molest him;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* \zsay-s |who="Charles Martel"\*For verily ’tis needful to provide,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* \zsay-s |who="Charles Martel"\*Through him or other, so that on his bark\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* \zsay-s |who="Charles Martel"\*Already freighted no more freight be placed.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* \zsay-s |who="Charles Martel"\*His nature, which from liberal covetous\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* \zsay-s |who="Charles Martel"\*Descended, such a soldiery would need\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* \zsay-s |who="Charles Martel"\*As should not care for hoarding in a chest.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“Because I do believe the lofty joy\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Thy speech infuses into me, my Lord,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Where every good thing doth begin and end\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Thou seest as I see it, the more grateful\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Is it to me; and this too hold I dear,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*That gazing upon God thou dost discern it.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Glad hast thou made me; so make clear to me,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Since speaking thou hast stirred me up to doubt,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*How from sweet seed can bitter issue forth.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* This I to him; and he to me: \zsay-s |who="Charles Martel"\*“If I\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* \zsay-s |who="Charles Martel"\*Can show to thee a truth, to what thou askest\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* \zsay-s |who="Charles Martel"\*Thy face thou’lt hold as thou dost hold thy back.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* \zsay-s |who="Charles Martel"\*The Good which all the realm thou art ascending\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* \zsay-s |who="Charles Martel"\*Turns and contents, maketh its providence\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* \zsay-s |who="Charles Martel"\*To be a power within these bodies vast;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* \zsay-s |who="Charles Martel"\*And not alone the natures are foreseen\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* \zsay-s |who="Charles Martel"\*Within the mind that in itself is perfect,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* \zsay-s |who="Charles Martel"\*But they together with their preservation.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* \zsay-s |who="Charles Martel"\*For whatsoever thing this bow shoots forth\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* \zsay-s |who="Charles Martel"\*Falls foreordained unto an end foreseen,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* \zsay-s |who="Charles Martel"\*Even as a shaft directed to its mark.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* \zsay-s |who="Charles Martel"\*If that were not, the heaven which thou dost walk\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* \zsay-s |who="Charles Martel"\*Would in such manner its effects produce,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* \zsay-s |who="Charles Martel"\*That they no longer would be arts, but ruins.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* \zsay-s |who="Charles Martel"\*This cannot be, if the Intelligences\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* \zsay-s |who="Charles Martel"\*That keep these stars in motion are not maimed,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* \zsay-s |who="Charles Martel"\*And maimed the First that has not made them perfect.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* \zsay-s |who="Charles Martel"\*Wilt thou this truth have clearer made to thee?”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* And I: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“Not so; for ’tis impossible\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*That nature tire, I see, in what is needful.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* Whence he again: \zsay-s |who="Charles Martel"\*“Now say, would it be worse\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* \zsay-s |who="Charles Martel"\*For men on earth were they not citizens?”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“Yes,”\zsay-e\* I replied; \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“and here I ask no reason.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* \zsay-s |who="Charles Martel"\*“And can they be so, if below they live not\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* \zsay-s |who="Charles Martel"\*Diversely unto offices diverse?\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* \zsay-s |who="Charles Martel"\*No, if your master writeth well for you.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* So came he with deductions to this point;
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* Then he concluded: \zsay-s |who="Charles Martel"\*“Therefore it behoves\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* \zsay-s |who="Charles Martel"\*The roots of your effects to be diverse.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* \zsay-s |who="Charles Martel"\*Hence one is Solon born, another Xerxes,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 125 \vp 125\vp* \zsay-s |who="Charles Martel"\*Another Melchisedec, and another he\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 126 \vp 126\vp* \zsay-s |who="Charles Martel"\*Who, flying through the air, his son did lose.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 127 \vp 127\vp* \zsay-s |who="Charles Martel"\*Revolving Nature, which a signet is\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 128 \vp 128\vp* \zsay-s |who="Charles Martel"\*To mortal wax, doth practise well her art,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 129 \vp 129\vp* \zsay-s |who="Charles Martel"\*But not one inn distinguish from another;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 130 \vp 130\vp* \zsay-s |who="Charles Martel"\*Thence happens it that Esau differeth\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 131 \vp 131\vp* \zsay-s |who="Charles Martel"\*In seed from Jacob; and Quirinus comes\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 132 \vp 132\vp* \zsay-s |who="Charles Martel"\*From sire so vile that he is given to Mars.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 133 \vp 133\vp* \zsay-s |who="Charles Martel"\*A generated nature its own way\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 134 \vp 134\vp* \zsay-s |who="Charles Martel"\*Would always make like its progenitors,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 135 \vp 135\vp* \zsay-s |who="Charles Martel"\*If Providence divine were not triumphant.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 136 \vp 136\vp* \zsay-s |who="Charles Martel"\*Now that which was behind thee is before thee;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 137 \vp 137\vp* \zsay-s |who="Charles Martel"\*But that thou know that I with thee am pleased,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 138 \vp 138\vp* \zsay-s |who="Charles Martel"\*With a corollary will I mantle thee.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 139 \vp 139\vp* \zsay-s |who="Charles Martel"\*Evermore nature, if it fortune find\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 140 \vp 140\vp* \zsay-s |who="Charles Martel"\*Discordant to it, like each other seed\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 141 \vp 141\vp* \zsay-s |who="Charles Martel"\*Out of its region, maketh evil thrift;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 142 \vp 142\vp* \zsay-s |who="Charles Martel"\*And if the world below would fix its mind\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 143 \vp 143\vp* \zsay-s |who="Charles Martel"\*On the foundation which is laid by nature,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 144 \vp 144\vp* \zsay-s |who="Charles Martel"\*Pursuing that, ’twould have the people good.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 145 \vp 145\vp* \zsay-s |who="Charles Martel"\*But you unto religion wrench aside\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 146 \vp 146\vp* \zsay-s |who="Charles Martel"\*Him who was born to gird him with the sword,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 147 \vp 147\vp* \zsay-s |who="Charles Martel"\*And make a king of him who is for sermons;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 148 \vp 148\vp* \zsay-s |who="Charles Martel"\*Therefore your footsteps wander from the road.”\zsay-e\*
\c 9
\cp 9
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* Beautiful Clemence, after that thy Charles
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* Had me enlightened, he narrated to me
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* The treacheries his seed should undergo;
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* But said: \zsay-s |who="Charles Martel"\*“Be still and let the years roll round;”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* So I can only say, that lamentation
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* Legitimate shall follow on your wrongs.
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* And of that holy light the life already
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* Had to the Sun which fills it turned again,
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* As to that good which for each thing sufficeth.
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* Ah, souls deceived, and creatures impious,
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* Who from such good do turn away your hearts,
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* Directing upon vanity your foreheads!
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* And now, behold, another of those splendours
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* Approached me, and its will to pleasure me
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* It signified by brightening outwardly.
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* The eyes of Beatrice, that fastened were
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* Upon me, as before, of dear assent
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* To my desire assurance gave to me.
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“Ah, bring swift compensation to my wish,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Thou blessed spirit,”\zsay-e\* I said, \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“and give me proof\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*That what I think in thee I can reflect!”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* Whereat the light, that still was new to me,
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* Out of its depths, whence it before was singing,
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* As one delighted to do good, continued:
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cunizza"\*“Within that region of the land depraved\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cunizza"\*Of Italy, that lies between Rialto\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cunizza"\*And fountain-heads of Brenta and of Piava,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cunizza"\*Rises a hill, and mounts not very high,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cunizza"\*Wherefrom descended formerly a torch\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cunizza"\*That made upon that region great assault.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cunizza"\*Out of one root were born both I and it;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cunizza"\*Cunizza was I called, and here I shine\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cunizza"\*Because the splendour of this star o’ercame me.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cunizza"\*But gladly to myself the cause I pardon\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cunizza"\*Of my allotment, and it does not grieve me;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cunizza"\*Which would perhaps seem strong unto your vulgar.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cunizza"\*Of this so luculent and precious jewel,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cunizza"\*Which of our heaven is nearest unto me,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cunizza"\*Great fame remained; and ere it die away\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cunizza"\*This hundredth year shall yet quintupled be.\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cunizza"\*See if man ought to make him excellent,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cunizza"\*So that another life the first may leave!\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cunizza"\*And thus thinks not the present multitude\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cunizza"\*Shut in by Adige and Tagliamento,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cunizza"\*Nor yet for being scourged is penitent.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cunizza"\*But soon ’twill be that Padua in the marsh\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cunizza"\*Will change the water that Vicenza bathes,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cunizza"\*Because the folk are stubborn against duty;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cunizza"\*And where the Sile and Cagnano join\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cunizza"\*One lordeth it, and goes with lofty head,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cunizza"\*For catching whom e’en now the net is making.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cunizza"\*Feltro moreover of her impious pastor\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cunizza"\*Shall weep the crime, which shall so monstrous be\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cunizza"\*That for the like none ever entered Malta.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cunizza"\*Ample exceedingly would be the vat\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cunizza"\*That of the Ferrarese could hold the blood,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cunizza"\*And weary who should weigh it ounce by ounce,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cunizza"\*Of which this courteous priest shall make a gift\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cunizza"\*To show himself a partisan; and such gifts\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cunizza"\*Will to the living of the land conform.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cunizza"\*Above us there are mirrors, Thrones you call them,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cunizza"\*From which shines out on us God Judicant,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cunizza"\*So that this utterance seems good to us.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* Here it was silent, and it had the semblance
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* Of being turned elsewhither, by the wheel
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* On which it entered as it was before.
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* The other joy, already known to me,
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* Became a thing transplendent in my sight,
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* As a fine ruby smitten by the sun.
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* Through joy effulgence is acquired above,
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* As here a smile; but down below, the shade
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* Outwardly darkens, as the mind is sad.
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“God seeth all things, and in Him, blest spirit,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Thy sight is,”\zsay-e\* said I, \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“so that never will\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Of his can possibly from thee be hidden;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Thy voice, then, that for ever makes the heavens\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Glad, with the singing of those holy fires\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Which of their six wings make themselves a cowl,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Wherefore does it not satisfy my longings?\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Indeed, I would not wait thy questioning\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*If I in thee were as thou art in me.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* \zsay-s |who="Folquet"\*“The greatest of the valleys where the water\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* \zsay-s |who="Folquet"\*Expands itself,”\zsay-e\* forthwith its words began,
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* \zsay-s |who="Folquet"\*“That sea excepted which the earth engarlands,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* \zsay-s |who="Folquet"\*Between discordant shores against the sun\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* \zsay-s |who="Folquet"\*Extends so far, that it meridian makes\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* \zsay-s |who="Folquet"\*Where it was wont before to make the horizon.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* \zsay-s |who="Folquet"\*I was a dweller on that valley’s shore\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* \zsay-s |who="Folquet"\*’Twixt Ebro and Magra that with journey short\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* \zsay-s |who="Folquet"\*Doth from the Tuscan part the Genoese.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* \zsay-s |who="Folquet"\*With the same sunset and same sunrise nearly\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* \zsay-s |who="Folquet"\*Sit Buggia and the city whence I was,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* \zsay-s |who="Folquet"\*That with its blood once made the harbour hot.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* \zsay-s |who="Folquet"\*Folco that people called me unto whom\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* \zsay-s |who="Folquet"\*My name was known; and now with me this heaven\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* \zsay-s |who="Folquet"\*Imprints itself, as I did once with it;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* \zsay-s |who="Folquet"\*For more the daughter of Belus never burned,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* \zsay-s |who="Folquet"\*Offending both Sichaeus and Creusa,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* \zsay-s |who="Folquet"\*Than I, so long as it became my locks,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* \zsay-s |who="Folquet"\*Nor yet that Rodophean, who deluded\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* \zsay-s |who="Folquet"\*Was by Demophoon, nor yet Alcides,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* \zsay-s |who="Folquet"\*When Iole he in his heart had locked.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* \zsay-s |who="Folquet"\*Yet here is no repenting, but we smile,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* \zsay-s |who="Folquet"\*Not at the fault, which comes not back to mind,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* \zsay-s |who="Folquet"\*But at the power which ordered and foresaw.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* \zsay-s |who="Folquet"\*Here we behold the art that doth adorn\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* \zsay-s |who="Folquet"\*With such affection, and the good discover\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* \zsay-s |who="Folquet"\*Whereby the world above turns that below.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* \zsay-s |who="Folquet"\*But that thou wholly satisfied mayst bear\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* \zsay-s |who="Folquet"\*Thy wishes hence which in this sphere are born,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* \zsay-s |who="Folquet"\*Still farther to proceed behoveth me.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* \zsay-s |who="Folquet"\*Thou fain wouldst know who is within this light\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* \zsay-s |who="Folquet"\*That here beside me thus is scintillating,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* \zsay-s |who="Folquet"\*Even as a sunbeam in the limpid water.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* \zsay-s |who="Folquet"\*Then know thou, that within there is at rest\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* \zsay-s |who="Folquet"\*Rahab, and being to our order joined,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* \zsay-s |who="Folquet"\*With her in its supremest grade ’tis sealed.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* \zsay-s |who="Folquet"\*Into this heaven, where ends the shadowy cone\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* \zsay-s |who="Folquet"\*Cast by your world, before all other souls\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* \zsay-s |who="Folquet"\*First of Christ’s triumph was she taken up.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* \zsay-s |who="Folquet"\*Full meet it was to leave her in some heaven,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* \zsay-s |who="Folquet"\*Even as a palm of the high victory\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* \zsay-s |who="Folquet"\*Which he acquired with one palm and the other,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* \zsay-s |who="Folquet"\*Because she favoured the first glorious deed\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 125 \vp 125\vp* \zsay-s |who="Folquet"\*Of Joshua upon the Holy Land,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 126 \vp 126\vp* \zsay-s |who="Folquet"\*That little stirs the memory of the Pope.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 127 \vp 127\vp* \zsay-s |who="Folquet"\*Thy city, which an offshoot is of him\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 128 \vp 128\vp* \zsay-s |who="Folquet"\*Who first upon his Maker turned his back,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 129 \vp 129\vp* \zsay-s |who="Folquet"\*And whose ambition is so sorely wept,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 130 \vp 130\vp* \zsay-s |who="Folquet"\*Brings forth and scatters the accursed flower\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 131 \vp 131\vp* \zsay-s |who="Folquet"\*Which both the sheep and lambs hath led astray\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 132 \vp 132\vp* \zsay-s |who="Folquet"\*Since it has turned the shepherd to a wolf.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 133 \vp 133\vp* \zsay-s |who="Folquet"\*For this the Evangel and the mighty Doctors\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 134 \vp 134\vp* \zsay-s |who="Folquet"\*Are derelict, and only the Decretals\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 135 \vp 135\vp* \zsay-s |who="Folquet"\*So studied that it shows upon their margins.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 136 \vp 136\vp* \zsay-s |who="Folquet"\*On this are Pope and Cardinals intent;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 137 \vp 137\vp* \zsay-s |who="Folquet"\*Their meditations reach not Nazareth,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 138 \vp 138\vp* \zsay-s |who="Folquet"\*There where his pinions Gabriel unfolded;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 139 \vp 139\vp* \zsay-s |who="Folquet"\*But Vatican and the other parts elect\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 140 \vp 140\vp* \zsay-s |who="Folquet"\*Of Rome, which have a cemetery been\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 141 \vp 141\vp* \zsay-s |who="Folquet"\*Unto the soldiery that followed Peter\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 142 \vp 142\vp* \zsay-s |who="Folquet"\*Shall soon be free from this adultery.”\zsay-e\*
\c 10
\cp 10
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* Looking into his Son with all the Love
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* Which each of them eternally breathes forth,
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* The Primal and unutterable Power
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* Whate’er before the mind or eye revolves
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* With so much order made, there can be none
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* Who this beholds without enjoying Him.
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* Lift up then, Reader, to the lofty wheels
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* With me thy vision straight unto that part
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* Where the one motion on the other strikes,
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* And there begin to contemplate with joy
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* That Master’s art, who in himself so loves it
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* That never doth his eye depart therefrom.
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* Behold how from that point goes branching off
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* The oblique circle, which conveys the planets,
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* To satisfy the world that calls upon them;
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* And if their pathway were not thus inflected,
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* Much virtue in the heavens would be in vain,
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* And almost every power below here dead.
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* If from the straight line distant more or less
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* Were the departure, much would wanting be
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* Above and underneath of mundane order.
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* Remain now, Reader, still upon thy bench,
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* In thought pursuing that which is foretasted,
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* If thou wouldst jocund be instead of weary.
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* I’ve set before thee; henceforth feed thyself,
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* For to itself diverteth all my care
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* That theme whereof I have been made the scribe.
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* The greatest of the ministers of nature,
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* Who with the power of heaven the world imprints
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* And measures with his light the time for us,
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* With that part which above is called to mind
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* Conjoined, along the spirals was revolving,
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* Where each time earlier he presents himself;
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* And I was with him; but of the ascending
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* I was not conscious, saving as a man
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* Of a first thought is conscious ere it come;
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* And Beatrice, she who is seen to pass
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* From good to better, and so suddenly
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* That not by time her action is expressed,
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* How lucent in herself must she have been!
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* And what was in the sun, wherein I entered,
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* Apparent not by colour but by light,
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* I, though I call on genius, art, and practice,
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* Cannot so tell that it could be imagined;
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* Believe one can, and let him long to see it.
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* And if our fantasies too lowly are
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* For altitude so great, it is no marvel,
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* Since o’er the sun was never eye could go.
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* Such in this place was the fourth family
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* Of the high Father, who forever sates it,
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* Showing how he breathes forth and how begets.
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* And Beatrice began: \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*“Give thanks, give thanks\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Unto the Sun of Angels, who to this\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Sensible one has raised thee by his grace!”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* Never was heart of mortal so disposed
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* To worship, nor to give itself to God
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* With all its gratitude was it so ready,
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* As at those words did I myself become;
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* And all my love was so absorbed in Him,
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* That in oblivion Beatrice was eclipsed.
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* Nor this displeased her; but she smiled at it
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* So that the splendour of her laughing eyes
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* My single mind on many things divided.
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* Lights many saw I, vivid and triumphant,
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* Make us a centre and themselves a circle,
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* More sweet in voice than luminous in aspect.
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* Thus girt about the daughter of Latona
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* We sometimes see, when pregnant is the air,
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* So that it holds the thread which makes her zone.
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* Within the court of Heaven, whence I return,
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* Are many jewels found, so fair and precious
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* They cannot be transported from the realm;
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* And of them was the singing of those lights.
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* Who takes not wings that he may fly up thither,
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* The tidings thence may from the dumb await!
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* As soon as singing thus those burning suns
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* Had round about us whirled themselves three times,
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* Like unto stars neighbouring the steadfast poles,
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* Ladies they seemed, not from the dance released,
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* But who stop short, in silence listening
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* Till they have gathered the new melody.
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* And within one I heard beginning: \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*“When\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*The radiance of grace, by which is kindled\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*True love, and which thereafter grows by loving,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Within thee multiplied is so resplendent\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*That it conducts thee upward by that stair,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Where without reascending none descends,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Who should deny the wine out of his vial\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Unto thy thirst, in liberty were not\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Except as water which descends not seaward.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Fain wouldst thou know with what plants is enflowered\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*This garland that encircles with delight\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*The Lady fair who makes thee strong for heaven.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Of the lambs was I of the holy flock\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Which Dominic conducteth by a road\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Where well one fattens if he strayeth not.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*He who is nearest to me on the right\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*My brother and master was; and he Albertus\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Is of Cologne, I Thomas of Aquinum.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*If thou of all the others wouldst be certain,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Follow behind my speaking with thy sight\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Upward along the blessed garland turning.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*That next effulgence issues from the smile\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Of Gratian, who assisted both the courts\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*In such wise that it pleased in Paradise.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*The other which near by adorns our choir\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*That Peter was who, e’en as the poor widow,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Offered his treasure unto Holy Church.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*The fifth light, that among us is the fairest,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Breathes forth from such a love, that all the world\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Below is greedy to learn tidings of it.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Within it is the lofty mind, where knowledge\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*So deep was put, that, if the true be true,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*To see so much there never rose a second.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Thou seest next the lustre of that taper,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Which in the flesh below looked most within\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*The angelic nature and its ministry.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Within that other little light is smiling\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*The advocate of the Christian centuries,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Out of whose rhetoric Augustine was furnished.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Now if thou trainest thy mind’s eye along\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*From light to light pursuant of my praise,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*With thirst already of the eighth thou waitest.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*By seeing every good therein exults\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 125 \vp 125\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*The sainted soul, which the fallacious world\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 126 \vp 126\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Makes manifest to him who listeneth well;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 127 \vp 127\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*The body whence ’twas hunted forth is lying\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 128 \vp 128\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Down in Cieldauro, and from martyrdom\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 129 \vp 129\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*And banishment it came unto this peace.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 130 \vp 130\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*See farther onward flame the burning breath\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 131 \vp 131\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Of Isidore, of Beda, and of Richard\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 132 \vp 132\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Who was in contemplation more than man.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 133 \vp 133\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*This, whence to me returneth thy regard,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 134 \vp 134\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*The light is of a spirit unto whom\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 135 \vp 135\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*In his grave meditations death seemed slow.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 136 \vp 136\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*It is the light eternal of Sigier,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 137 \vp 137\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Who, reading lectures in the Street of Straw,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 138 \vp 138\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Did syllogize invidious verities.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 139 \vp 139\vp* Then, as a horologe that calleth us
\q1 \v 140 \vp 140\vp* What time the Bride of God is rising up
\q1 \v 141 \vp 141\vp* With matins to her Spouse that he may love her,
\b
\q1 \v 142 \vp 142\vp* Wherein one part the other draws and urges,
\q1 \v 143 \vp 143\vp* Ting! ting! resounding with so sweet a note,
\q1 \v 144 \vp 144\vp* That swells with love the spirit well disposed,
\b
\q1 \v 145 \vp 145\vp* Thus I beheld the glorious wheel move round,
\q1 \v 146 \vp 146\vp* And render voice to voice, in modulation
\q1 \v 147 \vp 147\vp* And sweetness that can not be comprehended,
\b
\q1 \v 148 \vp 148\vp* Excepting there where joy is made eternal.
\c 11
\cp 11
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* O Thou insensate care of mortal men,
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* How inconclusive are the syllogisms
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* That make thee beat thy wings in downward flight!
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* One after laws and one to aphorisms
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* Was going, and one following the priesthood,
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* And one to reign by force or sophistry,
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* And one in theft, and one in state affairs,
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* One in the pleasures of the flesh involved
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* Wearied himself, one gave himself to ease;
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* When I, from all these things emancipate,
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* With Beatrice above there in the Heavens
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* With such exceeding glory was received!
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* When each one had returned unto that point
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* Within the circle where it was before,
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* It stood as in a candlestick a candle;
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* And from within the effulgence which at first
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* Had spoken unto me, I heard begin
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* Smiling while it more luminous became:
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*“Even as I am kindled in its ray,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*So, looking into the Eternal Light,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*The occasion of thy thoughts I apprehend.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Thou doubtest, and wouldst have me to resift\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*In language so extended and so open\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*My speech, that to thy sense it may be plain,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Where just before I said, ‘where well one fattens,’\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*And where I said, ‘there never rose a second;’\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*And here ’tis needful we distinguish well.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*The Providence, which governeth the world\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*With counsel, wherein all created vision\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Is vanquished ere it reach unto the bottom,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*(So that towards her own Beloved might go\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*The bride of Him who, uttering a loud cry,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Espoused her with his consecrated blood,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Self-confident and unto Him more faithful,)\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Two Princes did ordain in her behoof,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Which on this side and that might be her guide.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*The one was all seraphical in ardour;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*The other by his wisdom upon earth\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*A splendour was of light cherubical.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*One will I speak of, for of both is spoken\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*In praising one, whichever may be taken,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Because unto one end their labours were.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Between Tupino and the stream that falls\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Down from the hill elect of blessed Ubald,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*A fertile slope of lofty mountain hangs,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*From which Perugia feels the cold and heat\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Through Porta Sole, and behind it weep\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Gualdo and Nocera their grievous yoke.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*From out that slope, there where it breaketh most\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Its steepness, rose upon the world a sun\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*As this one does sometimes from out the Ganges;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Therefore let him who speaketh of that place,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Say not Ascesi, for he would say little,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*But Orient, if he properly would speak.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*He was not yet far distant from his rising\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Before he had begun to make the earth\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Some comfort from his mighty virtue feel.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*For he in youth his father’s wrath incurred\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*For certain Dame, to whom, as unto death,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*The gate of pleasure no one doth unlock;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*And was before his spiritual court\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*‘Et coram patre’ unto her united;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Then day by day more fervently he loved her.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*She, reft of her first husband, scorned, obscure,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*One thousand and one hundred years and more,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Waited without a suitor till he came.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Naught it availed to hear, that with Amyclas\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Found her unmoved at sounding of his voice\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*He who struck terror into all the world;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Naught it availed being constant and undaunted,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*So that, when Mary still remained below,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*She mounted up with Christ upon the cross.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*But that too darkly I may not proceed,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Francis and Poverty for these two lovers\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Take thou henceforward in my speech diffuse.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Their concord and their joyous semblances,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*The love, the wonder, and the sweet regard,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*They made to be the cause of holy thoughts;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*So much so that the venerable Bernard\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*First bared his feet, and after so great peace\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Ran, and, in running, thought himself too slow.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*O wealth unknown! O veritable good!\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Giles bares his feet, and bares his feet Sylvester\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Behind the bridegroom, so doth please the bride!\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Then goes his way that father and that master,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*He and his Lady and that family\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Which now was girding on the humble cord;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Nor cowardice of heart weighed down his brow\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*At being son of Peter Bernardone,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Nor for appearing marvellously scorned;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*But regally his hard determination\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*To Innocent he opened, and from him\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Received the primal seal upon his Order.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*After the people mendicant increased\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Behind this man, whose admirable life\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Better in glory of the heavens were sung,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Incoronated with a second crown\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Was through Honorius by the Eternal Spirit\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*The holy purpose of this Archimandrite.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*And when he had, through thirst of martyrdom,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*In the proud presence of the Sultan preached\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Christ and the others who came after him,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*And, finding for conversion too unripe\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*The folk, and not to tarry there in vain,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Returned to fruit of the Italic grass,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*On the rude rock ’twixt Tiber and the Arno\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*From Christ did he receive the final seal,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Which during two whole years his members bore.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*When He, who chose him unto so much good,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Was pleased to draw him up to the reward\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*That he had merited by being lowly,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Unto his friars, as to the rightful heirs,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*His most dear Lady did he recommend,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*And bade that they should love her faithfully;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*And from her bosom the illustrious soul\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Wished to depart, returning to its realm,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*And for its body wished no other bier.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Think now what man was he, who was a fit\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Companion over the high seas to keep\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*The bark of Peter to its proper bearings.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*And this man was our Patriarch; hence whoever\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Doth follow him as he commands can see\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*That he is laden with good merchandise.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*But for new pasturage his flock has grown\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 125 \vp 125\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*So greedy, that it is impossible\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 126 \vp 126\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*They be not scattered over fields diverse;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 127 \vp 127\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*And in proportion as his sheep remote\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 128 \vp 128\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*And vagabond go farther off from him,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 129 \vp 129\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*More void of milk return they to the fold.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 130 \vp 130\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Verily some there are that fear a hurt,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 131 \vp 131\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*And keep close to the shepherd; but so few,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 132 \vp 132\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*That little cloth doth furnish forth their hoods.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 133 \vp 133\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Now if my utterance be not indistinct,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 134 \vp 134\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*If thine own hearing hath attentive been,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 135 \vp 135\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*If thou recall to mind what I have said,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 136 \vp 136\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*In part contented shall thy wishes be;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 137 \vp 137\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*For thou shalt see the plant that’s chipped away,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 138 \vp 138\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*And the rebuke that lieth in the words,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 139 \vp 139\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*‘Where well one fattens, if he strayeth not.’”\zsay-e\*
\c 12
\cp 12
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* Soon as the blessed flame had taken up
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* The final word to give it utterance,
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* Began the holy millstone to revolve,
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* And in its gyre had not turned wholly round,
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* Before another in a ring enclosed it,
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* And motion joined to motion, song to song;
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* Song that as greatly doth transcend our Muses,
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* Our Sirens, in those dulcet clarions,
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* As primal splendour that which is reflected.
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* And as are spanned athwart a tender cloud
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* Two rainbows parallel and like in colour,
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* When Juno to her handmaid gives command,
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* (The one without born of the one within,
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* Like to the speaking of that vagrant one
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* Whom love consumed as doth the sun the vapours,)
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* And make the people here, through covenant
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* God set with Noah, presageful of the world
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* That shall no more be covered with a flood,
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* In such wise of those sempiternal roses
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* The garlands twain encompassed us about,
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* And thus the outer to the inner answered.
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* After the dance, and other grand rejoicings,
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* Both of the singing, and the flaming forth
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* Effulgence with effulgence blithe and tender,
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* Together, at once, with one accord had stopped,
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* (Even as the eyes, that, as volition moves them,
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* Must needs together shut and lift themselves,)
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* Out of the heart of one of the new lights
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* There came a voice, that needle to the star
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* Made me appear in turning thitherward.
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* And it began: \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*“The love that makes me fair\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*Draws me to speak about the other leader,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*By whom so well is spoken here of mine.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*’Tis right, where one is, to bring in the other,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*That, as they were united in their warfare,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*Together likewise may their glory shine.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*The soldiery of Christ, which it had cost\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*So dear to arm again, behind the standard\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*Moved slow and doubtful and in numbers few,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*When the Emperor who reigneth evermore\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*Provided for the host that was in peril,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*Through grace alone and not that it was worthy;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*And, as was said, he to his Bride brought succour\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*With champions twain, at whose deed, at whose word\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*The straggling people were together drawn.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*Within that region where the sweet west wind\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*Rises to open the new leaves, wherewith\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*Europe is seen to clothe herself afresh,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*Not far off from the beating of the waves,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*Behind which in his long career the sun\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*Sometimes conceals himself from every man,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*Is situate the fortunate Calahorra,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*Under protection of the mighty shield\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*In which the Lion subject is and sovereign.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*Therein was born the amorous paramour\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*Of Christian Faith, the athlete consecrate,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*Kind to his own and cruel to his foes;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*And when it was created was his mind\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*Replete with such a living energy,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*That in his mother her it made prophetic.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*As soon as the espousals were complete\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*Between him and the Faith at holy font,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*Where they with mutual safety dowered each other,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*The woman, who for him had given assent,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*Saw in a dream the admirable fruit\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*That issue would from him and from his heirs;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*And that he might be construed as he was,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*A spirit from this place went forth to name him\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*With His possessive whose he wholly was.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*Dominic was he called; and him I speak of\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*Even as of the husbandman whom Christ\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*Elected to his garden to assist him.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*Envoy and servant sooth he seemed of Christ,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*For the first love made manifest in him\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*Was the first counsel that was given by Christ.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*Silent and wakeful many a time was he\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*Discovered by his nurse upon the ground,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*As if he would have said, ‘For this I came.’\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*O thou his father, Felix verily!\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*O thou his mother, verily Joanna,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*If this, interpreted, means as is said!\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*Not for the world which people toil for now\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*In following Ostiense and Taddeo,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*But through his longing after the true manna,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*He in short time became so great a teacher,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*That he began to go about the vineyard,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*Which fadeth soon, if faithless be the dresser;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*And of the See, (that once was more benignant\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*Unto the righteous poor, not through itself,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*But him who sits there and degenerates,)\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*Not to dispense or two or three for six,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*Not any fortune of first vacancy,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*‘Non decimas quae sunt pauperum Dei,’\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*He asked for, but against the errant world\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*Permission to do battle for the seed,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*Of which these four and twenty plants surround thee.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*Then with the doctrine and the will together,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*With office apostolical he moved,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*Like torrent which some lofty vein out-presses;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*And in among the shoots heretical\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*His impetus with greater fury smote,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*Wherever the resistance was the greatest.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*Of him were made thereafter divers runnels,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*Whereby the garden catholic is watered,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*So that more living its plantations stand.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*If such the one wheel of the Biga was,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*In which the Holy Church itself defended\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*And in the field its civic battle won,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*Truly full manifest should be to thee\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*The excellence of the other, unto whom\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*Thomas so courteous was before my coming.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*But still the orbit, which the highest part\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*Of its circumference made, is derelict,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*So that the mould is where was once the crust.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*His family, that had straight forward moved\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*With feet upon his footprints, are turned round\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*So that they set the point upon the heel.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*And soon aware they will be of the harvest\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*Of this bad husbandry, when shall the tares\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*Complain the granary is taken from them.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*Yet say I, he who searcheth leaf by leaf\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*Our volume through, would still some page discover\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*Where he could read, ‘I am as I am wont.’\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*’Twill not be from Casal nor Acquasparta,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 125 \vp 125\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*From whence come such unto the written word\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 126 \vp 126\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*That one avoids it, and the other narrows.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 127 \vp 127\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*Bonaventura of Bagnoregio’s life\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 128 \vp 128\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*Am I, who always in great offices\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 129 \vp 129\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*Postponed considerations sinister.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 130 \vp 130\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*Here are Illuminato and Agostino,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 131 \vp 131\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*Who of the first barefooted beggars were\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 132 \vp 132\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*That with the cord the friends of God became.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 133 \vp 133\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*Hugh of Saint Victor is among them here,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 134 \vp 134\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*And Peter Mangiador, and Peter of Spain,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 135 \vp 135\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*Who down below in volumes twelve is shining;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 136 \vp 136\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*Nathan the seer, and metropolitan\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 137 \vp 137\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*Chrysostom, and Anselmus, and Donatus\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 138 \vp 138\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*Who deigned to lay his hand to the first art;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 139 \vp 139\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*Here is Rabanus, and beside me here\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 140 \vp 140\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*Shines the Calabrian Abbot Joachim,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 141 \vp 141\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*He with the spirit of prophecy endowed.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 142 \vp 142\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*To celebrate so great a paladin\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 143 \vp 143\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*Have moved me the impassioned courtesy\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 144 \vp 144\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*And the discreet discourses of Friar Thomas,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 145 \vp 145\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonaventure"\*And with me they have moved this company.”\zsay-e\*
\c 13
\cp 13
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* Let him imagine, who would well conceive
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* What now I saw, and let him while I speak
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* Retain the image as a steadfast rock,
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* The fifteen stars, that in their divers regions
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* The sky enliven with a light so great
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* That it transcends all clusters of the air;
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* Let him the Wain imagine unto which
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* Our vault of heaven sufficeth night and day,
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* So that in turning of its pole it fails not;
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* Let him the mouth imagine of the horn
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* That in the point beginneth of the axis
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* Round about which the primal wheel revolves,—
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* To have fashioned of themselves two signs in heaven,
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* Like unto that which Minos’ daughter made,
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* The moment when she felt the frost of death;
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* And one to have its rays within the other,
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* And both to whirl themselves in such a manner
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* That one should forward go, the other backward;
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* And he will have some shadowing forth of that
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* True constellation and the double dance
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* That circled round the point at which I was;
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* Because it is as much beyond our wont,
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* As swifter than the motion of the Chiana
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* Moveth the heaven that all the rest outspeeds.
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* There sang they neither Bacchus, nor Apollo,
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* But in the divine nature Persons three,
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* And in one person the divine and human.
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* The singing and the dance fulfilled their measure,
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* And unto us those holy lights gave need,
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* Growing in happiness from care to care.
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* Then broke the silence of those saints concordant
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* The light in which the admirable life
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* Of God’s own mendicant was told to me,
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* And said: \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*“Now that one straw is trodden out\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Now that its seed is garnered up already,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Sweet love invites me to thresh out the other.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Into that bosom, thou believest, whence\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Was drawn the rib to form the beauteous cheek\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Whose taste to all the world is costing dear,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*And into that which, by the lance transfixed,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Before and since, such satisfaction made\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*That it weighs down the balance of all sin,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Whate’er of light it has to human nature\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Been lawful to possess was all infused\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*By the same power that both of them created;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*And hence at what I said above dost wonder,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*When I narrated that no second had\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*The good which in the fifth light is enclosed.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Now ope thine eyes to what I answer thee,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*And thou shalt see thy creed and my discourse\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Fit in the truth as centre in a circle.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*That which can die, and that which dieth not,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Are nothing but the splendour of the idea\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Which by his love our Lord brings into being;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Because that living Light, which from its fount\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Effulgent flows, so that it disunites not\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*From Him nor from the Love in them intrined,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Through its own goodness reunites its rays\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*In nine subsistences, as in a mirror,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Itself eternally remaining One.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Thence it descends to the last potencies,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Downward from act to act becoming such\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*That only brief contingencies it makes;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*And these contingencies I hold to be\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Things generated, which the heaven produces\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*By its own motion, with seed and without.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Neither their wax, nor that which tempers it,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Remains immutable, and hence beneath\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*The ideal signet more and less shines through;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Therefore it happens, that the selfsame tree\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*After its kind bears worse and better fruit,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*And ye are born with characters diverse.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*If in perfection tempered were the wax,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*And were the heaven in its supremest virtue,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*The brilliance of the seal would all appear;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*But nature gives it evermore deficient,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*In the like manner working as the artist,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Who has the skill of art and hand that trembles.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*If then the fervent Love, the Vision clear,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Of primal Virtue do dispose and seal,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Perfection absolute is there acquired.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Thus was of old the earth created worthy\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Of all and every animal perfection;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*And thus the Virgin was impregnate made;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*So that thine own opinion I commend,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*That human nature never yet has been,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Nor will be, what it was in those two persons.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Now if no farther forth I should proceed,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*‘Then in what way was he without a peer?’\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Would be the first beginning of thy words.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*But, that may well appear what now appears not,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Think who he was, and what occasion moved him\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*To make request, when it was told him, ‘Ask.’\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*I’ve not so spoken that thou canst not see\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Clearly he was a king who asked for wisdom,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*That he might be sufficiently a king;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*’Twas not to know the number in which are\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*The motors here above, or if ‘necesse’\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*With a contingent e’er ‘necesse’ make,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*‘Non si est dare primum motum esse,’\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Or if in semicircle can be made\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Triangle so that it have no right angle.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Whence, if thou notest this and what I said,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*A regal prudence is that peerless seeing\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*In which the shaft of my intention strikes.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*And if on ‘rose’ thou turnest thy clear eyes,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Thou’lt see that it has reference alone\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*To kings who’re many, and the good are rare.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*With this distinction take thou what I said,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*And thus it can consist with thy belief\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Of the first father and of our Delight.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*And lead shall this be always to thy feet,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*To make thee, like a weary man, move slowly\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Both to the Yes and No thou seest not;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*For very low among the fools is he\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Who affirms without distinction, or denies,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*As well in one as in the other case;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Because it happens that full often bends\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Current opinion in the false direction,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*And then the feelings bind the intellect.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Far more than uselessly he leaves the shore,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*(Since he returneth not the same he went,)\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Who fishes for the truth, and has no skill;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*And in the world proofs manifest thereof\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 125 \vp 125\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Parmenides, Melissus, Brissus are,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 126 \vp 126\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*And many who went on and knew not whither;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 127 \vp 127\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Thus did Sabellius, Arius, and those fools\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 128 \vp 128\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Who have been even as swords unto the Scriptures\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 129 \vp 129\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*In rendering distorted their straight faces.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 130 \vp 130\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Nor yet shall people be too confident\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 131 \vp 131\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*In judging, even as he is who doth count\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 132 \vp 132\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*The corn in field or ever it be ripe.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 133 \vp 133\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*For I have seen all winter long the thorn\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 134 \vp 134\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*First show itself intractable and fierce,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 135 \vp 135\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*And after bear the rose upon its top;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 136 \vp 136\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*And I have seen a ship direct and swift\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 137 \vp 137\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Run o’er the sea throughout its course entire,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 138 \vp 138\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*To perish at the harbour’s mouth at last.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 139 \vp 139\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Let not Dame Bertha nor Ser Martin think,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 140 \vp 140\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*Seeing one steal, another offering make,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 141 \vp 141\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*To see them in the arbitrament divine;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 142 \vp 142\vp* \zsay-s |who="Thomas Aquinas"\*For one may rise, and fall the other may.”\zsay-e\*
\c 14
\cp 14
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* From centre unto rim, from rim to centre,
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* In a round vase the water moves itself,
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* As from without ’tis struck or from within.
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* Into my mind upon a sudden dropped
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* What I am saying, at the moment when
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* Silent became the glorious life of Thomas,
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* Because of the resemblance that was born
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* Of his discourse and that of Beatrice,
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* Whom, after him, it pleased thus to begin:
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*“This man has need (and does not tell you so,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Nor with the voice, nor even in his thought)\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Of going to the root of one truth more.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Declare unto him if the light wherewith\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Blossoms your substance shall remain with you\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Eternally the same that it is now;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*And if it do remain, say in what manner,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*After ye are again made visible,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*It can be that it injure not your sight.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* As by a greater gladness urged and drawn
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* They who are dancing in a ring sometimes
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* Uplift their voices and their motions quicken;
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* So, at that orison devout and prompt,
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* The holy circles a new joy displayed
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* In their revolving and their wondrous song.
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* Whoso lamenteth him that here we die
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* That we may live above, has never there
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* Seen the refreshment of the eternal rain.
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* The One and Two and Three who ever liveth,
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* And reigneth ever in Three and Two and One,
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* Not circumscribed and all things circumscribing,
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* Three several times was chanted by each one
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* Among those spirits, with such melody
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* That for all merit it were just reward;
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* And, in the lustre most divine of all
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* The lesser ring, I heard a modest voice,
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* Such as perhaps the Angel’s was to Mary,
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* Answer: \zsay-s |who="Solomon"\*“As long as the festivity\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* \zsay-s |who="Solomon"\*Of Paradise shall be, so long our love\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* \zsay-s |who="Solomon"\*Shall radiate round about us such a vesture.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* \zsay-s |who="Solomon"\*Its brightness is proportioned to the ardour,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* \zsay-s |who="Solomon"\*The ardour to the vision; and the vision\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* \zsay-s |who="Solomon"\*Equals what grace it has above its worth.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* \zsay-s |who="Solomon"\*When, glorious and sanctified, our flesh\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* \zsay-s |who="Solomon"\*Is reassumed, then shall our persons be\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* \zsay-s |who="Solomon"\*More pleasing by their being all complete;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* \zsay-s |who="Solomon"\*For will increase whate’er bestows on us\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* \zsay-s |who="Solomon"\*Of light gratuitous the Good Supreme,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* \zsay-s |who="Solomon"\*Light which enables us to look on Him;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* \zsay-s |who="Solomon"\*Therefore the vision must perforce increase,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* \zsay-s |who="Solomon"\*Increase the ardour which from that is kindled,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* \zsay-s |who="Solomon"\*Increase the radiance which from this proceeds.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* \zsay-s |who="Solomon"\*But even as a coal that sends forth flame,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* \zsay-s |who="Solomon"\*And by its vivid whiteness overpowers it\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* \zsay-s |who="Solomon"\*So that its own appearance it maintains,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* \zsay-s |who="Solomon"\*Thus the effulgence that surrounds us now\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* \zsay-s |who="Solomon"\*Shall be o’erpowered in aspect by the flesh,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* \zsay-s |who="Solomon"\*Which still to-day the earth doth cover up;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* \zsay-s |who="Solomon"\*Nor can so great a splendour weary us,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* \zsay-s |who="Solomon"\*For strong will be the organs of the body\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* \zsay-s |who="Solomon"\*To everything which hath the power to please us.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* So sudden and alert appeared to me
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* Both one and the other choir to say Amen,
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* That well they showed desire for their dead bodies;
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* Nor sole for them perhaps, but for the mothers,
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* The fathers, and the rest who had been dear
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* Or ever they became eternal flames.
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* And lo! all round about of equal brightness
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* Arose a lustre over what was there,
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* Like an horizon that is clearing up.
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* And as at rise of early eve begin
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* Along the welkin new appearances,
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* So that the sight seems real and unreal,
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* It seemed to me that new subsistences
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* Began there to be seen, and make a circle
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* Outside the other two circumferences.
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* O very sparkling of the Holy Spirit,
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* How sudden and incandescent it became
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* Unto mine eyes, that vanquished bore it not!
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* But Beatrice so beautiful and smiling
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* Appeared to me, that with the other sights
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* That followed not my memory I must leave her.
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* Then to uplift themselves mine eyes resumed
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* The power, and I beheld myself translated
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* To higher salvation with my Lady only.
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* Well was I ware that I was more uplifted
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* By the enkindled smiling of the star,
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* That seemed to me more ruddy than its wont.
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* With all my heart, and in that dialect
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* Which is the same in all, such holocaust
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* To God I made as the new grace beseemed;
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* And not yet from my bosom was exhausted
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* The ardour of sacrifice, before I knew
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* This offering was accepted and auspicious;
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* For with so great a lustre and so red
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* Splendours appeared to me in twofold rays,
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* I said: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“O Helios who dost so adorn them!”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* Even as distinct with less and greater lights
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* Glimmers between the two poles of the world
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* The Galaxy that maketh wise men doubt,
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* Thus constellated in the depths of Mars,
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* Those rays described the venerable sign
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* That quadrants joining in a circle make.
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* Here doth my memory overcome my genius;
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* For on that cross as levin gleamed forth Christ,
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* So that I cannot find ensample worthy;
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* But he who takes his cross and follows Christ
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* Again will pardon me what I omit,
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* Seeing in that aurora lighten Christ.
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* From horn to horn, and ’twixt the top and base,
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* Lights were in motion, brightly scintillating
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* As they together met and passed each other;
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* Thus level and aslant and swift and slow
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* We here behold, renewing still the sight,
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* The particles of bodies long and short,
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* Across the sunbeam move, wherewith is listed
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* Sometimes the shade, which for their own defence
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* People with cunning and with art contrive.
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* And as a lute and harp, accordant strung
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* With many strings, a dulcet tinkling make
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* To him by whom the notes are not distinguished,
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* So from the lights that there to me appeared
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* Upgathered through the cross a melody,
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* Which rapt me, not distinguishing the hymn.
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* Well was I ware it was of lofty laud,
\q1 \v 125 \vp 125\vp* Because there came to me, \zsay-s |who="The spirits"\*“Arise and conquer!”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 126 \vp 126\vp* As unto him who hears and comprehends not.
\b
\q1 \v 127 \vp 127\vp* So much enamoured I became therewith,
\q1 \v 128 \vp 128\vp* That until then there was not anything
\q1 \v 129 \vp 129\vp* That e’er had fettered me with such sweet bonds.
\b
\q1 \v 130 \vp 130\vp* Perhaps my word appears somewhat too bold,
\q1 \v 131 \vp 131\vp* Postponing the delight of those fair eyes,
\q1 \v 132 \vp 132\vp* Into which gazing my desire has rest;
\b
\q1 \v 133 \vp 133\vp* But who bethinks him that the living seals
\q1 \v 134 \vp 134\vp* Of every beauty grow in power ascending,
\q1 \v 135 \vp 135\vp* And that I there had not turned round to those,
\b
\q1 \v 136 \vp 136\vp* Can me excuse, if I myself accuse
\q1 \v 137 \vp 137\vp* To excuse myself, and see that I speak truly:
\q1 \v 138 \vp 138\vp* For here the holy joy is not disclosed,
\b
\q1 \v 139 \vp 139\vp* Because ascending it becomes more pure.
\c 15
\cp 15
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* A will benign, in which reveals itself
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* Ever the love that righteously inspires,
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* As in the iniquitous, cupidity,
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* Silence imposed upon that dulcet lyre,
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* And quieted the consecrated chords,
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* That Heaven’s right hand doth tighten and relax.
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* How unto just entreaties shall be deaf
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* Those substances, which, to give me desire
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* Of praying them, with one accord grew silent?
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* ’Tis well that without end he should lament,
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* Who for the love of thing that doth not last
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* Eternally despoils him of that love!
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* As through the pure and tranquil evening air
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* There shoots from time to time a sudden fire,
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* Moving the eyes that steadfast were before,
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* And seems to be a star that changeth place,
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* Except that in the part where it is kindled
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* Nothing is missed, and this endureth little;
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* So from the horn that to the right extends
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* Unto that cross’s foot there ran a star
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* Out of the constellation shining there;
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* Nor was the gem dissevered from its ribbon,
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* But down the radiant fillet ran along,
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* So that fire seemed it behind alabaster.
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* Thus piteous did Anchises’ shade reach forward,
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* If any faith our greatest Muse deserve,
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* When in Elysium he his son perceived.
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*“O sanguis meus, O superinfusa\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Gratia Dei, sicut tibi, cui\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Bis unquam Coeli janua reclusa?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* Thus that effulgence; whence I gave it heed;
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* Then round unto my Lady turned my sight,
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* And on this side and that was stupefied;
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* For in her eyes was burning such a smile
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* That with mine own methought I touched the bottom
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* Both of my grace and of my Paradise!
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* Then, pleasant to the hearing and the sight,
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* The spirit joined to its beginning things
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* I understood not, so profound it spake;
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* Nor did it hide itself from me by choice,
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* But by necessity; for its conception
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* Above the mark of mortals set itself.
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* And when the bow of burning sympathy
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* Was so far slackened, that its speech descended
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* Towards the mark of our intelligence,
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* The first thing that was understood by me
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* Was \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*“Benedight be Thou, O Trine and One,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Who hast unto my seed so courteous been!”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* And it continued: \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*“Hunger long and grateful,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Drawn from the reading of the mighty volume\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Wherein is never changed the white nor dark,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Thou hast appeased, my son, within this light\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*In which I speak to thee, by grace of her\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Who to this lofty flight with plumage clothed thee.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Thou thinkest that to me thy thought doth pass\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*From Him who is the first, as from the unit,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*If that be known, ray out the five and six;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*And therefore who I am thou askest not,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*And why I seem more joyous unto thee\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Than any other of this gladsome crowd.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Thou think’st the truth; because the small and great\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Of this existence look into the mirror\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Wherein, before thou think’st, thy thought thou showest.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*But that the sacred love, in which I watch\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*With sight perpetual, and which makes me thirst\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*With sweet desire, may better be fulfilled,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Now let thy voice secure and frank and glad\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Proclaim the wishes, the desire proclaim,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*To which my answer is decreed already.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* To Beatrice I turned me, and she heard
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* Before I spake, and smiled to me a sign,
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* That made the wings of my desire increase;
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* Then in this wise began I: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“Love and knowledge,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*When on you dawned the first Equality,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Of the same weight for each of you became;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*For in the Sun, which lighted you and burned\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*With heat and radiance, they so equal are,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*That all similitudes are insufficient.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*But among mortals will and argument,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*For reason that to you is manifest,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Diversely feathered in their pinions are.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Whence I, who mortal am, feel in myself\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*This inequality; so give not thanks,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Save in my heart, for this paternal welcome.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Truly do I entreat thee, living topaz!\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Set in this precious jewel as a gem,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*That thou wilt satisfy me with thy name.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*“O leaf of mine, in whom I pleasure took\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*E’en while awaiting, I was thine own root!”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* Such a beginning he in answer made me.
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* Then said to me: \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*“That one from whom is named\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Thy race, and who a hundred years and more\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Has circled round the mount on the first cornice,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*A son of mine and thy great-grandsire was;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Well it behoves thee that the long fatigue\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Thou shouldst for him make shorter with thy works.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Florence, within the ancient boundary\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*From which she taketh still her tierce and nones,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Abode in quiet, temperate and chaste.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*No golden chain she had, nor coronal,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Nor ladies shod with sandal shoon, nor girdle\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*That caught the eye more than the person did.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Not yet the daughter at her birth struck fear\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Into the father, for the time and dower\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Did not o’errun this side or that the measure.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*No houses had she void of families,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Not yet had thither come Sardanapalus\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*To show what in a chamber can be done;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Not yet surpassed had Montemalo been\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*By your Uccellatojo, which surpassed\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Shall in its downfall be as in its rise.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Bellincion Berti saw I go begirt\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*With leather and with bone, and from the mirror\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*His dame depart without a painted face;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*And him of Nerli saw, and him of Vecchio,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Contented with their simple suits of buff\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*And with the spindle and the flax their dames.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*O fortunate women! and each one was certain\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Of her own burial-place, and none as yet\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*For sake of France was in her bed deserted.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*One o’er the cradle kept her studious watch,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*And in her lullaby the language used\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*That first delights the fathers and the mothers;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Another, drawing tresses from her distaff,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 125 \vp 125\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Told o’er among her family the tales\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 126 \vp 126\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Of Trojans and of Fesole and Rome.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 127 \vp 127\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*As great a marvel then would have been held\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 128 \vp 128\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*A Lapo Salterello, a Cianghella,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 129 \vp 129\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*As Cincinnatus or Cornelia now.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 130 \vp 130\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*To such a quiet, such a beautiful\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 131 \vp 131\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Life of the citizen, to such a safe\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 132 \vp 132\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Community, and to so sweet an inn,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 133 \vp 133\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Did Mary give me, with loud cries invoked,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 134 \vp 134\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*And in your ancient Baptistery at once\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 135 \vp 135\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Christian and Cacciaguida I became.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 136 \vp 136\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Moronto was my brother, and Eliseo;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 137 \vp 137\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*From Val di Pado came to me my wife,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 138 \vp 138\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*And from that place thy surname was derived.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 139 \vp 139\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*I followed afterward the Emperor Conrad,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 140 \vp 140\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*And he begirt me of his chivalry,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 141 \vp 141\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*So much I pleased him with my noble deeds.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 142 \vp 142\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*I followed in his train against that law’s\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 143 \vp 143\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Iniquity, whose people doth usurp\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 144 \vp 144\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Your just possession, through your Pastor’s fault.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 145 \vp 145\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*There by that execrable race was I\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 146 \vp 146\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Released from bonds of the fallacious world,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 147 \vp 147\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*The love of which defileth many souls,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 148 \vp 148\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*And came from martyrdom unto this peace.”\zsay-e\*
\c 16
\cp 16
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* O thou our poor nobility of blood,
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* If thou dost make the people glory in thee
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* Down here where our affection languishes,
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* A marvellous thing it ne’er will be to me;
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* For there where appetite is not perverted,
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* I say in Heaven, of thee I made a boast!
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* Truly thou art a cloak that quickly shortens,
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* So that unless we piece thee day by day
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* Time goeth round about thee with his shears!
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* With ‘You,’ which Rome was first to tolerate,
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* (Wherein her family less perseveres,)
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* Yet once again my words beginning made;
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* Whence Beatrice, who stood somewhat apart,
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* Smiling, appeared like unto her who coughed
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* At the first failing writ of Guenever.
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* And I began: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“You are my ancestor,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*You give to me all hardihood to speak,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*You lift me so that I am more than I.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*So many rivulets with gladness fill\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*My mind, that of itself it makes a joy\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Because it can endure this and not burst.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Then tell me, my beloved root ancestral,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Who were your ancestors, and what the years\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*That in your boyhood chronicled themselves?\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Tell me about the sheepfold of Saint John,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*How large it was, and who the people were\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Within it worthy of the highest seats.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* As at the blowing of the winds a coal
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* Quickens to flame, so I beheld that light
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* Become resplendent at my blandishments.
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* And as unto mine eyes it grew more fair,
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* With voice more sweet and tender, but not in
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* This modern dialect, it said to me:
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*“From uttering of the ‘Ave,’ till the birth\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*In which my mother, who is now a saint,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Of me was lightened who had been her burden,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Unto its Lion had this fire returned\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Five hundred fifty times and thirty more,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*To reinflame itself beneath his paw.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*My ancestors and I our birthplace had\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Where first is found the last ward of the city\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*By him who runneth in your annual game.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Suffice it of my elders to hear this;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*But who they were, and whence they thither came,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Silence is more considerate than speech.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*All those who at that time were there between\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Mars and the Baptist, fit for bearing arms,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Were a fifth part of those who now are living;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*But the community, that now is mixed\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*With Campi and Certaldo and Figghine,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Pure in the lowest artisan was seen.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*O how much better ’twere to have as neighbours\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*The folk of whom I speak, and at Galluzzo\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*And at Trespiano have your boundary,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Than have them in the town, and bear the stench\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Of Aguglione’s churl, and him of Signa\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Who has sharp eyes for trickery already.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Had not the folk, which most of all the world\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Degenerates, been a step-dame unto Caesar,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*But as a mother to her son benignant,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Some who turn Florentines, and trade and discount,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Would have gone back again to Simifonte\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*There where their grandsires went about as beggars.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*At Montemurlo still would be the Counts,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*The Cerchi in the parish of Acone,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Perhaps in Valdigrieve the Buondelmonti.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Ever the intermingling of the people\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Has been the source of malady in cities,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*As in the body food it surfeits on;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*And a blind bull more headlong plunges down\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Than a blind lamb; and very often cuts\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Better and more a single sword than five.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*If Luni thou regard, and Urbisaglia,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*How they have passed away, and how are passing\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Chiusi and Sinigaglia after them,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*To hear how races waste themselves away,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Will seem to thee no novel thing nor hard,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Seeing that even cities have an end.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*All things of yours have their mortality,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Even as yourselves; but it is hidden in some\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*That a long while endure, and lives are short;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*And as the turning of the lunar heaven\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Covers and bares the shores without a pause,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*In the like manner fortune does with Florence.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Therefore should not appear a marvellous thing\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*What I shall say of the great Florentines\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Of whom the fame is hidden in the Past.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*I saw the Ughi, saw the Catellini,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Filippi, Greci, Ormanni, and Alberichi,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Even in their fall illustrious citizens;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*And saw, as mighty as they ancient were,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*With him of La Sannella him of Arca,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*And Soldanier, Ardinghi, and Bostichi.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Near to the gate that is at present laden\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*With a new felony of so much weight\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*That soon it shall be jetsam from the bark,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*The Ravignani were, from whom descended\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*The County Guido, and whoe’er the name\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Of the great Bellincione since hath taken.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*He of La Pressa knew the art of ruling\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Already, and already Galigajo\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Had hilt and pommel gilded in his house.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Mighty already was the Column Vair,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Sacchetti, Giuochi, Fifant, and Barucci,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*And Galli, and they who for the bushel blush.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*The stock from which were the Calfucci born\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Was great already, and already chosen\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*To curule chairs the Sizii and Arrigucci.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*O how beheld I those who are undone\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*By their own pride! and how the Balls of Gold\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Florence enflowered in all their mighty deeds!\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*So likewise did the ancestors of those\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Who evermore, when vacant is your church,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Fatten by staying in consistory.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*The insolent race, that like a dragon follows\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Whoever flees, and unto him that shows\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*His teeth or purse is gentle as a lamb,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Already rising was, but from low people;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*So that it pleased not Ubertin Donato\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*That his wife’s father should make him their kin.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Already had Caponsacco to the Market\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*From Fesole descended, and already\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Giuda and Infangato were good burghers.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*I’ll tell a thing incredible, but true;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 125 \vp 125\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*One entered the small circuit by a gate\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 126 \vp 126\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Which from the Della Pera took its name!\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 127 \vp 127\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Each one that bears the beautiful escutcheon\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 128 \vp 128\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Of the great baron whose renown and name\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 129 \vp 129\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*The festival of Thomas keepeth fresh,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 130 \vp 130\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Knighthood and privilege from him received;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 131 \vp 131\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Though with the populace unites himself\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 132 \vp 132\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*To-day the man who binds it with a border.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 133 \vp 133\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Already were Gualterotti and Importuni;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 134 \vp 134\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*And still more quiet would the Borgo be\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 135 \vp 135\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*If with new neighbours it remained unfed.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 136 \vp 136\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*The house from which is born your lamentation,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 137 \vp 137\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Through just disdain that death among you brought\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 138 \vp 138\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*And put an end unto your joyous life,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 139 \vp 139\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Was honoured in itself and its companions.\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 140 \vp 140\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*O Buondelmonte, how in evil hour\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 141 \vp 141\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Thou fled’st the bridal at another’s promptings!\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 142 \vp 142\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Many would be rejoicing who are sad,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 143 \vp 143\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*If God had thee surrendered to the Ema\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 144 \vp 144\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*The first time that thou camest to the city.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 145 \vp 145\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*But it behoved the mutilated stone\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 146 \vp 146\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Which guards the bridge, that Florence should provide\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 147 \vp 147\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*A victim in her latest hour of peace.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 148 \vp 148\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*With all these families, and others with them,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 149 \vp 149\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Florence beheld I in so great repose,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 150 \vp 150\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*That no occasion had she whence to weep;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 151 \vp 151\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*With all these families beheld so just\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 152 \vp 152\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*And glorious her people, that the lily\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 153 \vp 153\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Never upon the spear was placed reversed,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 154 \vp 154\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Nor by division was vermilion made.”\zsay-e\*
\c 17
\cp 17
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* As came to Clymene, to be made certain
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* Of that which he had heard against himself,
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* He who makes fathers chary still to children,
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* Even such was I, and such was I perceived
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* By Beatrice and by the holy light
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* That first on my account had changed its place.
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* Therefore my Lady said to me: \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*“Send forth\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*The flame of thy desire, so that it issue\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Imprinted well with the internal stamp;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Not that our knowledge may be greater made\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*By speech of thine, but to accustom thee\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*To tell thy thirst, that we may give thee drink.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“O my beloved tree, (that so dost lift thee,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*That even as minds terrestrial perceive\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*No triangle containeth two obtuse,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*So thou beholdest the contingent things\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Ere in themselves they are, fixing thine eyes\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Upon the point in which all times are present,)\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*While I was with Virgilius conjoined\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Upon the mountain that the souls doth heal,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*And when descending into the dead world,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Were spoken to me of my future life\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Some grievous words; although I feel myself\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*In sooth foursquare against the blows of chance.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*On this account my wish would be content\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*To hear what fortune is approaching me,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Because foreseen an arrow comes more slowly.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* Thus did I say unto that selfsame light
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* That unto me had spoken before; and even
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* As Beatrice willed was my own will confessed.
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* Not in vague phrase, in which the foolish folk
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* Ensnared themselves of old, ere yet was slain
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* The Lamb of God who taketh sins away,
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* But with clear words and unambiguous
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* Language responded that paternal love,
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* Hid and revealed by its own proper smile:
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*“Contingency, that outside of the volume\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Of your materiality extends not,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Is all depicted in the eternal aspect.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Necessity however thence it takes not,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Except as from the eye, in which ’tis mirrored,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*A ship that with the current down descends.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*From thence, e’en as there cometh to the ear\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Sweet harmony from an organ, comes in sight\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*To me the time that is preparing for thee.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*As forth from Athens went Hippolytus,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*By reason of his step-dame false and cruel,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*So thou from Florence must perforce depart.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Already this is willed, and this is sought for;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*And soon it shall be done by him who thinks it,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Where every day the Christ is bought and sold.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*The blame shall follow the offended party\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*In outcry as is usual; but the vengeance\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Shall witness to the truth that doth dispense it.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Thou shalt abandon everything beloved\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Most tenderly, and this the arrow is\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Which first the bow of banishment shoots forth.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Thou shalt have proof how savoureth of salt\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*The bread of others, and how hard a road\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*The going down and up another’s stairs.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*And that which most shall weigh upon thy shoulders\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Will be the bad and foolish company\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*With which into this valley thou shalt fall;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*For all ingrate, all mad and impious\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Will they become against thee; but soon after\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*They, and not thou, shall have the forehead scarlet.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Of their bestiality their own proceedings\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Shall furnish proof; so ’twill be well for thee\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*A party to have made thee by thyself.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Thine earliest refuge and thine earliest inn\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Shall be the mighty Lombard’s courtesy,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Who on the Ladder bears the holy bird,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Who such benign regard shall have for thee\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*That ’twixt you twain, in doing and in asking,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*That shall be first which is with others last.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*With him shalt thou see one who at his birth\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Has by this star of strength been so impressed,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*That notable shall his achievements be.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Not yet the people are aware of him\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Through his young age, since only nine years yet\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Around about him have these wheels revolved.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*But ere the Gascon cheat the noble Henry,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Some sparkles of his virtue shall appear\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*In caring not for silver nor for toil.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*So recognized shall his magnificence\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Become hereafter, that his enemies\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Will not have power to keep mute tongues about it.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*On him rely, and on his benefits;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*By him shall many people be transformed,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Changing condition rich and mendicant;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*And written in thy mind thou hence shalt bear\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Of him, but shalt not say it”\zsay-e\*—and things said he
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* Incredible to those who shall be present.
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* Then added: \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*“Son, these are the commentaries\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*On what was said to thee; behold the snares\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*That are concealed behind few revolutions;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Yet would I not thy neighbours thou shouldst envy,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Because thy life into the future reaches\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Beyond the punishment of their perfidies.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* When by its silence showed that sainted soul
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* That it had finished putting in the woof
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* Into that web which I had given it warped,
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* Began I, even as he who yearneth after,
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* Being in doubt, some counsel from a person
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* Who seeth, and uprightly wills, and loves:
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“Well see I, father mine, how spurreth on\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*The time towards me such a blow to deal me\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*As heaviest is to him who most gives way.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Therefore with foresight it is well I arm me,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*That, if the dearest place be taken from me,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*I may not lose the others by my songs.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Down through the world of infinite bitterness,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*And o’er the mountain, from whose beauteous summit\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*The eyes of my own Lady lifted me,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*And afterward through heaven from light to light,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*I have learned that which, if I tell again,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Will be a savour of strong herbs to many.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*And if I am a timid friend to truth,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*I fear lest I may lose my life with those\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Who will hereafter call this time the olden.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* The light in which was smiling my own treasure
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* Which there I had discovered, flashed at first
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* As in the sunshine doth a golden mirror;
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* Then made reply: \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*“A conscience overcast\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 125 \vp 125\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Or with its own or with another’s shame,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 126 \vp 126\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Will taste forsooth the tartness of thy word;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 127 \vp 127\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*But ne’ertheless, all falsehood laid aside,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 128 \vp 128\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Make manifest thy vision utterly,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 129 \vp 129\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*And let them scratch wherever is the itch;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 130 \vp 130\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*For if thine utterance shall offensive be\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 131 \vp 131\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*At the first taste, a vital nutriment\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 132 \vp 132\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*’Twill leave thereafter, when it is digested.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 133 \vp 133\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*This cry of thine shall do as doth the wind,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 134 \vp 134\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Which smiteth most the most exalted summits,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 135 \vp 135\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*And that is no slight argument of honour.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 136 \vp 136\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Therefore are shown to thee within these wheels,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 137 \vp 137\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Upon the mount and in the dolorous valley,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 138 \vp 138\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Only the souls that unto fame are known;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 139 \vp 139\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Because the spirit of the hearer rests not,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 140 \vp 140\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Nor doth confirm its faith by an example\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 141 \vp 141\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Which has the root of it unknown and hidden,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 142 \vp 142\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Or other reason that is not apparent.”\zsay-e\*
\c 18
\cp 18
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* Now was alone rejoicing in its word
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* That soul beatified, and I was tasting
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* My own, the bitter tempering with the sweet,
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* And the Lady who to God was leading me
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* Said: \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*“Change thy thought; consider that I am\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Near unto Him who every wrong disburdens.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* Unto the loving accents of my comfort
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* I turned me round, and then what love I saw
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* Within those holy eyes I here relinquish;
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* Not only that my language I distrust,
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* But that my mind cannot return so far
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* Above itself, unless another guide it.
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* Thus much upon that point can I repeat,
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* That, her again beholding, my affection
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* From every other longing was released.
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* While the eternal pleasure, which direct
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* Rayed upon Beatrice, from her fair face
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* Contented me with its reflected aspect,
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* Conquering me with the radiance of a smile,
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* She said to me, \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*“Turn thee about and listen;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Not in mine eyes alone is Paradise.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* Even as sometimes here do we behold
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* The affection in the look, if it be such
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* That all the soul is wrapt away by it,
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* So, by the flaming of the effulgence holy
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* To which I turned, I recognized therein
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* The wish of speaking to me somewhat farther.
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* And it began: \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*“In this fifth resting-place\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Upon the tree that liveth by its summit,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*And aye bears fruit, and never loses leaf,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Are blessed spirits that below, ere yet\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*They came to Heaven, were of such great renown\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*That every Muse therewith would affluent be.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*Therefore look thou upon the cross’s horns;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*He whom I now shall name will there enact\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cacciaguida"\*What doth within a cloud its own swift fire.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* I saw athwart the Cross a splendour drawn
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* By naming Joshua, (even as he did it,)
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* Nor noted I the word before the deed;
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* And at the name of the great Maccabee
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* I saw another move itself revolving,
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* And gladness was the whip unto that top.
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* Likewise for Charlemagne and for Orlando,
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* Two of them my regard attentive followed
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* As followeth the eye its falcon flying.
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* William thereafterward, and Renouard,
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* And the Duke Godfrey, did attract my sight
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* Along upon that Cross, and Robert Guiscard.
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* Then, moved and mingled with the other lights,
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* The soul that had addressed me showed how great
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* An artist ’twas among the heavenly singers.
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* To my right side I turned myself around,
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* My duty to behold in Beatrice
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* Either by words or gesture signified;
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* And so translucent I beheld her eyes,
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* So full of pleasure, that her countenance
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* Surpassed its other and its latest wont.
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* And as, by feeling greater delectation,
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* A man in doing good from day to day
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* Becomes aware his virtue is increasing,
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* So I became aware that my gyration
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* With heaven together had increased its arc,
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* That miracle beholding more adorned.
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* And such as is the change, in little lapse
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* Of time, in a pale woman, when her face
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* Is from the load of bashfulness unladen,
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* Such was it in mine eyes, when I had turned,
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* Caused by the whiteness of the temperate star,
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* The sixth, which to itself had gathered me.
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* Within that Jovial torch did I behold
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* The sparkling of the love which was therein
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* Delineate our language to mine eyes.
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* And even as birds uprisen from the shore,
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* As in congratulation o’er their food,
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* Make squadrons of themselves, now round, now long,
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* So from within those lights the holy creatures
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* Sang flying to and fro, and in their figures
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* Made of themselves now D, now I, now L.
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* First singing they to their own music moved;
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* Then one becoming of these characters,
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* A little while they rested and were silent.
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* O divine Pegasea, thou who genius
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* Dost glorious make, and render it long-lived,
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* And this through thee the cities and the kingdoms,
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* Illume me with thyself, that I may bring
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* Their figures out as I have them conceived!
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* Apparent be thy power in these brief verses!
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* Themselves then they displayed in five times seven
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* Vowels and consonants; and I observed
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* The parts as they seemed spoken unto me.
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* ‘Diligite justitiam,’ these were
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* First verb and noun of all that was depicted;
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* ‘Qui judicatis terram’ were the last.
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* Thereafter in the M of the fifth word
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* Remained they so arranged, that Jupiter
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* Seemed to be silver there with gold inlaid.
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* And other lights I saw descend where was
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* The summit of the M, and pause there singing
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* The good, I think, that draws them to itself.
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* Then, as in striking upon burning logs
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* Upward there fly innumerable sparks,
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* Whence fools are wont to look for auguries,
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* More than a thousand lights seemed thence to rise,
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* And to ascend, some more, and others less,
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* Even as the Sun that lights them had allotted;
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* And, each one being quiet in its place,
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* The head and neck beheld I of an eagle
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* Delineated by that inlaid fire.
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* He who there paints has none to be his guide;
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* But Himself guides; and is from Him remembered
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* That virtue which is form unto the nest.
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* The other beatitude, that contented seemed
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* At first to bloom a lily on the M,
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* By a slight motion followed out the imprint.
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* O gentle star! what and how many gems
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* Did demonstrate to me, that all our justice
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* Effect is of that heaven which thou ingemmest!
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* Wherefore I pray the Mind, in which begin
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* Thy motion and thy virtue, to regard
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* Whence comes the smoke that vitiates thy rays;
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* So that a second time it now be wroth
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* With buying and with selling in the temple
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* Whose walls were built with signs and martyrdoms!
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* O soldiery of heaven, whom I contemplate,
\q1 \v 125 \vp 125\vp* Implore for those who are upon the earth
\q1 \v 126 \vp 126\vp* All gone astray after the bad example!
\b
\q1 \v 127 \vp 127\vp* Once ’twas the custom to make war with swords;
\q1 \v 128 \vp 128\vp* But now ’tis made by taking here and there
\q1 \v 129 \vp 129\vp* The bread the pitying Father shuts from none.
\b
\q1 \v 130 \vp 130\vp* Yet thou, who writest but to cancel, think
\q1 \v 131 \vp 131\vp* That Peter and that Paul, who for this vineyard
\q1 \v 132 \vp 132\vp* Which thou art spoiling died, are still alive!
\b
\q1 \v 133 \vp 133\vp* Well canst thou say: \zsay-s |who="John XXII"\*“So steadfast my desire\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 134 \vp 134\vp* \zsay-s |who="John XXII"\*Is unto him who willed to live alone,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 135 \vp 135\vp* \zsay-s |who="John XXII"\*And for a dance was led to martyrdom,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 136 \vp 136\vp* \zsay-s |who="John XXII"\*That I know not the Fisherman nor Paul.”\zsay-e\*
\c 19
\cp 19
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* Appeared before me with its wings outspread
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* The beautiful image that in sweet fruition
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* Made jubilant the interwoven souls;
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* Appeared a little ruby each, wherein
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* Ray of the sun was burning so enkindled
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* That each into mine eyes refracted it.
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* And what it now behoves me to retrace
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* Nor voice has e’er reported, nor ink written,
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* Nor was by fantasy e’er comprehended;
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* For speak I saw, and likewise heard, the beak,
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* And utter with its voice both ‘I’ and ‘My,’
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* When in conception it was ‘We’ and ‘Our.’
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* And it began: \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*“Being just and merciful\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Am I exalted here unto that glory\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Which cannot be exceeded by desire;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*And upon earth I left my memory\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Such, that the evil-minded people there\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Commend it, but continue not the story.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* So doth a single heat from many embers
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* Make itself felt, even as from many loves
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* Issued a single sound from out that image.
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* Whence I thereafter: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“O perpetual flowers\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Of the eternal joy, that only one\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Make me perceive your odours manifold,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Exhaling, break within me the great fast\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Which a long season has in hunger held me,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Not finding for it any food on earth.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Well do I know, that if in heaven its mirror\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Justice Divine another realm doth make,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Yours apprehends it not through any veil.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*You know how I attentively address me\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*To listen; and you know what is the doubt\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*That is in me so very old a fast.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* Even as a falcon, issuing from his hood,
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* Doth move his head, and with his wings applaud him,
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* Showing desire, and making himself fine,
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* Saw I become that standard, which of lauds
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* Was interwoven of the grace divine,
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* With such songs as he knows who there rejoices.
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* Then it began: \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*“He who a compass turned\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*On the world’s outer verge, and who within it\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Devised so much occult and manifest,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Could not the impress of his power so make\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*On all the universe, as that his Word\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Should not remain in infinite excess.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*And this makes certain that the first proud being,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Who was the paragon of every creature,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*By not awaiting light fell immature.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*And hence appears it, that each minor nature\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Is scant receptacle unto that good\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Which has no end, and by itself is measured.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*In consequence our vision, which perforce\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Must be some ray of that intelligence\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*With which all things whatever are replete,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Cannot in its own nature be so potent,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*That it shall not its origin discern\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Far beyond that which is apparent to it.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Therefore into the justice sempiternal\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*The power of vision that your world receives,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*As eye into the ocean, penetrates;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Which, though it see the bottom near the shore,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Upon the deep perceives it not, and yet\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*’Tis there, but it is hidden by the depth.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*There is no light but comes from the serene\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*That never is o’ercast, nay, it is darkness\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Or shadow of the flesh, or else its poison.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Amply to thee is opened now the cavern\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Which has concealed from thee the living justice\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Of which thou mad’st such frequent questioning.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*For saidst thou: ‘Born a man is on the shore\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Of Indus, and is none who there can speak\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Of Christ, nor who can read, nor who can write;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*And all his inclinations and his actions\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Are good, so far as human reason sees,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Without a sin in life or in discourse:\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*He dieth unbaptised and without faith;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Where is this justice that condemneth him?\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Where is his fault, if he do not believe?’\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Now who art thou, that on the bench wouldst sit\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*In judgment at a thousand miles away,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*With the short vision of a single span?\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Truly to him who with me subtilizes,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*If so the Scripture were not over you,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*For doubting there were marvellous occasion.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*O animals terrene, O stolid minds,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*The primal will, that in itself is good,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Ne’er from itself, the Good Supreme, has moved.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*So much is just as is accordant with it;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*No good created draws it to itself,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*But it, by raying forth, occasions that.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* Even as above her nest goes circling round
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* The stork when she has fed her little ones,
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* And he who has been fed looks up at her,
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* So lifted I my brows, and even such
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* Became the blessed image, which its wings
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* Was moving, by so many counsels urged.
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* Circling around it sang, and said: \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*“As are\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*My notes to thee, who dost not comprehend them,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Such is the eternal judgment to you mortals.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* Those lucent splendours of the Holy Spirit
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* Grew quiet then, but still within the standard
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* That made the Romans reverend to the world.
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* It recommenced: \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*“Unto this kingdom never\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Ascended one who had not faith in Christ,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Before or since he to the tree was nailed.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*But look thou, many crying are, ‘Christ, Christ!’\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Who at the judgment shall be far less near\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*To him than some shall be who knew not Christ.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Such Christians shall the Ethiop condemn,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*When the two companies shall be divided,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*The one for ever rich, the other poor.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*What to your kings may not the Persians say,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*When they that volume opened shall behold\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*In which are written down all their dispraises?\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*There shall be seen, among the deeds of Albert,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*That which ere long shall set the pen in motion,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*For which the realm of Prague shall be deserted.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*There shall be seen the woe that on the Seine\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*He brings by falsifying of the coin,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Who by the blow of a wild boar shall die.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*There shall be seen the pride that causes thirst,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Which makes the Scot and Englishman so mad\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*That they within their boundaries cannot rest;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Be seen the luxury and effeminate life\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 125 \vp 125\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Of him of Spain, and the Bohemian,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 126 \vp 126\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Who valour never knew and never wished;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 127 \vp 127\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Be seen the Cripple of Jerusalem,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 128 \vp 128\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*His goodness represented by an I,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 129 \vp 129\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*While the reverse an M shall represent;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 130 \vp 130\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Be seen the avarice and poltroonery\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 131 \vp 131\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Of him who guards the Island of the Fire,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 132 \vp 132\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Wherein Anchises finished his long life;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 133 \vp 133\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*And to declare how pitiful he is\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 134 \vp 134\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Shall be his record in contracted letters\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 135 \vp 135\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Which shall make note of much in little space.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 136 \vp 136\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*And shall appear to each one the foul deeds\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 137 \vp 137\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Of uncle and of brother who a nation\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 138 \vp 138\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*So famous have dishonoured, and two crowns.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 139 \vp 139\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*And he of Portugal and he of Norway\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 140 \vp 140\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Shall there be known, and he of Rascia too,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 141 \vp 141\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Who saw in evil hour the coin of Venice.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 142 \vp 142\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*O happy Hungary, if she let herself\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 143 \vp 143\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Be wronged no farther! and Navarre the happy,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 144 \vp 144\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*If with the hills that gird her she be armed!\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 145 \vp 145\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*And each one may believe that now, as hansel\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 146 \vp 146\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Thereof, do Nicosia and Famagosta\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 147 \vp 147\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Lament and rage because of their own beast,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 148 \vp 148\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Who from the others’ flank departeth not.”\zsay-e\*
\c 20
\cp 20
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* When he who all the world illuminates
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* Out of our hemisphere so far descends
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* That on all sides the daylight is consumed,
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* The heaven, that erst by him alone was kindled,
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* Doth suddenly reveal itself again
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* By many lights, wherein is one resplendent.
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* And came into my mind this act of heaven,
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* When the ensign of the world and of its leaders
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* Had silent in the blessed beak become;
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* Because those living luminaries all,
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* By far more luminous, did songs begin
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* Lapsing and falling from my memory.
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* O gentle Love, that with a smile dost cloak thee,
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* How ardent in those sparks didst thou appear,
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* That had the breath alone of holy thoughts!
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* After the precious and pellucid crystals,
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* With which begemmed the sixth light I beheld,
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* Silence imposed on the angelic bells,
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* I seemed to hear the murmuring of a river
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* That clear descendeth down from rock to rock,
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* Showing the affluence of its mountain-top.
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* And as the sound upon the cithern’s neck
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* Taketh its form, and as upon the vent
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* Of rustic pipe the wind that enters it,
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* Even thus, relieved from the delay of waiting,
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* That murmuring of the eagle mounted up
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* Along its neck, as if it had been hollow.
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* There it became a voice, and issued thence
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* From out its beak, in such a form of words
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* As the heart waited for wherein I wrote them.
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*“The part in me which sees and bears the sun\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*In mortal eagles,”\zsay-e\* it began to me,
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*“Now fixedly must needs be looked upon;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*For of the fires of which I make my figure,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Those whence the eye doth sparkle in my head\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Of all their orders the supremest are.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*He who is shining in the midst as pupil\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Was once the singer of the Holy Spirit,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Who bore the ark from city unto city;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Now knoweth he the merit of his song,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*In so far as effect of his own counsel,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*By the reward which is commensurate.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Of five, that make a circle for my brow,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*He that approacheth nearest to my beak\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Did the poor widow for her son console;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Now knoweth he how dearly it doth cost\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Not following Christ, by the experience\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Of this sweet life and of its opposite.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*He who comes next in the circumference\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Of which I speak, upon its highest arc,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Did death postpone by penitence sincere;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Now knoweth he that the eternal judgment\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Suffers no change, albeit worthy prayer\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Maketh below to-morrow of to-day.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*The next who follows, with the laws and me,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Under the good intent that bore bad fruit\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Became a Greek by ceding to the pastor;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Now knoweth he how all the ill deduced\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*From his good action is not harmful to him,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Although the world thereby may be destroyed.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*And he, whom in the downward arc thou seest,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Guglielmo was, whom the same land deplores\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*That weepeth Charles and Frederick yet alive;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Now knoweth he how heaven enamoured is\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*With a just king; and in the outward show\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Of his effulgence he reveals it still.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Who would believe, down in the errant world,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*That e’er the Trojan Ripheus in this round\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Could be the fifth one of the holy lights?\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Now knoweth he enough of what the world\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Has not the power to see of grace divine,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Although his sight may not discern the bottom.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* Like as a lark that in the air expatiates,
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* First singing and then silent with content
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* Of the last sweetness that doth satisfy her,
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* Such seemed to me the image of the imprint
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* Of the eternal pleasure, by whose will
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* Doth everything become the thing it is.
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* And notwithstanding to my doubt I was
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* As glass is to the colour that invests it,
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* To wait the time in silence it endured not,
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* But forth from out my mouth, \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“What things are these?”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* Extorted with the force of its own weight;
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* Whereat I saw great joy of coruscation.
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* Thereafterward with eye still more enkindled
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* The blessed standard made to me reply,
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* To keep me not in wonderment suspended:
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*“I see that thou believest in these things\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Because I say them, but thou seest not how;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*So that, although believed in, they are hidden.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Thou doest as he doth who a thing by name\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Well apprehendeth, but its quiddity\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Cannot perceive, unless another show it.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*‘Regnum coelorum’ suffereth violence\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*From fervent love, and from that living hope\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*That overcometh the Divine volition;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Not in the guise that man o’ercometh man,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*But conquers it because it will be conquered,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*And conquered conquers by benignity.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*The first life of the eyebrow and the fifth\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Cause thee astonishment, because with them\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Thou seest the region of the angels painted.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*They passed not from their bodies, as thou thinkest,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Gentiles, but Christians in the steadfast faith\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Of feet that were to suffer and had suffered.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*For one from Hell, where no one e’er turns back\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Unto good will, returned unto his bones,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*And that of living hope was the reward,—\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Of living hope, that placed its efficacy\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*In prayers to God made to resuscitate him,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*So that ’twere possible to move his will.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*The glorious soul concerning which I speak,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Returning to the flesh, where brief its stay,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Believed in Him who had the power to aid it;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*And, in believing, kindled to such fire\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Of genuine love, that at the second death\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Worthy it was to come unto this joy.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*The other one, through grace, that from so deep\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*A fountain wells that never hath the eye\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Of any creature reached its primal wave,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Set all his love below on righteousness;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Wherefore from grace to grace did God unclose\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*His eye to our redemption yet to be,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Whence he believed therein, and suffered not\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 125 \vp 125\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*From that day forth the stench of paganism,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 126 \vp 126\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*And he reproved therefor the folk perverse.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 127 \vp 127\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Those Maidens three, whom at the right-hand wheel\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 128 \vp 128\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Thou didst behold, were unto him for baptism\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 129 \vp 129\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*More than a thousand years before baptizing.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 130 \vp 130\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*O thou predestination, how remote\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 131 \vp 131\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Thy root is from the aspect of all those\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 132 \vp 132\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Who the First Cause do not behold entire!\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 133 \vp 133\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*And you, O mortals! hold yourselves restrained\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 134 \vp 134\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*In judging; for ourselves, who look on God,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 135 \vp 135\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*We do not know as yet all the elect;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 136 \vp 136\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*And sweet to us is such a deprivation,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 137 \vp 137\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*Because our good in this good is made perfect,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 138 \vp 138\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Eagle"\*That whatsoe’er God wills, we also will.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 139 \vp 139\vp* After this manner by that shape divine,
\q1 \v 140 \vp 140\vp* To make clear in me my short-sightedness,
\q1 \v 141 \vp 141\vp* Was given to me a pleasant medicine;
\b
\q1 \v 142 \vp 142\vp* And as good singer a good lutanist
\q1 \v 143 \vp 143\vp* Accompanies with vibrations of the chords,
\q1 \v 144 \vp 144\vp* Whereby more pleasantness the song acquires,
\b
\q1 \v 145 \vp 145\vp* So, while it spake, do I remember me
\q1 \v 146 \vp 146\vp* That I beheld both of those blessed lights,
\q1 \v 147 \vp 147\vp* Even as the winking of the eyes concords,
\b
\q1 \v 148 \vp 148\vp* Moving unto the words their little flames.
\c 21
\cp 21
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* Already on my Lady’s face mine eyes
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* Again were fastened, and with these my mind,
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* And from all other purpose was withdrawn;
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* And she smiled not; but \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*“If I were to smile,”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* She unto me began, \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*“thou wouldst become\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Like Semele, when she was turned to ashes.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Because my beauty, that along the stairs\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Of the eternal palace more enkindles,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*As thou hast seen, the farther we ascend,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*If it were tempered not, is so resplendent\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*That all thy mortal power in its effulgence\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Would seem a leaflet that the thunder crushes.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*We are uplifted to the seventh splendour,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*That underneath the burning Lion’s breast\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Now radiates downward mingled with his power.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Fix in direction of thine eyes the mind,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*And make of them a mirror for the figure\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*That in this mirror shall appear to thee.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* He who could know what was the pasturage
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* My sight had in that blessed countenance,
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* When I transferred me to another care,
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* Would recognize how grateful was to me
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* Obedience unto my celestial escort,
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* By counterpoising one side with the other.
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* Within the crystal which, around the world
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* Revolving, bears the name of its dear leader,
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* Under whom every wickedness lay dead,
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* Coloured like gold, on which the sunshine gleams,
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* A stairway I beheld to such a height
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* Uplifted, that mine eye pursued it not.
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* Likewise beheld I down the steps descending
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* So many splendours, that I thought each light
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* That in the heaven appears was there diffused.
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* And as accordant with their natural custom
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* The rooks together at the break of day
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* Bestir themselves to warm their feathers cold;
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* Then some of them fly off without return,
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* Others come back to where they started from,
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* And others, wheeling round, still keep at home;
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* Such fashion it appeared to me was there
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* Within the sparkling that together came,
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* As soon as on a certain step it struck,
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* And that which nearest unto us remained
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* Became so clear, that in my thought I said,
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“Well I perceive the love thou showest me;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*But she, from whom I wait the how and when\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Of speech and silence, standeth still; whence I\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Against desire do well if I ask not.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* She thereupon, who saw my silentness
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* In the sight of Him who seeth everything,
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* Said unto me, \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*“Let loose thy warm desire.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* And I began: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“No merit of my own\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Renders me worthy of response from thee;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*But for her sake who granteth me the asking,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Thou blessed life that dost remain concealed\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*In thy beatitude, make known to me\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*The cause which draweth thee so near my side;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*And tell me why is silent in this wheel\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*The dulcet symphony of Paradise,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*That through the rest below sounds so devoutly.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* \zsay-s |who="Peter Damian"\*“Thou hast thy hearing mortal as thy sight,”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* It answer made to me; \zsay-s |who="Peter Damian"\*“they sing not here,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* \zsay-s |who="Peter Damian"\*For the same cause that Beatrice has not smiled.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* \zsay-s |who="Peter Damian"\*Thus far adown the holy stairway’s steps\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* \zsay-s |who="Peter Damian"\*Have I descended but to give thee welcome\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* \zsay-s |who="Peter Damian"\*With words, and with the light that mantles me;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* \zsay-s |who="Peter Damian"\*Nor did more love cause me to be more ready,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* \zsay-s |who="Peter Damian"\*For love as much and more up there is burning,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* \zsay-s |who="Peter Damian"\*As doth the flaming manifest to thee.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* \zsay-s |who="Peter Damian"\*But the high charity, that makes us servants\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* \zsay-s |who="Peter Damian"\*Prompt to the counsel which controls the world,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* \zsay-s |who="Peter Damian"\*Allotteth here, even as thou dost observe.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“I see full well,”\zsay-e\* said I, \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“O sacred lamp!\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*How love unfettered in this court sufficeth\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*To follow the eternal Providence;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*But this is what seems hard for me to see,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Wherefore predestinate wast thou alone\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Unto this office from among thy consorts.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* No sooner had I come to the last word,
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* Than of its middle made the light a centre,
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* Whirling itself about like a swift millstone.
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* When answer made the love that was therein:
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* \zsay-s |who="Peter Damian"\*“On me directed is a light divine,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* \zsay-s |who="Peter Damian"\*Piercing through this in which I am embosomed,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* \zsay-s |who="Peter Damian"\*Of which the virtue with my sight conjoined\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* \zsay-s |who="Peter Damian"\*Lifts me above myself so far, I see\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* \zsay-s |who="Peter Damian"\*The supreme essence from which this is drawn.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* \zsay-s |who="Peter Damian"\*Hence comes the joyfulness with which I flame,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* \zsay-s |who="Peter Damian"\*For to my sight, as far as it is clear,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* \zsay-s |who="Peter Damian"\*The clearness of the flame I equal make.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* \zsay-s |who="Peter Damian"\*But that soul in the heaven which is most pure,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* \zsay-s |who="Peter Damian"\*That seraph which his eye on God most fixes,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* \zsay-s |who="Peter Damian"\*Could this demand of thine not satisfy;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* \zsay-s |who="Peter Damian"\*Because so deeply sinks in the abyss\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* \zsay-s |who="Peter Damian"\*Of the eternal statute what thou askest,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* \zsay-s |who="Peter Damian"\*From all created sight it is cut off.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* \zsay-s |who="Peter Damian"\*And to the mortal world, when thou returnest,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* \zsay-s |who="Peter Damian"\*This carry back, that it may not presume\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* \zsay-s |who="Peter Damian"\*Longer tow’rd such a goal to move its feet.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* \zsay-s |who="Peter Damian"\*The mind, that shineth here, on earth doth smoke;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* \zsay-s |who="Peter Damian"\*From this observe how can it do below\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* \zsay-s |who="Peter Damian"\*That which it cannot though the heaven assume it?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* Such limit did its words prescribe to me,
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* The question I relinquished, and restricted
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* Myself to ask it humbly who it was.
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* \zsay-s |who="Peter Damian"\*“Between two shores of Italy rise cliffs,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* \zsay-s |who="Peter Damian"\*And not far distant from thy native place,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* \zsay-s |who="Peter Damian"\*So high, the thunders far below them sound,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* \zsay-s |who="Peter Damian"\*And form a ridge that Catria is called,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* \zsay-s |who="Peter Damian"\*’Neath which is consecrate a hermitage\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* \zsay-s |who="Peter Damian"\*Wont to be dedicate to worship only.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* Thus unto me the third speech recommenced,
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* And then, continuing, it said: \zsay-s |who="Peter Damian"\*“Therein\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* \zsay-s |who="Peter Damian"\*Unto God’s service I became so steadfast,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* \zsay-s |who="Peter Damian"\*That feeding only on the juice of olives\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* \zsay-s |who="Peter Damian"\*Lightly I passed away the heats and frosts,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* \zsay-s |who="Peter Damian"\*Contented in my thoughts contemplative.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* \zsay-s |who="Peter Damian"\*That cloister used to render to these heavens\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* \zsay-s |who="Peter Damian"\*Abundantly, and now is empty grown,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* \zsay-s |who="Peter Damian"\*So that perforce it soon must be revealed.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* \zsay-s |who="Peter Damian"\*I in that place was Peter Damiano;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* \zsay-s |who="Peter Damian"\*And Peter the Sinner was I in the house\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* \zsay-s |who="Peter Damian"\*Of Our Lady on the Adriatic shore.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* \zsay-s |who="Peter Damian"\*Little of mortal life remained to me,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 125 \vp 125\vp* \zsay-s |who="Peter Damian"\*When I was called and dragged forth to the hat\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 126 \vp 126\vp* \zsay-s |who="Peter Damian"\*Which shifteth evermore from bad to worse.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 127 \vp 127\vp* \zsay-s |who="Peter Damian"\*Came Cephas, and the mighty Vessel came\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 128 \vp 128\vp* \zsay-s |who="Peter Damian"\*Of the Holy Spirit, meagre and barefooted,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 129 \vp 129\vp* \zsay-s |who="Peter Damian"\*Taking the food of any hostelry.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 130 \vp 130\vp* \zsay-s |who="Peter Damian"\*Now some one to support them on each side\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 131 \vp 131\vp* \zsay-s |who="Peter Damian"\*The modern shepherds need, and some to lead them,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 132 \vp 132\vp* \zsay-s |who="Peter Damian"\*So heavy are they, and to hold their trains.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 133 \vp 133\vp* \zsay-s |who="Peter Damian"\*They cover up their palfreys with their cloaks,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 134 \vp 134\vp* \zsay-s |who="Peter Damian"\*So that two beasts go underneath one skin;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 135 \vp 135\vp* \zsay-s |who="Peter Damian"\*O Patience, that dost tolerate so much!”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 136 \vp 136\vp* At this voice saw I many little flames
\q1 \v 137 \vp 137\vp* From step to step descending and revolving,
\q1 \v 138 \vp 138\vp* And every revolution made them fairer.
\b
\q1 \v 139 \vp 139\vp* Round about this one came they and stood still,
\q1 \v 140 \vp 140\vp* And a cry uttered of so loud a sound,
\q1 \v 141 \vp 141\vp* It here could find no parallel, nor I
\b
\q1 \v 142 \vp 142\vp* Distinguished it, the thunder so o’ercame me.
\c 22
\cp 22
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* Oppressed with stupor, I unto my guide
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* Turned like a little child who always runs
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* For refuge there where he confideth most;
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* And she, even as a mother who straightway
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* Gives comfort to her pale and breathless boy
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* With voice whose wont it is to reassure him,
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* Said to me: \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*“Knowest thou not thou art in heaven,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*And knowest thou not that heaven is holy all\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*And what is done here cometh from good zeal?\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*After what wise the singing would have changed thee\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*And I by smiling, thou canst now imagine,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Since that the cry has startled thee so much,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*In which if thou hadst understood its prayers\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Already would be known to thee the vengeance\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Which thou shalt look upon before thou diest.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*The sword above here smiteth not in haste\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Nor tardily, howe’er it seem to him\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Who fearing or desiring waits for it.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*But turn thee round towards the others now,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*For very illustrious spirits shalt thou see,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*If thou thy sight directest as I say.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* As it seemed good to her mine eyes I turned,
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* And saw a hundred spherules that together
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* With mutual rays each other more embellished.
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* I stood as one who in himself represses
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* The point of his desire, and ventures not
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* To question, he so feareth the too much.
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* And now the largest and most luculent
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* Among those pearls came forward, that it might
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* Make my desire concerning it content.
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* Within it then I heard: \zsay-s |who="Benedict"\*“If thou couldst see\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* \zsay-s |who="Benedict"\*Even as myself the charity that burns\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* \zsay-s |who="Benedict"\*Among us, thy conceits would be expressed;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* \zsay-s |who="Benedict"\*But, that by waiting thou mayst not come late\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* \zsay-s |who="Benedict"\*To the high end, I will make answer even\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* \zsay-s |who="Benedict"\*Unto the thought of which thou art so chary.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* \zsay-s |who="Benedict"\*That mountain on whose slope Cassino stands\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* \zsay-s |who="Benedict"\*Was frequented of old upon its summit\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* \zsay-s |who="Benedict"\*By a deluded folk and ill-disposed;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* \zsay-s |who="Benedict"\*And I am he who first up thither bore\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* \zsay-s |who="Benedict"\*The name of Him who brought upon the earth\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* \zsay-s |who="Benedict"\*The truth that so much sublimateth us.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* \zsay-s |who="Benedict"\*And such abundant grace upon me shone\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* \zsay-s |who="Benedict"\*That all the neighbouring towns I drew away\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* \zsay-s |who="Benedict"\*From the impious worship that seduced the world.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* \zsay-s |who="Benedict"\*These other fires, each one of them, were men\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* \zsay-s |who="Benedict"\*Contemplative, enkindled by that heat\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* \zsay-s |who="Benedict"\*Which maketh holy flowers and fruits spring up.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* \zsay-s |who="Benedict"\*Here is Macarius, here is Romualdus,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* \zsay-s |who="Benedict"\*Here are my brethren, who within the cloisters\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* \zsay-s |who="Benedict"\*Their footsteps stayed and kept a steadfast heart.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* And I to him: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“The affection which thou showest\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Speaking with me, and the good countenance\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Which I behold and note in all your ardours,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*In me have so my confidence dilated\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*As the sun doth the rose, when it becomes\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*As far unfolded as it hath the power.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Therefore I pray, and thou assure me, father,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*If I may so much grace receive, that I\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*May thee behold with countenance unveiled.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* He thereupon: \zsay-s |who="Benedict"\*“Brother, thy high desire\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* \zsay-s |who="Benedict"\*In the remotest sphere shall be fulfilled,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* \zsay-s |who="Benedict"\*Where are fulfilled all others and my own.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* \zsay-s |who="Benedict"\*There perfect is, and ripened, and complete,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* \zsay-s |who="Benedict"\*Every desire; within that one alone\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* \zsay-s |who="Benedict"\*Is every part where it has always been;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* \zsay-s |who="Benedict"\*For it is not in space, nor turns on poles,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* \zsay-s |who="Benedict"\*And unto it our stairway reaches up,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* \zsay-s |who="Benedict"\*Whence thus from out thy sight it steals away.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* \zsay-s |who="Benedict"\*Up to that height the Patriarch Jacob saw it\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* \zsay-s |who="Benedict"\*Extending its supernal part, what time\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* \zsay-s |who="Benedict"\*So thronged with angels it appeared to him.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* \zsay-s |who="Benedict"\*But to ascend it now no one uplifts\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* \zsay-s |who="Benedict"\*His feet from off the earth, and now my Rule\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* \zsay-s |who="Benedict"\*Below remaineth for mere waste of paper.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* \zsay-s |who="Benedict"\*The walls that used of old to be an Abbey\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* \zsay-s |who="Benedict"\*Are changed to dens of robbers, and the cowls\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* \zsay-s |who="Benedict"\*Are sacks filled full of miserable flour.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* \zsay-s |who="Benedict"\*But heavy usury is not taken up\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* \zsay-s |who="Benedict"\*So much against God’s pleasure as that fruit\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* \zsay-s |who="Benedict"\*Which maketh so insane the heart of monks;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* \zsay-s |who="Benedict"\*For whatsoever hath the Church in keeping\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* \zsay-s |who="Benedict"\*Is for the folk that ask it in God’s name,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* \zsay-s |who="Benedict"\*Not for one’s kindred or for something worse.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* \zsay-s |who="Benedict"\*The flesh of mortals is so very soft,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* \zsay-s |who="Benedict"\*That good beginnings down below suffice not\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* \zsay-s |who="Benedict"\*From springing of the oak to bearing acorns.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* \zsay-s |who="Benedict"\*Peter began with neither gold nor silver,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* \zsay-s |who="Benedict"\*And I with orison and abstinence,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* \zsay-s |who="Benedict"\*And Francis with humility his convent.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* \zsay-s |who="Benedict"\*And if thou lookest at each one’s beginning,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* \zsay-s |who="Benedict"\*And then regardest whither he has run,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* \zsay-s |who="Benedict"\*Thou shalt behold the white changed into brown.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* \zsay-s |who="Benedict"\*In verity the Jordan backward turned,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* \zsay-s |who="Benedict"\*And the sea’s fleeing, when God willed were more\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* \zsay-s |who="Benedict"\*A wonder to behold, than succour here.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* Thus unto me he said; and then withdrew
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* To his own band, and the band closed together;
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* Then like a whirlwind all was upward rapt.
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* The gentle Lady urged me on behind them
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* Up o’er that stairway by a single sign,
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* So did her virtue overcome my nature;
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* Nor here below, where one goes up and down
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* By natural law, was motion e’er so swift
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* That it could be compared unto my wing.
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* Reader, as I may unto that devout
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* Triumph return, on whose account I often
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* For my transgressions weep and beat my breast,—
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* Thou hadst not thrust thy finger in the fire
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* And drawn it out again, before I saw
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* The sign that follows Taurus, and was in it.
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* O glorious stars, O light impregnated
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* With mighty virtue, from which I acknowledge
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* All of my genius, whatsoe’er it be,
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* With you was born, and hid himself with you,
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* He who is father of all mortal life,
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* When first I tasted of the Tuscan air;
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* And then when grace was freely given to me
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* To enter the high wheel which turns you round,
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* Your region was allotted unto me.
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* To you devoutly at this hour my soul
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* Is sighing, that it virtue may acquire
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* For the stern pass that draws it to itself.
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*“Thou art so near unto the last salvation,”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 125 \vp 125\vp* Thus Beatrice began, \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*“thou oughtest now\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 126 \vp 126\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*To have thine eyes unclouded and acute;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 127 \vp 127\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*And therefore, ere thou enter farther in,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 128 \vp 128\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Look down once more, and see how vast a world\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 129 \vp 129\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Thou hast already put beneath thy feet;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 130 \vp 130\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*So that thy heart, as jocund as it may,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 131 \vp 131\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Present itself to the triumphant throng\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 132 \vp 132\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*That comes rejoicing through this rounded ether.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 133 \vp 133\vp* I with my sight returned through one and all
\q1 \v 134 \vp 134\vp* The sevenfold spheres, and I beheld this globe
\q1 \v 135 \vp 135\vp* Such that I smiled at its ignoble semblance;
\b
\q1 \v 136 \vp 136\vp* And that opinion I approve as best
\q1 \v 137 \vp 137\vp* Which doth account it least; and he who thinks
\q1 \v 138 \vp 138\vp* Of something else may truly be called just.
\b
\q1 \v 139 \vp 139\vp* I saw the daughter of Latona shining
\q1 \v 140 \vp 140\vp* Without that shadow, which to me was cause
\q1 \v 141 \vp 141\vp* That once I had believed her rare and dense.
\b
\q1 \v 142 \vp 142\vp* The aspect of thy son, Hyperion,
\q1 \v 143 \vp 143\vp* Here I sustained, and saw how move themselves
\q1 \v 144 \vp 144\vp* Around and near him Maia and Dione.
\b
\q1 \v 145 \vp 145\vp* Thence there appeared the temperateness of Jove
\q1 \v 146 \vp 146\vp* ’Twixt son and father, and to me was clear
\q1 \v 147 \vp 147\vp* The change that of their whereabout they make;
\b
\q1 \v 148 \vp 148\vp* And all the seven made manifest to me
\q1 \v 149 \vp 149\vp* How great they are, and eke how swift they are,
\q1 \v 150 \vp 150\vp* And how they are in distant habitations.
\b
\q1 \v 151 \vp 151\vp* The threshing-floor that maketh us so proud,
\q1 \v 152 \vp 152\vp* To me revolving with the eternal Twins,
\q1 \v 153 \vp 153\vp* Was all apparent made from hill to harbour!
\b
\q1 \v 154 \vp 154\vp* Then to the beauteous eyes mine eyes I turned.
\c 23
\cp 23
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* Even as a bird, ’mid the beloved leaves,
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* Quiet upon the nest of her sweet brood
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* Throughout the night, that hideth all things from us,
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* Who, that she may behold their longed-for looks
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* And find the food wherewith to nourish them,
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* In which, to her, grave labours grateful are,
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* Anticipates the time on open spray
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* And with an ardent longing waits the sun,
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* Gazing intent as soon as breaks the dawn:
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* Even thus my Lady standing was, erect
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* And vigilant, turned round towards the zone
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* Underneath which the sun displays less haste;
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* So that beholding her distraught and wistful,
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* Such I became as he is who desiring
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* For something yearns, and hoping is appeased.
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* But brief the space from one When to the other;
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* Of my awaiting, say I, and the seeing
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* The welkin grow resplendent more and more.
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* And Beatrice exclaimed: \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*“Behold the hosts\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Of Christ’s triumphal march, and all the fruit\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Harvested by the rolling of these spheres!”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* It seemed to me her face was all aflame;
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* And eyes she had so full of ecstasy
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* That I must needs pass on without describing.
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* As when in nights serene of the full moon
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* Smiles Trivia among the nymphs eternal
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* Who paint the firmament through all its gulfs,
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* Saw I, above the myriads of lamps,
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* A Sun that one and all of them enkindled,
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* E’en as our own doth the supernal sights,
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* And through the living light transparent shone
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* The lucent substance so intensely clear
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* Into my sight, that I sustained it not.
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* O Beatrice, thou gentle guide and dear!
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* To me she said: \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*“What overmasters thee\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*A virtue is from which naught shields itself.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*There are the wisdom and the omnipotence\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*That oped the thoroughfares ’twixt heaven and earth,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*For which there erst had been so long a yearning.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* As fire from out a cloud unlocks itself,
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* Dilating so it finds not room therein,
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* And down, against its nature, falls to earth,
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* So did my mind, among those aliments
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* Becoming larger, issue from itself,
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* And that which it became cannot remember.
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*“Open thine eyes, and look at what I am:\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Thou hast beheld such things, that strong enough\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Hast thou become to tolerate my smile.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* I was as one who still retains the feeling
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* Of a forgotten vision, and endeavours
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* In vain to bring it back into his mind,
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* When I this invitation heard, deserving
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* Of so much gratitude, it never fades
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* Out of the book that chronicles the past.
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* If at this moment sounded all the tongues
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* That Polyhymnia and her sisters made
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* Most lubrical with their delicious milk,
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* To aid me, to a thousandth of the truth
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* It would not reach, singing the holy smile
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* And how the holy aspect it illumed.
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* And therefore, representing Paradise,
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* The sacred poem must perforce leap over,
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* Even as a man who finds his way cut off;
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* But whoso thinketh of the ponderous theme,
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* And of the mortal shoulder laden with it,
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* Should blame it not, if under this it tremble.
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* It is no passage for a little boat
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* This which goes cleaving the audacious prow,
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* Nor for a pilot who would spare himself.
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*“Why doth my face so much enamour thee,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*That to the garden fair thou turnest not,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Which under the rays of Christ is blossoming?\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*There is the Rose in which the Word Divine\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Became incarnate; there the lilies are\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*By whose perfume the good way was discovered.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* Thus Beatrice; and I, who to her counsels
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* Was wholly ready, once again betook me
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* Unto the battle of the feeble brows.
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* As in the sunshine, that unsullied streams
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* Through fractured cloud, ere now a meadow of flowers
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* Mine eyes with shadow covered o’er have seen,
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* So troops of splendours manifold I saw
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* Illumined from above with burning rays,
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* Beholding not the source of the effulgence.
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* O power benignant that dost so imprint them!
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* Thou didst exalt thyself to give more scope
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* There to mine eyes, that were not strong enough.
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* The name of that fair flower I e’er invoke
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* Morning and evening utterly enthralled
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* My soul to gaze upon the greater fire.
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* And when in both mine eyes depicted were
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* The glory and greatness of the living star
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* Which there excelleth, as it here excelled,
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* Athwart the heavens a little torch descended
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* Formed in a circle like a coronal,
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* And cinctured it, and whirled itself about it.
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* Whatever melody most sweetly soundeth
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* On earth, and to itself most draws the soul,
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* Would seem a cloud that, rent asunder, thunders,
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* Compared unto the sounding of that lyre
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* Wherewith was crowned the sapphire beautiful,
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* Which gives the clearest heaven its sapphire hue.
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* \zsay-s |who="Gabriel"\*“I am Angelic Love, that circle round\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* \zsay-s |who="Gabriel"\*The joy sublime which breathes from out the womb\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* \zsay-s |who="Gabriel"\*That was the hostelry of our Desire;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* \zsay-s |who="Gabriel"\*And I shall circle, Lady of Heaven, while\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* \zsay-s |who="Gabriel"\*Thou followest thy Son, and mak’st diviner\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* \zsay-s |who="Gabriel"\*The sphere supreme, because thou enterest there.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* Thus did the circulated melody
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* Seal itself up; and all the other lights
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* Were making to resound the name of Mary.
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* The regal mantle of the volumes all
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* Of that world, which most fervid is and living
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* With breath of God and with his works and ways,
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* Extended over us its inner border,
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* So very distant, that the semblance of it
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* There where I was not yet appeared to me.
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* Therefore mine eyes did not possess the power
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* Of following the incoronated flame,
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* Which mounted upward near to its own seed.
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* And as a little child, that towards its mother
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* Stretches its arms, when it the milk has taken,
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* Through impulse kindled into outward flame,
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* Each of those gleams of whiteness upward reached
\q1 \v 125 \vp 125\vp* So with its summit, that the deep affection
\q1 \v 126 \vp 126\vp* They had for Mary was revealed to me.
\b
\q1 \v 127 \vp 127\vp* Thereafter they remained there in my sight,
\q1 \v 128 \vp 128\vp* ‘Regina coeli’ singing with such sweetness,
\q1 \v 129 \vp 129\vp* That ne’er from me has the delight departed.
\b
\q1 \v 130 \vp 130\vp* O, what exuberance is garnered up
\q1 \v 131 \vp 131\vp* Within those richest coffers, which had been
\q1 \v 132 \vp 132\vp* Good husbandmen for sowing here below!
\b
\q1 \v 133 \vp 133\vp* There they enjoy and live upon the treasure
\q1 \v 134 \vp 134\vp* Which was acquired while weeping in the exile
\q1 \v 135 \vp 135\vp* Of Babylon, wherein the gold was left.
\b
\q1 \v 136 \vp 136\vp* There triumpheth, beneath the exalted Son
\q1 \v 137 \vp 137\vp* Of God and Mary, in his victory,
\q1 \v 138 \vp 138\vp* Both with the ancient council and the new,
\b
\q1 \v 139 \vp 139\vp* He who doth keep the keys of such a glory.
\c 24
\cp 24
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*“O company elect to the great supper\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Of the Lamb benedight, who feedeth you\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*So that for ever full is your desire,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*If by the grace of God this man foretaste\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Something of that which falleth from your table,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Or ever death prescribe to him the time,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Direct your mind to his immense desire,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*And him somewhat bedew; ye drinking are\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*For ever at the fount whence comes his thought.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* Thus Beatrice; and those souls beatified
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* Transformed themselves to spheres on steadfast poles,
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* Flaming intensely in the guise of comets.
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* And as the wheels in works of horologes
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* Revolve so that the first to the beholder
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* Motionless seems, and the last one to fly,
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* So in like manner did those carols, dancing
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* In different measure, of their affluence
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* Give me the gauge, as they were swift or slow.
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* From that one which I noted of most beauty
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* Beheld I issue forth a fire so happy
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* That none it left there of a greater brightness;
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* And around Beatrice three several times
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* It whirled itself with so divine a song,
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* My fantasy repeats it not to me;
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* Therefore the pen skips, and I write it not,
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* Since our imagination for such folds,
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* Much more our speech, is of a tint too glaring.
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* \zsay-s |who="Saint Peter"\*“O holy sister mine, who us implorest\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* \zsay-s |who="Saint Peter"\*With such devotion, by thine ardent love\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* \zsay-s |who="Saint Peter"\*Thou dost unbind me from that beautiful sphere!”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* Thereafter, having stopped, the blessed fire
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* Unto my Lady did direct its breath,
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* Which spake in fashion as I here have said.
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* And she: \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*“O light eterne of the great man\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*To whom our Lord delivered up the keys\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*He carried down of this miraculous joy,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*This one examine on points light and grave,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*As good beseemeth thee, about the Faith\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*By means of which thou on the sea didst walk.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*If he love well, and hope well, and believe,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*From thee ’tis hid not; for thou hast thy sight\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*There where depicted everything is seen.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*But since this kingdom has made citizens\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*By means of the true Faith, to glorify it\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*’Tis well he have the chance to speak thereof.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* As baccalaureate arms himself, and speaks not
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* Until the master doth propose the question,
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* To argue it, and not to terminate it,
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* So did I arm myself with every reason,
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* While she was speaking, that I might be ready
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* For such a questioner and such profession.
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* \zsay-s |who="Saint Peter"\*“Say, thou good Christian; manifest thyself;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* \zsay-s |who="Saint Peter"\*What is the Faith?”\zsay-e\* Whereat I raised my brow
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* Unto that light wherefrom was this breathed forth.
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* Then turned I round to Beatrice, and she
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* Prompt signals made to me that I should pour
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* The water forth from my internal fountain.
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“May grace, that suffers me to make confession,”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* Began I, \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“to the great centurion,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Cause my conceptions all to be explicit!”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* And I continued: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“As the truthful pen,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Father, of thy dear brother wrote of it,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Who put with thee Rome into the good way,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Faith is the substance of the things we hope for,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*And evidence of those that are not seen;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*And this appears to me its quiddity.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* Then heard I: \zsay-s |who="Saint Peter"\*“Very rightly thou perceivest,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* \zsay-s |who="Saint Peter"\*If well thou understandest why he placed it\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* \zsay-s |who="Saint Peter"\*With substances and then with evidences.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* And I thereafterward: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“The things profound,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*That here vouchsafe to me their apparition,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Unto all eyes below are so concealed,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*That they exist there only in belief,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Upon the which is founded the high hope,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*And hence it takes the nature of a substance.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*And it behoveth us from this belief\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*To reason without having other sight,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*And hence it has the nature of evidence.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* Then heard I: \zsay-s |who="Saint Peter"\*“If whatever is acquired\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* \zsay-s |who="Saint Peter"\*Below by doctrine were thus understood,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* \zsay-s |who="Saint Peter"\*No sophist’s subtlety would there find place.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* Thus was breathed forth from that enkindled love;
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* Then added: \zsay-s |who="Saint Peter"\*“Very well has been gone over\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* \zsay-s |who="Saint Peter"\*Already of this coin the alloy and weight;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* \zsay-s |who="Saint Peter"\*But tell me if thou hast it in thy purse?”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* And I: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“Yes, both so shining and so round\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*That in its stamp there is no peradventure.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* Thereafter issued from the light profound
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* That there resplendent was: \zsay-s |who="Saint Peter"\*“This precious jewel,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* \zsay-s |who="Saint Peter"\*Upon the which is every virtue founded,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* \zsay-s |who="Saint Peter"\*Whence hadst thou it?”\zsay-e\* And I: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“The large outpouring\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Of Holy Spirit, which has been diffused\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Upon the ancient parchments and the new,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*A syllogism is, which proved it to me\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*With such acuteness, that, compared therewith,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*All demonstration seems to me obtuse.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* And then I heard: \zsay-s |who="Saint Peter"\*“The ancient and the new\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* \zsay-s |who="Saint Peter"\*Postulates, that to thee are so conclusive,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* \zsay-s |who="Saint Peter"\*Why dost thou take them for the word divine?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* And I: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“The proofs, which show the truth to me,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Are the works subsequent, whereunto Nature\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Ne’er heated iron yet, nor anvil beat.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* ’Twas answered me: \zsay-s |who="Saint Peter"\*“Say, who assureth thee\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* \zsay-s |who="Saint Peter"\*That those works ever were? the thing itself\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* \zsay-s |who="Saint Peter"\*That must be proved, nought else to thee affirms it.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“Were the world to Christianity converted,”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* I said, \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“withouten miracles, this one\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Is such, the rest are not its hundredth part;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Because that poor and fasting thou didst enter\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Into the field to sow there the good plant,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Which was a vine and has become a thorn!”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* This being finished, the high, holy Court
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* Resounded through the spheres, \zsay-s |who="The heavenly court"\*“One God we praise!”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* In melody that there above is chanted.
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* And then that Baron, who from branch to branch,
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* Examining, had thus conducted me,
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* Till the extremest leaves we were approaching,
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* Again began: \zsay-s |who="Saint Peter"\*“The Grace that dallying\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* \zsay-s |who="Saint Peter"\*Plays with thine intellect thy mouth has opened,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* \zsay-s |who="Saint Peter"\*Up to this point, as it should opened be,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* \zsay-s |who="Saint Peter"\*So that I do approve what forth emerged;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* \zsay-s |who="Saint Peter"\*But now thou must express what thou believest,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* \zsay-s |who="Saint Peter"\*And whence to thy belief it was presented.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“O holy father, spirit who beholdest\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 125 \vp 125\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*What thou believedst so that thou o’ercamest,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 126 \vp 126\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Towards the sepulchre, more youthful feet,”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 127 \vp 127\vp* Began I, \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“thou dost wish me in this place\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 128 \vp 128\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*The form to manifest of my prompt belief,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 129 \vp 129\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*And likewise thou the cause thereof demandest.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 130 \vp 130\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*And I respond: In one God I believe,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 131 \vp 131\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Sole and eterne, who moveth all the heavens\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 132 \vp 132\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*With love and with desire, himself unmoved;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 133 \vp 133\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*And of such faith not only have I proofs\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 134 \vp 134\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Physical and metaphysical, but gives them\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 135 \vp 135\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Likewise the truth that from this place rains down\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 136 \vp 136\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Through Moses, through the Prophets and the Psalms,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 137 \vp 137\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Through the Evangel, and through you, who wrote\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 138 \vp 138\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*After the fiery Spirit sanctified you;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 139 \vp 139\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*In Persons three eterne believe, and these\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 140 \vp 140\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*One essence I believe, so one and trine\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 141 \vp 141\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*They bear conjunction both with ‘sunt’ and ‘est.’\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 142 \vp 142\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*With the profound condition and divine\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 143 \vp 143\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Which now I touch upon, doth stamp my mind\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 144 \vp 144\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Ofttimes the doctrine evangelical.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 145 \vp 145\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*This the beginning is, this is the spark\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 146 \vp 146\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Which afterwards dilates to vivid flame,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 147 \vp 147\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*And, like a star in heaven, is sparkling in me.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 148 \vp 148\vp* Even as a lord who hears what pleaseth him
\q1 \v 149 \vp 149\vp* His servant straight embraces, gratulating
\q1 \v 150 \vp 150\vp* For the good news as soon as he is silent;
\b
\q1 \v 151 \vp 151\vp* So, giving me its benediction, singing,
\q1 \v 152 \vp 152\vp* Three times encircled me, when I was silent,
\q1 \v 153 \vp 153\vp* The apostolic light, at whose command
\b
\q1 \v 154 \vp 154\vp* I spoken had, in speaking I so pleased him.
\c 25
\cp 25
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* If e’er it happen that the Poem Sacred,
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* To which both heaven and earth have set their hand,
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* So that it many a year hath made me lean,
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* O’ercome the cruelty that bars me out
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* From the fair sheepfold, where a lamb I slumbered,
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* An enemy to the wolves that war upon it,
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* With other voice forthwith, with other fleece
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* Poet will I return, and at my font
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* Baptismal will I take the laurel crown;
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* Because into the Faith that maketh known
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* All souls to God there entered I, and then
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* Peter for her sake thus my brow encircled.
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* Thereafterward towards us moved a light
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* Out of that band whence issued the first-fruits
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* Which of his vicars Christ behind him left,
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* And then my Lady, full of ecstasy,
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* Said unto me: \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*“Look, look! behold the Baron\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*For whom below Galicia is frequented.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* In the same way as, when a dove alights
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* Near his companion, both of them pour forth,
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* Circling about and murmuring, their affection,
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* So one beheld I by the other grand
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* Prince glorified to be with welcome greeted,
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* Lauding the food that there above is eaten.
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* But when their gratulations were complete,
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* Silently ‘coram me’ each one stood still,
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* So incandescent it o’ercame my sight.
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* Smiling thereafterwards, said Beatrice:
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*“Illustrious life, by whom the benefactions\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Of our Basilica have been described,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Make Hope resound within this altitude;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Thou knowest as oft thou dost personify it\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*As Jesus to the three gave greater clearness.”\zsay-e\*—
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* \zsay-s |who="Saint James"\*“Lift up thy head, and make thyself assured;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* \zsay-s |who="Saint James"\*For what comes hither from the mortal world\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* \zsay-s |who="Saint James"\*Must needs be ripened in our radiance.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* This comfort came to me from the second fire;
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* Wherefore mine eyes I lifted to the hills,
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* Which bent them down before with too great weight.
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* \zsay-s |who="Saint James"\*“Since, through his grace, our Emperor wills that thou\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* \zsay-s |who="Saint James"\*Shouldst find thee face to face, before thy death,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* \zsay-s |who="Saint James"\*In the most secret chamber, with his Counts,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* \zsay-s |who="Saint James"\*So that, the truth beholden of this court,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* \zsay-s |who="Saint James"\*Hope, which below there rightfully enamours,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* \zsay-s |who="Saint James"\*Thereby thou strengthen in thyself and others,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* \zsay-s |who="Saint James"\*Say what it is, and how is flowering with it\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* \zsay-s |who="Saint James"\*Thy mind, and say from whence it came to thee.”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* Thus did the second light again continue.
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* And the Compassionate, who piloted
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* The plumage of my wings in such high flight,
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* Did in reply anticipate me thus:
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*“No child whatever the Church Militant\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Of greater hope possesses, as is written\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*In that Sun which irradiates all our band;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Therefore it is conceded him from Egypt\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*To come into Jerusalem to see,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Or ever yet his warfare be completed.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*The two remaining points, that not for knowledge\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Have been demanded, but that he report\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*How much this virtue unto thee is pleasing,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*To him I leave; for hard he will not find them,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Nor of self-praise; and let him answer them;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*And may the grace of God in this assist him!”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* As a disciple, who his teacher follows,
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* Ready and willing, where he is expert,
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* That his proficiency may be displayed,
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“Hope,”\zsay-e\* said I, \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“is the certain expectation\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Of future glory, which is the effect\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Of grace divine and merit precedent.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*From many stars this light comes unto me;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*But he instilled it first into my heart\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Who was chief singer unto the chief captain.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*‘Sperent in te,’ in the high Theody\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*He sayeth, ‘those who know thy name;’ and who\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Knoweth it not, if he my faith possess?\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Thou didst instil me, then, with his instilling\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*In the Epistle, so that I am full,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*And upon others rain again your rain.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* While I was speaking, in the living bosom
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* Of that combustion quivered an effulgence,
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* Sudden and frequent, in the guise of lightning;
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* Then breathed: \zsay-s |who="Saint James"\*“The love wherewith I am inflamed\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* \zsay-s |who="Saint James"\*Towards the virtue still which followed me\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* \zsay-s |who="Saint James"\*Unto the palm and issue of the field,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* \zsay-s |who="Saint James"\*Wills that I breathe to thee that thou delight\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* \zsay-s |who="Saint James"\*In her; and grateful to me is thy telling\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* \zsay-s |who="Saint James"\*Whatever things Hope promises to thee.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* And I: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“The ancient Scriptures and the new\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*The mark establish, and this shows it me,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Of all the souls whom God hath made his friends.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Isaiah saith, that each one garmented\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*In his own land shall be with twofold garments,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*And his own land is this delightful life.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Thy brother, too, far more explicitly,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*There where he treateth of the robes of white,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*This revelation manifests to us.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* And first, and near the ending of these words,
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* \zsay-s |who="A voice"\*“Sperent in te”\zsay-e\* from over us was heard,
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* To which responsive answered all the carols.
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* Thereafterward a light among them brightened,
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* So that, if Cancer one such crystal had,
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* Winter would have a month of one sole day.
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* And as uprises, goes, and enters the dance
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* A winsome maiden, only to do honour
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* To the new bride, and not from any failing,
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* Even thus did I behold the brightened splendour
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* Approach the two, who in a wheel revolved
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* As was beseeming to their ardent love.
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* Into the song and music there it entered;
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* And fixed on them my Lady kept her look,
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* Even as a bride silent and motionless.
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*“This is the one who lay upon the breast\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Of him our Pelican; and this is he\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*To the great office from the cross elected.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* My Lady thus; but therefore none the more
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* Did move her sight from its attentive gaze
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* Before or afterward these words of hers.
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* Even as a man who gazes, and endeavours
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* To see the eclipsing of the sun a little,
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* And who, by seeing, sightless doth become,
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* So I became before that latest fire,
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* While it was said, \zsay-s |who="Saint John"\*“Why dost thou daze thyself\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* \zsay-s |who="Saint John"\*To see a thing which here hath no existence?\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* \zsay-s |who="Saint John"\*Earth in the earth my body is, and shall be\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 125 \vp 125\vp* \zsay-s |who="Saint John"\*With all the others there, until our number\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 126 \vp 126\vp* \zsay-s |who="Saint John"\*With the eternal proposition tallies.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 127 \vp 127\vp* \zsay-s |who="Saint John"\*With the two garments in the blessed cloister\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 128 \vp 128\vp* \zsay-s |who="Saint John"\*Are the two lights alone that have ascended:\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 129 \vp 129\vp* \zsay-s |who="Saint John"\*And this shalt thou take back into your world.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 130 \vp 130\vp* And at this utterance the flaming circle
\q1 \v 131 \vp 131\vp* Grew quiet, with the dulcet intermingling
\q1 \v 132 \vp 132\vp* Of sound that by the trinal breath was made,
\b
\q1 \v 133 \vp 133\vp* As to escape from danger or fatigue
\q1 \v 134 \vp 134\vp* The oars that erst were in the water beaten
\q1 \v 135 \vp 135\vp* Are all suspended at a whistle’s sound.
\b
\q1 \v 136 \vp 136\vp* Ah, how much in my mind was I disturbed,
\q1 \v 137 \vp 137\vp* When I turned round to look on Beatrice,
\q1 \v 138 \vp 138\vp* That her I could not see, although I was
\b
\q1 \v 139 \vp 139\vp* Close at her side and in the Happy World!
\c 26
\cp 26
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* While I was doubting for my vision quenched,
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* Out of the flame refulgent that had quenched it
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* Issued a breathing, that attentive made me,
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* Saying: \zsay-s |who="Saint John"\*“While thou recoverest the sense\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* \zsay-s |who="Saint John"\*Of seeing which in me thou hast consumed,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* \zsay-s |who="Saint John"\*’Tis well that speaking thou shouldst compensate it.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* \zsay-s |who="Saint John"\*Begin then, and declare to what thy soul\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* \zsay-s |who="Saint John"\*Is aimed, and count it for a certainty,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* \zsay-s |who="Saint John"\*Sight is in thee bewildered and not dead;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* \zsay-s |who="Saint John"\*Because the Lady, who through this divine\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* \zsay-s |who="Saint John"\*Region conducteth thee, has in her look\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* \zsay-s |who="Saint John"\*The power the hand of Ananias had.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* I said: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“As pleaseth her, or soon or late\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Let the cure come to eyes that portals were\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*When she with fire I ever burn with entered.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*The Good, that gives contentment to this Court,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*The Alpha and Omega is of all\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*The writing that love reads me low or loud.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* The selfsame voice, that taken had from me
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* The terror of the sudden dazzlement,
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* To speak still farther put it in my thought;
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* And said: \zsay-s |who="Saint John"\*“In verity with finer sieve\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* \zsay-s |who="Saint John"\*Behoveth thee to sift; thee it behoveth\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* \zsay-s |who="Saint John"\*To say who aimed thy bow at such a target.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* And I: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“By philosophic arguments,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*And by authority that hence descends,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Such love must needs imprint itself in me;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*For Good, so far as good, when comprehended\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Doth straight enkindle love, and so much greater\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*As more of goodness in itself it holds;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Then to that Essence (whose is such advantage\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*That every good which out of it is found\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Is nothing but a ray of its own light)\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*More than elsewhither must the mind be moved\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Of every one, in loving, who discerns\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*The truth in which this evidence is founded.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Such truth he to my intellect reveals\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Who demonstrates to me the primal love\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Of all the sempiternal substances.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*The voice reveals it of the truthful Author,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Who says to Moses, speaking of Himself,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*‘I will make all my goodness pass before thee.’\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Thou too revealest it to me, beginning\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*The loud Evangel, that proclaims the secret\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Of heaven to earth above all other edict.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* And I heard say: \zsay-s |who="Saint John"\*“By human intellect\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* \zsay-s |who="Saint John"\*And by authority concordant with it,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* \zsay-s |who="Saint John"\*Of all thy loves reserve for God the highest.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* \zsay-s |who="Saint John"\*But say again if other cords thou feelest,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* \zsay-s |who="Saint John"\*Draw thee towards Him, that thou mayst proclaim\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* \zsay-s |who="Saint John"\*With how many teeth this love is biting thee.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* The holy purpose of the Eagle of Christ
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* Not latent was, nay, rather I perceived
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* Whither he fain would my profession lead.
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* Therefore I recommenced: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“All of those bites\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Which have the power to turn the heart to God\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Unto my charity have been concurrent.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*The being of the world, and my own being,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*The death which He endured that I may live,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*And that which all the faithful hope, as I do,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*With the forementioned vivid consciousness\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Have drawn me from the sea of love perverse,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*And of the right have placed me on the shore.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*The leaves, wherewith embowered is all the garden\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Of the Eternal Gardener, do I love\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*As much as he has granted them of good.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* As soon as I had ceased, a song most sweet
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* Throughout the heaven resounded, and my Lady
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* Said with the others, \zsay-s |who="Beatrice and the spirits"\*“Holy, holy, holy!”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* And as at some keen light one wakes from sleep
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* By reason of the visual spirit that runs
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* Unto the splendour passed from coat to coat,
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* And he who wakes abhorreth what he sees,
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* So all unconscious is his sudden waking,
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* Until the judgment cometh to his aid,
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* So from before mine eyes did Beatrice
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* Chase every mote with radiance of her own,
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* That cast its light a thousand miles and more.
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* Whence better after than before I saw,
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* And in a kind of wonderment I asked
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* About a fourth light that I saw with us.
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* And said my Lady: \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*“There within those rays\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Gazes upon its Maker the first soul\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*That ever the first virtue did create.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* Even as the bough that downward bends its top
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* At transit of the wind, and then is lifted
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* By its own virtue, which inclines it upward,
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* Likewise did I, the while that she was speaking,
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* Being amazed, and then I was made bold
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* By a desire to speak wherewith I burned.
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* And I began: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“O apple, that mature\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Alone hast been produced, O ancient father,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*To whom each wife is daughter and daughter-in-law,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Devoutly as I can I supplicate thee\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*That thou wouldst speak to me; thou seest my wish;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*And I, to hear thee quickly, speak it not.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* Sometimes an animal, when covered, struggles
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* So that his impulse needs must be apparent,
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* By reason of the wrappage following it;
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* And in like manner the primeval soul
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* Made clear to me athwart its covering
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* How jubilant it was to give me pleasure.
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* Then breathed: \zsay-s |who="Adam"\*“Without thy uttering it to me,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* \zsay-s |who="Adam"\*Thine inclination better I discern\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* \zsay-s |who="Adam"\*Than thou whatever thing is surest to thee;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* \zsay-s |who="Adam"\*For I behold it in the truthful mirror,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* \zsay-s |who="Adam"\*That of Himself all things parhelion makes,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* \zsay-s |who="Adam"\*And none makes Him parhelion of itself.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* \zsay-s |who="Adam"\*Thou fain wouldst hear how long ago God placed me\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* \zsay-s |who="Adam"\*Within the lofty garden, where this Lady\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* \zsay-s |who="Adam"\*Unto so long a stairway thee disposed.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* \zsay-s |who="Adam"\*And how long to mine eyes it was a pleasure,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* \zsay-s |who="Adam"\*And of the great disdain the proper cause,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* \zsay-s |who="Adam"\*And the language that I used and that I made.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* \zsay-s |who="Adam"\*Now, son of mine, the tasting of the tree\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* \zsay-s |who="Adam"\*Not in itself was cause of so great exile,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* \zsay-s |who="Adam"\*But solely the o’erstepping of the bounds.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* \zsay-s |who="Adam"\*There, whence thy Lady moved Virgilius,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* \zsay-s |who="Adam"\*Four thousand and three hundred and two circuits\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* \zsay-s |who="Adam"\*Made by the sun, this Council I desired;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* \zsay-s |who="Adam"\*And him I saw return to all the lights\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* \zsay-s |who="Adam"\*Of his highway nine hundred times and thirty,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* \zsay-s |who="Adam"\*Whilst I upon the earth was tarrying.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* \zsay-s |who="Adam"\*The language that I spake was quite extinct\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 125 \vp 125\vp* \zsay-s |who="Adam"\*Before that in the work interminable\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 126 \vp 126\vp* \zsay-s |who="Adam"\*The people under Nimrod were employed;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 127 \vp 127\vp* \zsay-s |who="Adam"\*For nevermore result of reasoning\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 128 \vp 128\vp* \zsay-s |who="Adam"\*(Because of human pleasure that doth change,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 129 \vp 129\vp* \zsay-s |who="Adam"\*Obedient to the heavens) was durable.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 130 \vp 130\vp* \zsay-s |who="Adam"\*A natural action is it that man speaks;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 131 \vp 131\vp* \zsay-s |who="Adam"\*But whether thus or thus, doth nature leave\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 132 \vp 132\vp* \zsay-s |who="Adam"\*To your own art, as seemeth best to you.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 133 \vp 133\vp* \zsay-s |who="Adam"\*Ere I descended to the infernal anguish,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 134 \vp 134\vp* \zsay-s |who="Adam"\*‘El’ was on earth the name of the Chief Good,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 135 \vp 135\vp* \zsay-s |who="Adam"\*From whom comes all the joy that wraps me round\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 136 \vp 136\vp* \zsay-s |who="Adam"\*‘Eli’ he then was called, and that is proper,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 137 \vp 137\vp* \zsay-s |who="Adam"\*Because the use of men is like a leaf\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 138 \vp 138\vp* \zsay-s |who="Adam"\*On bough, which goeth and another cometh.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 139 \vp 139\vp* \zsay-s |who="Adam"\*Upon the mount that highest o’er the wave\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 140 \vp 140\vp* \zsay-s |who="Adam"\*Rises was I, in life or pure or sinful,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 141 \vp 141\vp* \zsay-s |who="Adam"\*From the first hour to that which is the second,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 142 \vp 142\vp* \zsay-s |who="Adam"\*As the sun changes quadrant, to the sixth.”\zsay-e\*
\c 27
\cp 27
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* \zsay-s |who="All Paradise"\*“Glory be to the Father, to the Son,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* \zsay-s |who="All Paradise"\*And Holy Ghost!”\zsay-e\* all Paradise began,
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* So that the melody inebriate made me.
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* What I beheld seemed unto me a smile
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* Of the universe; for my inebriation
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* Found entrance through the hearing and the sight.
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* O joy! O gladness inexpressible!
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* O perfect life of love and peacefulness!
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* O riches without hankering secure!
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* Before mine eyes were standing the four torches
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* Enkindled, and the one that first had come
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* Began to make itself more luminous;
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* And even such in semblance it became
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* As Jupiter would become, if he and Mars
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* Were birds, and they should interchange their feathers.
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* That Providence, which here distributeth
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* Season and service, in the blessed choir
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* Had silence upon every side imposed.
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* When I heard say: \zsay-s |who="Saint Peter"\*“If I my colour change,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* \zsay-s |who="Saint Peter"\*Marvel not at it; for while I am speaking\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* \zsay-s |who="Saint Peter"\*Thou shalt behold all these their colour change.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* \zsay-s |who="Saint Peter"\*He who usurps upon the earth my place,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* \zsay-s |who="Saint Peter"\*My place, my place, which vacant has become\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* \zsay-s |who="Saint Peter"\*Before the presence of the Son of God,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* \zsay-s |who="Saint Peter"\*Has of my cemetery made a sewer\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* \zsay-s |who="Saint Peter"\*Of blood and stench, whereby the Perverse One,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* \zsay-s |who="Saint Peter"\*Who fell from here, below there is appeased!”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* With the same colour which, through sun adverse,
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* Painteth the clouds at evening or at morn,
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* Beheld I then the whole of heaven suffused.
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* And as a modest woman, who abides
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* Sure of herself, and at another’s failing,
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* From listening only, timorous becomes,
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* Even thus did Beatrice change countenance;
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* And I believe in heaven was such eclipse,
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* When suffered the supreme Omnipotence;
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* Thereafterward proceeded forth his words
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* With voice so much transmuted from itself,
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* The very countenance was not more changed.
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* \zsay-s |who="Saint Peter"\*“The spouse of Christ has never nurtured been\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* \zsay-s |who="Saint Peter"\*On blood of mine, of Linus and of Cletus,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* \zsay-s |who="Saint Peter"\*To be made use of in acquest of gold;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* \zsay-s |who="Saint Peter"\*But in acquest of this delightful life\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* \zsay-s |who="Saint Peter"\*Sixtus and Pius, Urban and Calixtus,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* \zsay-s |who="Saint Peter"\*After much lamentation, shed their blood.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* \zsay-s |who="Saint Peter"\*Our purpose was not, that on the right hand\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* \zsay-s |who="Saint Peter"\*Of our successors should in part be seated\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* \zsay-s |who="Saint Peter"\*The Christian folk, in part upon the other;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* \zsay-s |who="Saint Peter"\*Nor that the keys which were to me confided\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* \zsay-s |who="Saint Peter"\*Should e’er become the escutcheon on a banner,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* \zsay-s |who="Saint Peter"\*That should wage war on those who are baptized;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* \zsay-s |who="Saint Peter"\*Nor I be made the figure of a seal\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* \zsay-s |who="Saint Peter"\*To privileges venal and mendacious,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* \zsay-s |who="Saint Peter"\*Whereat I often redden and flash with fire.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* \zsay-s |who="Saint Peter"\*In garb of shepherds the rapacious wolves\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* \zsay-s |who="Saint Peter"\*Are seen from here above o’er all the pastures!\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* \zsay-s |who="Saint Peter"\*O wrath of God, why dost thou slumber still?\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* \zsay-s |who="Saint Peter"\*To drink our blood the Caorsines and Gascons\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* \zsay-s |who="Saint Peter"\*Are making ready. O thou good beginning,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* \zsay-s |who="Saint Peter"\*Unto how vile an end must thou needs fall!\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* \zsay-s |who="Saint Peter"\*But the high Providence, that with Scipio\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* \zsay-s |who="Saint Peter"\*At Rome the glory of the world defended,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* \zsay-s |who="Saint Peter"\*Will speedily bring aid, as I conceive;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* \zsay-s |who="Saint Peter"\*And thou, my son, who by thy mortal weight\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* \zsay-s |who="Saint Peter"\*Shalt down return again, open thy mouth;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* \zsay-s |who="Saint Peter"\*What I conceal not, do not thou conceal.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* As with its frozen vapours downward falls
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* In flakes our atmosphere, what time the horn
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* Of the celestial Goat doth touch the sun,
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* Upward in such array saw I the ether
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* Become, and flaked with the triumphant vapours,
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* Which there together with us had remained.
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* My sight was following up their semblances,
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* And followed till the medium, by excess,
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* The passing farther onward took from it;
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* Whereat the Lady, who beheld me freed
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* From gazing upward, said to me: \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*“Cast down\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Thy sight, and see how far thou art turned round.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* Since the first time that I had downward looked,
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* I saw that I had moved through the whole arc
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* Which the first climate makes from midst to end;
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* So that I saw the mad track of Ulysses
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* Past Gades, and this side, well nigh the shore
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* Whereon became Europa a sweet burden.
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* And of this threshing-floor the site to me
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* Were more unveiled, but the sun was proceeding
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* Under my feet, a sign and more removed.
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* My mind enamoured, which is dallying
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* At all times with my Lady, to bring back
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* To her mine eyes was more than ever ardent.
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* And if or Art or Nature has made bait
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* To catch the eyes and so possess the mind,
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* In human flesh or in its portraiture,
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* All joined together would appear as nought
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* To the divine delight which shone upon me
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* When to her smiling face I turned me round.
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* The virtue that her look endowed me with
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* From the fair nest of Leda tore me forth,
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* And up into the swiftest heaven impelled me.
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* Its parts exceeding full of life and lofty
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* Are all so uniform, I cannot say
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* Which Beatrice selected for my place.
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* But she, who was aware of my desire,
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* Began, the while she smiled so joyously
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* That God seemed in her countenance to rejoice:
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*“The nature of that motion, which keeps quiet\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*The centre and all the rest about it moves,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*From hence begins as from its starting point.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*And in this heaven there is no other Where\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Than in the Mind Divine, wherein is kindled\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*The love that turns it, and the power it rains.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Within a circle light and love embrace it,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Even as this doth the others, and that precinct\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*He who encircles it alone controls.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Its motion is not by another meted,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*But all the others measured are by this,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*As ten is by the half and by the fifth.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*And in what manner time in such a pot\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*May have its roots, and in the rest its leaves,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Now unto thee can manifest be made.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*O Covetousness, that mortals dost ingulf\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Beneath thee so, that no one hath the power\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Of drawing back his eyes from out thy waves!\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Full fairly blossoms in mankind the will;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 125 \vp 125\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*But the uninterrupted rain converts\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 126 \vp 126\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Into abortive wildings the true plums.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 127 \vp 127\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Fidelity and innocence are found\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 128 \vp 128\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Only in children; afterwards they both\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 129 \vp 129\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Take flight or e’er the cheeks with down are covered.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 130 \vp 130\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*One, while he prattles still, observes the fasts,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 131 \vp 131\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Who, when his tongue is loosed, forthwith devours\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 132 \vp 132\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Whatever food under whatever moon;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 133 \vp 133\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Another, while he prattles, loves and listens\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 134 \vp 134\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Unto his mother, who when speech is perfect\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 135 \vp 135\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Forthwith desires to see her in her grave.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 136 \vp 136\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Even thus is swarthy made the skin so white\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 137 \vp 137\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*In its first aspect of the daughter fair\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 138 \vp 138\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Of him who brings the morn, and leaves the night.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 139 \vp 139\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Thou, that it may not be a marvel to thee,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 140 \vp 140\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Think that on earth there is no one who governs;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 141 \vp 141\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Whence goes astray the human family.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 142 \vp 142\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Ere January be unwintered wholly\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 143 \vp 143\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*By the centesimal on earth neglected,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 144 \vp 144\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Shall these supernal circles roar so loud\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 145 \vp 145\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*The tempest that has been so long awaited\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 146 \vp 146\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Shall whirl the poops about where are the prows;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 147 \vp 147\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*So that the fleet shall run its course direct,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 148 \vp 148\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*And the true fruit shall follow on the flower.”\zsay-e\*
\c 28
\cp 28
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* After the truth against the present life
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* Of miserable mortals was unfolded
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* By her who doth imparadise my mind,
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* As in a looking-glass a taper’s flame
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* He sees who from behind is lighted by it,
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* Before he has it in his sight or thought,
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* And turns him round to see if so the glass
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* Tell him the truth, and sees that it accords
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* Therewith as doth a music with its metre,
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* In similar wise my memory recollecteth
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* That I did, looking into those fair eyes,
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* Of which Love made the springes to ensnare me.
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* And as I turned me round, and mine were touched
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* By that which is apparent in that volume,
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* Whenever on its gyre we gaze intent,
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* A point beheld I, that was raying out
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* Light so acute, the sight which it enkindles
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* Must close perforce before such great acuteness.
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* And whatsoever star seems smallest here
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* Would seem to be a moon, if placed beside it.
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* As one star with another star is placed.
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* Perhaps at such a distance as appears
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* A halo cincturing the light that paints it,
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* When densest is the vapour that sustains it,
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* Thus distant round the point a circle of fire
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* So swiftly whirled, that it would have surpassed
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* Whatever motion soonest girds the world;
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* And this was by another circumcinct,
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* That by a third, the third then by a fourth,
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* By a fifth the fourth, and then by a sixth the fifth;
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* The seventh followed thereupon in width
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* So ample now, that Juno’s messenger
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* Entire would be too narrow to contain it.
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* Even so the eighth and ninth; and every one
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* More slowly moved, according as it was
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* In number distant farther from the first.
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* And that one had its flame most crystalline
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* From which less distant was the stainless spark,
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* I think because more with its truth imbued.
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* My Lady, who in my anxiety
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* Beheld me much perplexed, said: \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*“From that point\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Dependent is the heaven and nature all.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Behold that circle most conjoined to it,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*And know thou, that its motion is so swift\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Through burning love whereby it is spurred on.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* And I to her: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“If the world were arranged\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*In the order which I see in yonder wheels,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*What’s set before me would have satisfied me;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*But in the world of sense we can perceive\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*That evermore the circles are diviner\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*As they are from the centre more remote\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Wherefore if my desire is to be ended\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*In this miraculous and angelic temple,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*That has for confines only love and light,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*To hear behoves me still how the example\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*And the exemplar go not in one fashion,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Since for myself in vain I contemplate it.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*“If thine own fingers unto such a knot\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Be insufficient, it is no great wonder,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*So hard hath it become for want of trying.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* My Lady thus; then said she: \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*“Do thou take\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*What I shall tell thee, if thou wouldst be sated,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*And exercise on that thy subtlety.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*The circles corporal are wide and narrow\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*According to the more or less of virtue\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Which is distributed through all their parts.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*The greater goodness works the greater weal,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*The greater weal the greater body holds,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*If perfect equally are all its parts.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Therefore this one which sweeps along with it\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*The universe sublime, doth correspond\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Unto the circle which most loves and knows.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*On which account, if thou unto the virtue\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Apply thy measure, not to the appearance\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Of substances that unto thee seem round,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Thou wilt behold a marvellous agreement,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Of more to greater, and of less to smaller,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*In every heaven, with its Intelligence.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* Even as remaineth splendid and serene
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* The hemisphere of air, when Boreas
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* Is blowing from that cheek where he is mildest,
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* Because is purified and resolved the rack
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* That erst disturbed it, till the welkin laughs
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* With all the beauties of its pageantry;
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* Thus did I likewise, after that my Lady
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* Had me provided with her clear response,
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* And like a star in heaven the truth was seen.
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* And soon as to a stop her words had come,
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* Not otherwise does iron scintillate
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* When molten, than those circles scintillated.
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* Their coruscation all the sparks repeated,
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* And they so many were, their number makes
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* More millions than the doubling of the chess.
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* I heard them sing hosanna choir by choir
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* To the fixed point which holds them at the ‘Ubi,’
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* And ever will, where they have ever been.
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* And she, who saw the dubious meditations
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* Within my mind, \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*“The primal circles,”\zsay-e\* said,
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*“Have shown thee Seraphim and Cherubim.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Thus rapidly they follow their own bonds,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*To be as like the point as most they can,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*And can as far as they are high in vision.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Those other Loves, that round about them go,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Thrones of the countenance divine are called,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Because they terminate the primal Triad.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*And thou shouldst know that they all have delight\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*As much as their own vision penetrates\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*The Truth, in which all intellect finds rest.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*From this it may be seen how blessedness\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Is founded in the faculty which sees,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*And not in that which loves, and follows next;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*And of this seeing merit is the measure,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Which is brought forth by grace, and by good will;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Thus on from grade to grade doth it proceed.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*The second Triad, which is germinating\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*In such wise in this sempiternal spring,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*That no nocturnal Aries despoils,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Perpetually hosanna warbles forth\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*With threefold melody, that sounds in three\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Orders of joy, with which it is intrined.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*The three Divine are in this hierarchy,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*First the Dominions, and the Virtues next;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*And the third order is that of the Powers.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Then in the dances twain penultimate\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 125 \vp 125\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*The Principalities and Archangels wheel;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 126 \vp 126\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*The last is wholly of angelic sports.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 127 \vp 127\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*These orders upward all of them are gazing,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 128 \vp 128\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*And downward so prevail, that unto God\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 129 \vp 129\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*They all attracted are and all attract.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 130 \vp 130\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*And Dionysius with so great desire\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 131 \vp 131\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*To contemplate these Orders set himself,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 132 \vp 132\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*He named them and distinguished them as I do.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 133 \vp 133\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*But Gregory afterwards dissented from him;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 134 \vp 134\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Wherefore, as soon as he unclosed his eyes\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 135 \vp 135\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Within this heaven, he at himself did smile.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 136 \vp 136\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*And if so much of secret truth a mortal\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 137 \vp 137\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Proffered on earth, I would not have thee marvel,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 138 \vp 138\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*For he who saw it here revealed it to him,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 139 \vp 139\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*With much more of the truth about these circles.”\zsay-e\*
\c 29
\cp 29
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* At what time both the children of Latona,
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* Surmounted by the Ram and by the Scales,
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* Together make a zone of the horizon,
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* As long as from the time the zenith holds them
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* In equipoise, till from that girdle both
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* Changing their hemisphere disturb the balance,
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* So long, her face depicted with a smile,
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* Did Beatrice keep silence while she gazed
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* Fixedly at the point which had o’ercome me.
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* Then she began: \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*“I say, and I ask not\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*What thou dost wish to hear, for I have seen it\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Where centres every When and every ‘Ubi.’\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Not to acquire some good unto himself,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Which is impossible, but that his splendour\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*In its resplendency may say, ‘Subsisto,’\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*In his eternity outside of time,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Outside all other limits, as it pleased him,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Into new Loves the Eternal Love unfolded.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Nor as if torpid did he lie before;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*For neither after nor before proceeded\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*The going forth of God upon these waters.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Matter and Form unmingled and conjoined\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Came into being that had no defect,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*E’en as three arrows from a three-stringed bow.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*And as in glass, in amber, or in crystal\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*A sunbeam flashes so, that from its coming\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*To its full being is no interval,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*So from its Lord did the triform effect\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Ray forth into its being all together,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Without discrimination of beginning.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Order was con-created and constructed\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*In substances, and summit of the world\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Were those wherein the pure act was produced.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Pure potentiality held the lowest part;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Midway bound potentiality with act\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Such bond that it shall never be unbound.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Jerome has written unto you of angels\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Created a long lapse of centuries\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Or ever yet the other world was made;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*But written is this truth in many places\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*By writers of the Holy Ghost, and thou\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Shalt see it, if thou lookest well thereat.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*And even reason seeth it somewhat,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*For it would not concede that for so long\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Could be the motors without their perfection.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Now dost thou know both where and when these Loves\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Created were, and how; so that extinct\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*In thy desire already are three fires.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Nor could one reach, in counting, unto twenty\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*So swiftly, as a portion of these angels\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Disturbed the subject of your elements.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*The rest remained, and they began this art\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Which thou discernest, with so great delight\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*That never from their circling do they cease.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*The occasion of the fall was the accursed\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Presumption of that One, whom thou hast seen\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*By all the burden of the world constrained.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Those whom thou here beholdest modest were\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*To recognise themselves as of that goodness\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Which made them apt for so much understanding;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*On which account their vision was exalted\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*By the enlightening grace and their own merit,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*So that they have a full and steadfast will.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*I would not have thee doubt, but certain be,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*’Tis meritorious to receive this grace,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*According as the affection opens to it.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Now round about in this consistory\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Much mayst thou contemplate, if these my words\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Be gathered up, without all further aid.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*But since upon the earth, throughout your schools,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*They teach that such is the angelic nature\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*That it doth hear, and recollect, and will,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*More will I say, that thou mayst see unmixed\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*The truth that is confounded there below,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Equivocating in such like prelections.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*These substances, since in God’s countenance\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*They jocund were, turned not away their sight\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*From that wherefrom not anything is hidden;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Hence they have not their vision intercepted\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*By object new, and hence they do not need\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*To recollect, through interrupted thought.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*So that below, not sleeping, people dream,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Believing they speak truth, and not believing;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*And in the last is greater sin and shame.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Below you do not journey by one path\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Philosophising; so transporteth you\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Love of appearance and the thought thereof.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*And even this above here is endured\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*With less disdain, than when is set aside\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*The Holy Writ, or when it is distorted.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*They think not there how much of blood it costs\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*To sow it in the world, and how he pleases\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Who in humility keeps close to it.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Each striveth for appearance, and doth make\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*His own inventions; and these treated are\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*By preachers, and the Evangel holds its peace.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*One sayeth that the moon did backward turn,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*In the Passion of Christ, and interpose herself\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*So that the sunlight reached not down below;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*And lies; for of its own accord the light\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Hid itself; whence to Spaniards and to Indians,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*As to the Jews, did such eclipse respond.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Florence has not so many Lapi and Bindi\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*As fables such as these, that every year\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Are shouted from the pulpit back and forth,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*In such wise that the lambs, who do not know,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Come back from pasture fed upon the wind,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*And not to see the harm doth not excuse them.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Christ did not to his first disciples say,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*‘Go forth, and to the world preach idle tales,’\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*But unto them a true foundation gave;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*And this so loudly sounded from their lips,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*That, in the warfare to enkindle Faith,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*They made of the Evangel shields and lances.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Now men go forth with jests and drolleries\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*To preach, and if but well the people laugh,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*The hood puffs out, and nothing more is asked.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*But in the cowl there nestles such a bird,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*That, if the common people were to see it,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*They would perceive what pardons they confide in,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*For which so great on earth has grown the folly,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*That, without proof of any testimony,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*To each indulgence they would flock together.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*By this Saint Anthony his pig doth fatten,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 125 \vp 125\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*And many others, who are worse than pigs,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 126 \vp 126\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Paying in money without mark of coinage.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 127 \vp 127\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*But since we have digressed abundantly,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 128 \vp 128\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Turn back thine eyes forthwith to the right path,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 129 \vp 129\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*So that the way be shortened with the time.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 130 \vp 130\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*This nature doth so multiply itself\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 131 \vp 131\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*In numbers, that there never yet was speech\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 132 \vp 132\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Nor mortal fancy that can go so far.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 133 \vp 133\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*And if thou notest that which is revealed\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 134 \vp 134\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*By Daniel, thou wilt see that in his thousands\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 135 \vp 135\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Number determinate is kept concealed.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 136 \vp 136\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*The primal light, that all irradiates it,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 137 \vp 137\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*By modes as many is received therein,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 138 \vp 138\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*As are the splendours wherewith it is mated.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 139 \vp 139\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Hence, inasmuch as on the act conceptive\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 140 \vp 140\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*The affection followeth, of love the sweetness\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 141 \vp 141\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Therein diversely fervid is or tepid.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 142 \vp 142\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*The height behold now and the amplitude\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 143 \vp 143\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Of the eternal power, since it hath made\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 144 \vp 144\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Itself so many mirrors, where ’tis broken,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 145 \vp 145\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*One in itself remaining as before.”\zsay-e\*
\c 30
\cp 30
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* Perchance six thousand miles remote from us
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* Is glowing the sixth hour, and now this world
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* Inclines its shadow almost to a level,
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* When the mid-heaven begins to make itself
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* So deep to us, that here and there a star
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* Ceases to shine so far down as this depth,
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* And as advances bright exceedingly
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* The handmaid of the sun, the heaven is closed
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* Light after light to the most beautiful;
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* Not otherwise the Triumph, which for ever
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* Plays round about the point that vanquished me,
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* Seeming enclosed by what itself encloses,
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* Little by little from my vision faded;
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* Whereat to turn mine eyes on Beatrice
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* My seeing nothing and my love constrained me.
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* If what has hitherto been said of her
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* Were all concluded in a single praise,
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* Scant would it be to serve the present turn.
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* Not only does the beauty I beheld
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* Transcend ourselves, but truly I believe
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* Its Maker only may enjoy it all.
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* Vanquished do I confess me by this passage
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* More than by problem of his theme was ever
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* O’ercome the comic or the tragic poet;
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* For as the sun the sight that trembles most,
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* Even so the memory of that sweet smile
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* My mind depriveth of its very self.
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* From the first day that I beheld her face
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* In this life, to the moment of this look,
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* The sequence of my song has ne’er been severed;
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* But now perforce this sequence must desist
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* From following her beauty with my verse,
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* As every artist at his uttermost.
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* Such as I leave her to a greater fame
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* Than any of my trumpet, which is bringing
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* Its arduous matter to a final close,
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* With voice and gesture of a perfect leader
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* She recommenced: \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*“We from the greatest body\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Have issued to the heaven that is pure light;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Light intellectual replete with love,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Love of true good replete with ecstasy,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Ecstasy that transcendeth every sweetness.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Here shalt thou see the one host and the other\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Of Paradise, and one in the same aspects\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Which at the final judgment thou shalt see.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* Even as a sudden lightning that disperses
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* The visual spirits, so that it deprives
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* The eye of impress from the strongest objects,
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* Thus round about me flashed a living light,
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* And left me swathed around with such a veil
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* Of its effulgence, that I nothing saw.
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*“Ever the Love which quieteth this heaven\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Welcomes into itself with such salute,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*To make the candle ready for its flame.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* No sooner had within me these brief words
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* An entrance found, than I perceived myself
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* To be uplifted over my own power,
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* And I with vision new rekindled me,
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* Such that no light whatever is so pure
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* But that mine eyes were fortified against it.
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* And light I saw in fashion of a river
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* Fulvid with its effulgence, ’twixt two banks
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* Depicted with an admirable Spring.
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* Out of this river issued living sparks,
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* And on all sides sank down into the flowers,
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* Like unto rubies that are set in gold;
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* And then, as if inebriate with the odours,
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* They plunged again into the wondrous torrent,
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* And as one entered issued forth another.
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*“The high desire, that now inflames and moves thee\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*To have intelligence of what thou seest,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Pleaseth me all the more, the more it swells.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*But of this water it behoves thee drink\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Before so great a thirst in thee be slaked.”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* Thus said to me the sunshine of mine eyes;
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* And added: \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*“The river and the topazes\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Going in and out, and the laughing of the herbage,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Are of their truth foreshadowing prefaces;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Not that these things are difficult in themselves,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*But the deficiency is on thy side,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*For yet thou hast not vision so exalted.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* There is no babe that leaps so suddenly
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* With face towards the milk, if he awake
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* Much later than his usual custom is,
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* As I did, that I might make better mirrors
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* Still of mine eyes, down stooping to the wave
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* Which flows that we therein be better made.
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* And even as the penthouse of mine eyelids
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* Drank of it, it forthwith appeared to me
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* Out of its length to be transformed to round.
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* Then as a folk who have been under masks
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* Seem other than before, if they divest
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* The semblance not their own they disappeared in,
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* Thus into greater pomp were changed for me
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* The flowerets and the sparks, so that I saw
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* Both of the Courts of Heaven made manifest.
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* O splendour of God! by means of which I saw
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* The lofty triumph of the realm veracious,
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* Give me the power to say how it I saw!
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* There is a light above, which visible
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* Makes the Creator unto every creature,
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* Who only in beholding Him has peace,
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* And it expands itself in circular form
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* To such extent, that its circumference
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* Would be too large a girdle for the sun.
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* The semblance of it is all made of rays
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* Reflected from the top of Primal Motion,
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* Which takes therefrom vitality and power.
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* And as a hill in water at its base
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* Mirrors itself, as if to see its beauty
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* When affluent most in verdure and in flowers,
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* So, ranged aloft all round about the light,
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* Mirrored I saw in more ranks than a thousand
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* All who above there have from us returned.
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* And if the lowest row collect within it
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* So great a light, how vast the amplitude
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* Is of this Rose in its extremest leaves!
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* My vision in the vastness and the height
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* Lost not itself, but comprehended all
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* The quantity and quality of that gladness.
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* There near and far nor add nor take away;
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* For there where God immediately doth govern,
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* The natural law in naught is relevant.
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* Into the yellow of the Rose Eternal
\q1 \v 125 \vp 125\vp* That spreads, and multiplies, and breathes an odour
\q1 \v 126 \vp 126\vp* Of praise unto the ever-vernal Sun,
\b
\q1 \v 127 \vp 127\vp* As one who silent is and fain would speak,
\q1 \v 128 \vp 128\vp* Me Beatrice drew on, and said: \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*“Behold\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 129 \vp 129\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Of the white stoles how vast the convent is!\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 130 \vp 130\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Behold how vast the circuit of our city!\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 131 \vp 131\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Behold our seats so filled to overflowing,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 132 \vp 132\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*That here henceforward are few people wanting!\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 133 \vp 133\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*On that great throne whereon thine eyes are fixed\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 134 \vp 134\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*For the crown’s sake already placed upon it,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 135 \vp 135\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Before thou suppest at this wedding feast\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 136 \vp 136\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Shall sit the soul (that is to be Augustus\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 137 \vp 137\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*On earth) of noble Henry, who shall come\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 138 \vp 138\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*To redress Italy ere she be ready.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 139 \vp 139\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Blind covetousness, that casts its spell upon you,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 140 \vp 140\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Has made you like unto the little child,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 141 \vp 141\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Who dies of hunger and drives off the nurse.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 142 \vp 142\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*And in the sacred forum then shall be\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 143 \vp 143\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*A Prefect such, that openly or covert\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 144 \vp 144\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*On the same road he will not walk with him.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 145 \vp 145\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*But long of God he will not be endured\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 146 \vp 146\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*In holy office; he shall be thrust down\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 147 \vp 147\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Where Simon Magus is for his deserts,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 148 \vp 148\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*And make him of Alagna lower go!”\zsay-e\*
\c 31
\cp 31
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* In fashion then as of a snow-white rose
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* Displayed itself to me the saintly host,
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* Whom Christ in his own blood had made his bride,
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* But the other host, that flying sees and sings
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* The glory of Him who doth enamour it,
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* And the goodness that created it so noble,
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* Even as a swarm of bees, that sinks in flowers
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* One moment, and the next returns again
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* To where its labour is to sweetness turned,
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* Sank into the great flower, that is adorned
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* With leaves so many, and thence reascended
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* To where its love abideth evermore.
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* Their faces had they all of living flame,
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* And wings of gold, and all the rest so white
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* No snow unto that limit doth attain.
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* From bench to bench, into the flower descending,
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* They carried something of the peace and ardour
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* Which by the fanning of their flanks they won.
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* Nor did the interposing ’twixt the flower
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* And what was o’er it of such plenitude
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* Of flying shapes impede the sight and splendour;
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* Because the light divine so penetrates
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* The universe, according to its merit,
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* That naught can be an obstacle against it.
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* This realm secure and full of gladsomeness,
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* Crowded with ancient people and with modern,
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* Unto one mark had all its look and love.
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* O Trinal Light, that in a single star
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* Sparkling upon their sight so satisfies them,
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* Look down upon our tempest here below!
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* If the barbarians, coming from some region
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* That every day by Helice is covered,
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* Revolving with her son whom she delights in,
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* Beholding Rome and all her noble works,
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* Were wonder-struck, what time the Lateran
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* Above all mortal things was eminent,—
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* I who to the divine had from the human,
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* From time unto eternity, had come,
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* From Florence to a people just and sane,
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* With what amazement must I have been filled!
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* Truly between this and the joy, it was
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* My pleasure not to hear, and to be mute.
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* And as a pilgrim who delighteth him
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* In gazing round the temple of his vow,
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* And hopes some day to retell how it was,
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* So through the living light my way pursuing
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* Directed I mine eyes o’er all the ranks,
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* Now up, now down, and now all round about.
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* Faces I saw of charity persuasive,
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* Embellished by His light and their own smile,
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* And attitudes adorned with every grace.
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* The general form of Paradise already
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* My glance had comprehended as a whole,
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* In no part hitherto remaining fixed,
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* And round I turned me with rekindled wish
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* My Lady to interrogate of things
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* Concerning which my mind was in suspense.
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* One thing I meant, another answered me;
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* I thought I should see Beatrice, and saw
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* An Old Man habited like the glorious people.
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* O’erflowing was he in his eyes and cheeks
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* With joy benign, in attitude of pity
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* As to a tender father is becoming.
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* And \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“She, where is she?”\zsay-e\* instantly I said;
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* Whence he: \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*“To put an end to thy desire,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*Me Beatrice hath sent from mine own place.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*And if thou lookest up to the third round\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*Of the first rank, again shalt thou behold her\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*Upon the throne her merits have assigned her.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* Without reply I lifted up mine eyes,
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* And saw her, as she made herself a crown
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* Reflecting from herself the eternal rays.
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* Not from that region which the highest thunders
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* Is any mortal eye so far removed,
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* In whatsoever sea it deepest sinks,
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* As there from Beatrice my sight; but this
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* Was nothing unto me; because her image
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* Descended not to me by medium blurred.
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“O Lady, thou in whom my hope is strong,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*And who for my salvation didst endure\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*In Hell to leave the imprint of thy feet,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Of whatsoever things I have beheld,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*As coming from thy power and from thy goodness\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*I recognise the virtue and the grace.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Thou from a slave hast brought me unto freedom,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*By all those ways, by all the expedients,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Whereby thou hadst the power of doing it.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Preserve towards me thy magnificence,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*So that this soul of mine, which thou hast healed,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Pleasing to thee be loosened from the body.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* Thus I implored; and she, so far away,
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* Smiled, as it seemed, and looked once more at me;
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* Then unto the eternal fountain turned.
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* And said the Old Man holy: \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*“That thou mayst\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*Accomplish perfectly thy journeying,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*Whereunto prayer and holy love have sent me,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*Fly with thine eyes all round about this garden;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*For seeing it will discipline thy sight\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*Farther to mount along the ray divine.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*And she, the Queen of Heaven, for whom I burn\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*Wholly with love, will grant us every grace,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*Because that I her faithful Bernard am.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* As he who peradventure from Croatia
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* Cometh to gaze at our Veronica,
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* Who through its ancient fame is never sated,
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* But says in thought, the while it is displayed,
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* \zsay-s |who="A pilgrim"\*“My Lord, Christ Jesus, God of very God,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* \zsay-s |who="A pilgrim"\*Now was your semblance made like unto this?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* Even such was I while gazing at the living
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* Charity of the man, who in this world
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* By contemplation tasted of that peace.
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*“Thou son of grace, this jocund life,”\zsay-e\* began he,
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*“Will not be known to thee by keeping ever\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*Thine eyes below here on the lowest place;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*But mark the circles to the most remote,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*Until thou shalt behold enthroned the Queen\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*To whom this realm is subject and devoted.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* I lifted up mine eyes, and as at morn
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* The oriental part of the horizon
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* Surpasses that wherein the sun goes down,
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* Thus, as if going with mine eyes from vale
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* To mount, I saw a part in the remoteness
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* Surpass in splendour all the other front.
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* And even as there where we await the pole
\q1 \v 125 \vp 125\vp* That Phaeton drove badly, blazes more
\q1 \v 126 \vp 126\vp* The light, and is on either side diminished,
\b
\q1 \v 127 \vp 127\vp* So likewise that pacific oriflamme
\q1 \v 128 \vp 128\vp* Gleamed brightest in the centre, and each side
\q1 \v 129 \vp 129\vp* In equal measure did the flame abate.
\b
\q1 \v 130 \vp 130\vp* And at that centre, with their wings expanded,
\q1 \v 131 \vp 131\vp* More than a thousand jubilant Angels saw I,
\q1 \v 132 \vp 132\vp* Each differing in effulgence and in kind.
\b
\q1 \v 133 \vp 133\vp* I saw there at their sports and at their songs
\q1 \v 134 \vp 134\vp* A beauty smiling, which the gladness was
\q1 \v 135 \vp 135\vp* Within the eyes of all the other saints;
\b
\q1 \v 136 \vp 136\vp* And if I had in speaking as much wealth
\q1 \v 137 \vp 137\vp* As in imagining, I should not dare
\q1 \v 138 \vp 138\vp* To attempt the smallest part of its delight.
\b
\q1 \v 139 \vp 139\vp* Bernard, as soon as he beheld mine eyes
\q1 \v 140 \vp 140\vp* Fixed and intent upon its fervid fervour,
\q1 \v 141 \vp 141\vp* His own with such affection turned to her
\b
\q1 \v 142 \vp 142\vp* That it made mine more ardent to behold.
\c 32
\cp 32
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* Absorbed in his delight, that contemplator
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* Assumed the willing office of a teacher,
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* And gave beginning to these holy words:
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*“The wound that Mary closed up and anointed,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*She at her feet who is so beautiful,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*She is the one who opened it and pierced it.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*Within that order which the third seats make\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*Is seated Rachel, lower than the other,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*With Beatrice, in manner as thou seest.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*Sarah, Rebecca, Judith, and her who was\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*Ancestress of the Singer, who for dole\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*Of the misdeed said, ‘Miserere mei,’\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*Canst thou behold from seat to seat descending\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*Down in gradation, as with each one’s name\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*I through the Rose go down from leaf to leaf.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*And downward from the seventh row, even as\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*Above the same, succeed the Hebrew women,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*Dividing all the tresses of the flower;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*Because, according to the view which Faith\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*In Christ had taken, these are the partition\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*By which the sacred stairways are divided.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*Upon this side, where perfect is the flower\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*With each one of its petals, seated are\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*Those who believed in Christ who was to come.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*Upon the other side, where intersected\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*With vacant spaces are the semicircles,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*Are those who looked to Christ already come.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*And as, upon this side, the glorious seat\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*Of the Lady of Heaven, and the other seats\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*Below it, such a great division make,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*So opposite doth that of the great John,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*Who, ever holy, desert and martyrdom\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*Endured, and afterwards two years in Hell.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*And under him thus to divide were chosen\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*Francis, and Benedict, and Augustine,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*And down to us the rest from round to round.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*Behold now the high providence divine;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*For one and other aspect of the Faith\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*In equal measure shall this garden fill.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*And know that downward from that rank which cleaves\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*Midway the sequence of the two divisions,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*Not by their proper merit are they seated;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*But by another’s under fixed conditions;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*For these are spirits one and all assoiled\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*Before they any true election had.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*Well canst thou recognise it in their faces,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*And also in their voices puerile,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*If thou regard them well and hearken to them.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*Now doubtest thou, and doubting thou art silent;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*But I will loosen for thee the strong bond\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*In which thy subtile fancies hold thee fast.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*Within the amplitude of this domain\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*No casual point can possibly find place,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*No more than sadness can, or thirst, or hunger;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*For by eternal law has been established\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*Whatever thou beholdest, so that closely\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*The ring is fitted to the finger here.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*And therefore are these people, festinate\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*Unto true life, not ‘sine causa’ here\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*More and less excellent among themselves.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*The King, by means of whom this realm reposes\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*In so great love and in so great delight\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*That no will ventureth to ask for more,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*In his own joyous aspect every mind\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*Creating, at his pleasure dowers with grace\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*Diversely; and let here the effect suffice.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*And this is clearly and expressly noted\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*For you in Holy Scripture, in those twins\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*Who in their mother had their anger roused.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*According to the colour of the hair,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*Therefore, with such a grace the light supreme\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*Consenteth that they worthily be crowned.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*Without, then, any merit of their deeds,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*Stationed are they in different gradations,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*Differing only in their first acuteness.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*’Tis true that in the early centuries,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*With innocence, to work out their salvation\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*Sufficient was the faith of parents only.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*After the earlier ages were completed,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*Behoved it that the males by circumcision\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*Unto their innocent wings should virtue add;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*But after that the time of grace had come\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*Without the baptism absolute of Christ,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*Such innocence below there was retained.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*Look now into the face that unto Christ\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*Hath most resemblance; for its brightness only\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*Is able to prepare thee to see Christ.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* On her did I behold so great a gladness
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* Rain down, borne onward in the holy minds
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* Created through that altitude to fly,
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* That whatsoever I had seen before
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* Did not suspend me in such admiration,
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* Nor show me such similitude of God.
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* And the same Love that first descended there,
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* \zsay-s |who="Gabriel"\*“Ave Maria, gratia plena,”\zsay-e\* singing,
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* In front of her his wings expanded wide.
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* Unto the canticle divine responded
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* From every part the court beatified,
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* So that each sight became serener for it.
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“O holy father, who for me endurest\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*To be below here, leaving the sweet place\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*In which thou sittest by eternal lot,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Who is the Angel that with so much joy\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Into the eyes is looking of our Queen,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Enamoured so that he seems made of fire?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* Thus I again recourse had to the teaching
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* Of that one who delighted him in Mary
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* As doth the star of morning in the sun.
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* And he to me: \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*“Such gallantry and grace\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*As there can be in Angel and in soul,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*All is in him; and thus we fain would have it;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*Because he is the one who bore the palm\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*Down unto Mary, when the Son of God\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*To take our burden on himself decreed.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*But now come onward with thine eyes, as I\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*Speaking shall go, and note the great patricians\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*Of this most just and merciful of empires.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*Those two that sit above there most enrapture\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*As being very near unto Augusta,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*Are as it were the two roots of this Rose.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*He who upon the left is near her placed\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*The father is, by whose audacious taste\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*The human species so much bitter tastes.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*Upon the right thou seest that ancient father\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 125 \vp 125\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*Of Holy Church, into whose keeping Christ\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 126 \vp 126\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*The keys committed of this lovely flower.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 127 \vp 127\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*And he who all the evil days beheld,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 128 \vp 128\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*Before his death, of her the beauteous bride\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 129 \vp 129\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*Who with the spear and with the nails was won,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 130 \vp 130\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*Beside him sits, and by the other rests\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 131 \vp 131\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*That leader under whom on manna lived\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 132 \vp 132\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*The people ingrate, fickle, and stiff-necked.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 133 \vp 133\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*Opposite Peter seest thou Anna seated,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 134 \vp 134\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*So well content to look upon her daughter,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 135 \vp 135\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*Her eyes she moves not while she sings Hosanna.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 136 \vp 136\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*And opposite the eldest household father\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 137 \vp 137\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*Lucia sits, she who thy Lady moved\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 138 \vp 138\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*When to rush downward thou didst bend thy brows.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 139 \vp 139\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*But since the moments of thy vision fly,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 140 \vp 140\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*Here will we make full stop, as a good tailor\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 141 \vp 141\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*Who makes the gown according to his cloth,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 142 \vp 142\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*And unto the first Love will turn our eyes,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 143 \vp 143\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*That looking upon Him thou penetrate\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 144 \vp 144\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*As far as possible through his effulgence.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 145 \vp 145\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*Truly, lest peradventure thou recede,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 146 \vp 146\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*Moving thy wings believing to advance,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 147 \vp 147\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*By prayer behoves it that grace be obtained;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 148 \vp 148\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*Grace from that one who has the power to aid thee;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 149 \vp 149\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*And thou shalt follow me with thy affection\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 150 \vp 150\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*That from my words thy heart turn not aside.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 151 \vp 151\vp* And he began this holy orison.
\c 33
\cp 33
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*“Thou Virgin Mother, daughter of thy Son,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*Humble and high beyond all other creature,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*The limit fixed of the eternal counsel,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*Thou art the one who such nobility\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*To human nature gave, that its Creator\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*Did not disdain to make himself its creature.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*Within thy womb rekindled was the love,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*By heat of which in the eternal peace\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*After such wise this flower has germinated.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*Here unto us thou art a noonday torch\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*Of charity, and below there among mortals\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*Thou art the living fountain-head of hope.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*Lady, thou art so great, and so prevailing,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*That he who wishes grace, nor runs to thee,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*His aspirations without wings would fly.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*Not only thy benignity gives succour\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*To him who asketh it, but oftentimes\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*Forerunneth of its own accord the asking.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*In thee compassion is, in thee is pity,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*In thee magnificence; in thee unites\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*Whate’er of goodness is in any creature.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*Now doth this man, who from the lowest depth\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*Of the universe as far as here has seen\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*One after one the spiritual lives,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*Supplicate thee through grace for so much power\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*That with his eyes he may uplift himself\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*Higher towards the uttermost salvation.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*And I, who never burned for my own seeing\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*More than I do for his, all of my prayers\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*Proffer to thee, and pray they come not short,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*That thou wouldst scatter from him every cloud\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*Of his mortality so with thy prayers,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*That the Chief Pleasure be to him displayed.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*Still farther do I pray thee, Queen, who canst\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*Whate’er thou wilt, that sound thou mayst preserve\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*After so great a vision his affections.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*Let thy protection conquer human movements;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*See Beatrice and all the blessed ones\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bernard"\*My prayers to second clasp their hands to thee!”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* The eyes beloved and revered of God,
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* Fastened upon the speaker, showed to us
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* How grateful unto her are prayers devout;
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* Then unto the Eternal Light they turned,
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* On which it is not credible could be
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* By any creature bent an eye so clear.
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* And I, who to the end of all desires
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* Was now approaching, even as I ought
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* The ardour of desire within me ended.
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* Bernard was beckoning unto me, and smiling,
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* That I should upward look; but I already
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* Was of my own accord such as he wished;
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* Because my sight, becoming purified,
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* Was entering more and more into the ray
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* Of the High Light which of itself is true.
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* From that time forward what I saw was greater
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* Than our discourse, that to such vision yields,
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* And yields the memory unto such excess.
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* Even as he is who seeth in a dream,
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* And after dreaming the imprinted passion
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* Remains, and to his mind the rest returns not,
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* Even such am I, for almost utterly
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* Ceases my vision, and distilleth yet
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* Within my heart the sweetness born of it;
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* Even thus the snow is in the sun unsealed,
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* Even thus upon the wind in the light leaves
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* Were the soothsayings of the Sibyl lost.
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* O Light Supreme, that dost so far uplift thee
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* From the conceits of mortals, to my mind
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* Of what thou didst appear re-lend a little,
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* And make my tongue of so great puissance,
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* That but a single sparkle of thy glory
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* It may bequeath unto the future people;
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* For by returning to my memory somewhat,
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* And by a little sounding in these verses,
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* More of thy victory shall be conceived!
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* I think the keenness of the living ray
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* Which I endured would have bewildered me,
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* If but mine eyes had been averted from it;
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* And I remember that I was more bold
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* On this account to bear, so that I joined
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* My aspect with the Glory Infinite.
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* O grace abundant, by which I presumed
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* To fix my sight upon the Light Eternal,
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* So that the seeing I consumed therein!
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* I saw that in its depth far down is lying
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* Bound up with love together in one volume,
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* What through the universe in leaves is scattered;
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* Substance, and accident, and their operations,
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* All interfused together in such wise
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* That what I speak of is one simple light.
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* The universal fashion of this knot
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* Methinks I saw, since more abundantly
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* In saying this I feel that I rejoice.
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* One moment is more lethargy to me,
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* Than five and twenty centuries to the emprise
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* That startled Neptune with the shade of Argo!
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* My mind in this wise wholly in suspense,
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* Steadfast, immovable, attentive gazed,
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* And evermore with gazing grew enkindled.
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* In presence of that light one such becomes,
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* That to withdraw therefrom for other prospect
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* It is impossible he e’er consent;
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* Because the good, which object is of will,
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* Is gathered all in this, and out of it
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* That is defective which is perfect there.
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* Shorter henceforward will my language fall
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* Of what I yet remember, than an infant’s
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* Who still his tongue doth moisten at the breast.
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* Not because more than one unmingled semblance
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* Was in the living light on which I looked,
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* For it is always what it was before;
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* But through the sight, that fortified itself
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* In me by looking, one appearance only
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* To me was ever changing as I changed.
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* Within the deep and luminous subsistence
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* Of the High Light appeared to me three circles,
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* Of threefold colour and of one dimension,
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* And by the second seemed the first reflected
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* As Iris is by Iris, and the third
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* Seemed fire that equally from both is breathed.
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* O how all speech is feeble and falls short
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* Of my conceit, and this to what I saw
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* Is such, ’tis not enough to call it little!
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* O Light Eterne, sole in thyself that dwellest,
\q1 \v 125 \vp 125\vp* Sole knowest thyself, and, known unto thyself
\q1 \v 126 \vp 126\vp* And knowing, lovest and smilest on thyself!
\b
\q1 \v 127 \vp 127\vp* That circulation, which being thus conceived
\q1 \v 128 \vp 128\vp* Appeared in thee as a reflected light,
\q1 \v 129 \vp 129\vp* When somewhat contemplated by mine eyes,
\b
\q1 \v 130 \vp 130\vp* Within itself, of its own very colour
\q1 \v 131 \vp 131\vp* Seemed to me painted with our effigy,
\q1 \v 132 \vp 132\vp* Wherefore my sight was all absorbed therein.
\b
\q1 \v 133 \vp 133\vp* As the geometrician, who endeavours
\q1 \v 134 \vp 134\vp* To square the circle, and discovers not,
\q1 \v 135 \vp 135\vp* By taking thought, the principle he wants,
\b
\q1 \v 136 \vp 136\vp* Even such was I at that new apparition;
\q1 \v 137 \vp 137\vp* I wished to see how the image to the circle
\q1 \v 138 \vp 138\vp* Conformed itself, and how it there finds place;
\b
\q1 \v 139 \vp 139\vp* But my own wings were not enough for this,
\q1 \v 140 \vp 140\vp* Had it not been that then my mind there smote
\q1 \v 141 \vp 141\vp* A flash of lightning, wherein came its wish.
\b
\q1 \v 142 \vp 142\vp* Here vigour failed the lofty fantasy:
\q1 \v 143 \vp 143\vp* But now was turning my desire and will,
\q1 \v 144 \vp 144\vp* Even as a wheel that equally is moved,
\b
\q1 \v 145 \vp 145\vp* The Love which moves the sun and the other stars.
